\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Democrats introduce bill that aims to protect  \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health \highlight\uc2  data\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 770 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 June 2025\par 17:23 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18My body, my data\u8217\'20\'19 law is necessary to protect women from persecution in post-Roe era, lawmakers say\par \par \uc2 Three Democratic members of Congress have introduced a bill to limit companies\u8217\'20\'19 ability to hoover up data about people\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 a measure, they say, that is necessary to protect women from persecution in the post-Roe v Wade era.\par \par \uc2 Representative Sara Jacobs of California, Senator Mazie Hirono of Hawaii and Senator \uc2 Ron Wyden\uc2  of Oregon on Wednesday filed the \u8220\'20\'1Cmy body, my data\u8221\'20\'1D bill in both the US House and Senate. The bill aims to block companies from collecting, using, retaining or disclosing information about someone\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless that data is essential to providing a requested service. This provision would apply to information about pregnancy, menstruation, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , contraception and other matters relating to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYoung people live our lives online, right? That includes tracking our periods, but it also includes our phones tracking our location and using \uc2 Google\uc2  to think about your medical care or how to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for yourself or a friend, or ordering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills online, or using an Uber to travel to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic,\u8221\'20\'1D Jacobs said. \u8220\'20\'1CAll of those things are tracked online, and none of those are protected right now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Law enforcement officials have already attempted to use people\u8217\'20\'19s data trails to identify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers. In 2022, the year that the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe, Nebraska brought a series of felony and misdemeanor charges against a teenager and her mother in connection to the teen\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vice.com/en/article/this-is-the-data-facebook-gave-police-to-prosecute-a-teenager-for-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The charges relied on Facebook chats}}}\uc2 , which the social media giant had turned over. (Both the teenager and her mother {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/09/22/us/jessica-burgess-abortion-pill-nebraska.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pleaded guilty}}}\uc2  and were sentenced to time behind bars.) In 2023, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists used cellphone location information to send anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  messages to people who had visited some \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinics. And in May, a Texas police officer searched tens of automatic license plate reader cameras, including in states that permit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , for a woman who officials suspected of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.404media.co/a-texas-cop-searched-license-plate-cameras-nationwide-for-a-woman-who-got-an-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 self-managing an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The post-Roe landscape is also creating more opportunities for online surveillance. In recent years, orders for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills online {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/mar/25/self-managed-abortions-avoid-bans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have spiked}}}\uc2 , as tens of thousands more Americans have used online services to obtain pills to self-manage their own abortions.\par \par \uc2 A number of women have also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/jan/11/ohio-woman-miscarriage-not-indicted-abuse-corpse-brittany-watts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 faced criminal charges}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/apr/04/georgia-miscarriage-charges-dropped"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 over miscarriages}}}\uc2 , leading \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates to worry that women who \uc2 Google\uc2  phrases like \u8220\'20\'1Chow to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D and then miscarry could find themselves in law enforcement\u8217\'20\'19s crosshairs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt doesn\u8217\'20\'19t deal with everything in terms of data brokers, but it does put women in a much stronger position to protect their rights,\u8221\'20\'1D Wyden said of the \u8220\'20\'1Cmy body, my data\u8221\'20\'1D bill. \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  are the ultimate privacy priority, because the fundamental right of a woman to control her own body and her own healthcare is as private as it gets.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 An earlier version of the bill was introduced in 2023. Given that Republicans control Congress, the bill is not likely to pass.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI have many Republican colleagues who say they care about data privacy. We work together on data privacy in every other area, but when it comes to anything \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related, they refuse to do it,\u8221\'20\'1D Jacobs said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is also the third oldest Congress in history, and I\u8217\'20\'19ll be honest, many of my colleagues don\u8217\'20\'19t understand how period-tracking apps or website searches or location data even work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Jacobs says she uses a period tracker run by a company based in Europe that is subject to the \uc2 European Union\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s General Data Protection Regulation, a set of strict regulations that governs how businesses obtain and handle people\u8217\'20\'19s online data.\par \par \uc2 While the US has no similar regulations on the federal level, Washington state in 2023 became the first in the country to create a state version of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://app.leg.wa.gov/billsummary?BillNumber=1155&Initiative=false&Year=2023"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 My Health, My Data Act}}}\uc2 . That law covers health data that is not otherwise protected by the US Health Information Portability and Accountability Act (Hipaa) \u8211\'20\'13 including information about reproductive healthcare services \u8211\'20\'13 and requires companies to give their customers more privacy disclosures and seek their authorization before selling their data. It also gives Washington residents the ability to demand those companies delete their personal information.\par \par \uc2 Jacobs advises people to use apps based in states with some degree of protection for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  data.\par \par \uc2 She added: \u8220\'20\'1CIf you live in a state that is really criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and going after people, you should be careful about what you put online.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250611el6b001s3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
