\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Women and men diverge more than ever on support for  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights, poll shows\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 659 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 June 2025\par 20:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Gallup poll found 61% of women identify as pro-choice, while only 41% of men do so, in three years after fall of Roe\par \par \uc2 Three years after the fall of Roe v Wade and months after an election that heavily focused on the fight over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, men and women have never diverged more on their support for access to the procedure, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/691370/gender-gaps-abortion-reach-historic-highs.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new polling from Gallup}}}\uc2  released Monday.\par \par \uc2 Sixty-one percent of women now identify as \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-choice\u8221\'20\'1D, but only 41% of men say the same, Gallup found. The same percentage of women identified as \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-choice\u8221\'20\'1D in 2022, just after the decision to overturn Roe was leaked, but at the time, 48% of men also did so. Prior to Roe\u8217\'20\'19s collapse, men and women were never more than 10 points apart from one another on the issue, according to decades of Gallup polling.\par \par \uc2 Men and women are also in record disagreement over whether \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is moral, as 57% of women and 40% men say that it is. Just 41% of men say that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or more circumstances, while 56% of women say the same.\par \par \uc2 These gender gaps are likely less due to post-Roe changes in men\u8217\'20\'19s attitudes towards \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than in changes in women\u8217\'20\'19s attitudes, said Lydia Saad, Gallup\u8217\'20\'19s director of US social research. Specifically: women have become a lot more supportive of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Roe fell. In 2021, 52% of women and 45% of men identified as \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-choice\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn general, we see that with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that the party that wants to change the status quo is the one that has more energy on the issue,\u8221\'20\'1D Saad said. \u8220\'20\'1CFor years, it was more the pro-life respondents who said that they will only support a candidate who shares their views on that issue. Whereas, since 2022, we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen it flip.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Sudden political upsets do have the power to dramatically change people\u8217\'20\'19s beliefs, Saad said. Typically, however, those changes don\u8217\'20\'19t last and people revert to their norm views within a few years. Men\u8217\'20\'19s declining support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may thus be a sign that they are reverting to their norm \u8211\'20\'13 but Saad was surprised women are still so energized by the issue.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA line had been crossed for women,\u8221\'20\'1D Saad said. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you were generally supportive of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights before, you became much more so.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Similarly, men who identify as Democrats have, like women, become much more likely to back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Between 2020 and 2021, 63% of Democratic men said that they believed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in most circumstances; as of 2025, 78% of Democratic men say the same.\par \par \uc2 Saad is not exactly sure why support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights is dwindling among men. Although this is the lowest level of support among men for the \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-choice\u8221\'20\'1D label in a decade, she is not convinced that this decline will continue.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s more just a out of sight, out of mind issue for men,\u8221\'20\'1D Saad said of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s legality. \u8220\'20\'1CWhereas for women \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s just been more salient.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At this point, it\u8217\'20\'19s difficult to tell whether men are becoming more actively opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or whether they are simply becoming more conservative overall, Saad said. Men are already more likely to be Republicans, and Republicans typically oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. A mere 19% of Republican men think \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in most circumstances.\par \par \uc2 Saad suspects Gallup\u8217\'20\'19s findings may be tied to shifts in the political views of young men, who proved to be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/nov/08/young-voters-trump-gen-z"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 surprisingly conservative in the 2024 election}}}\uc2 . Fifty-six percent of men between the ages of 18 and 29 voted for Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to see where this goes,\u8221\'20\'1D Saad cautioned. \u8220\'20\'1CIf it\u8217\'20\'19s sustained, then we would really have to take a close look at why.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250609el69003bh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
