\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Trump rescinds guidance protecting women in need of emergency abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 562 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 June 2025\par 11:46 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters say scaling back Biden officials\u8217\'20\'19 Emtala guidance will endanger pregnant patients\u8217\'20\'19 lives\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump administration}}}\uc2  on Tuesday rescinded Biden-era guidance clarifying that hospitals in states with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  bans cannot turn away pregnant patients who are in the midst of medical emergencies \u8211\'20\'13 a move that comes amid multiple red-state court battles over the guidance.\par \par \uc2 The guidance deals with the federal Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act (Emtala), which requires hospitals to stabilize patients facing medical emergencies. States such as Idaho and Texas have argued that the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s guidance, which it issued in the wake of the 2022 overturning of Roe v Wade, interpreted Emtala incorrectly.\par \par \uc2 In its letter rescinding the guidance, the Trump administration said that the \uc2 Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services\uc2  (CMS) \u8220\'20\'1Cwill continue to enforce Emtala, which protects all individuals who present to a hospital emergency department seeking examination or treatment, including for identified emergency medical conditions that place the health of a pregnant woman or her unborn child in serious jeopardy. CMS will work to rectify any perceived legal confusion and instability created by the former administration\u8217\'20\'19s actions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters said on Tuesday that rescinding the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s guidance will muddy hospitals\u8217\'20\'19 ability to interpret Emtala and endanger pregnant patients\u8217\'20\'19 lives. Since Roe\u8217\'20\'19s collapse, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/04/tennessee-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dozens of women have come forward}}}\uc2  to say that they were denied medical treatment due to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/series/life-of-the-mother"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported five pregnant women have died}}}\uc2  after having their care denied or delayed, or being unable to access legal abortions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis action sends a clear message: the lives and health of pregnant people are not worth protecting,\u8221\'20\'1D Dr Jamila Perritt, an OB-GYN and the president of Physicians for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CComplying with this law can mean the difference between life and death for pregnant people, forcing providers like me to choose between caring for someone in their time of need and turning my back on them to comply with cruel and dangerous laws.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Last year, the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  heard arguments in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/23/supreme-court-verdict-emergency-abortions-patients-doctors"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 case involving Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban}}}\uc2 , which at the time only allowed abortions in cases where a woman\u8217\'20\'19s life was at risk. In contrast, most state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans permit abortions when a patient\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Chealth\u8221\'20\'1D is in danger \u8211\'20\'13 a lower standard that could make it easier for doctors to intervene. Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s standard, the Biden administration said, blocked doctors from providing abortions in some emergencies and thus violated Emtala\u8217\'20\'19s requirement that hospitals must stabilize patients.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, the supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jun/27/ketanji-brown-jackson-dissent-idaho-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 punted on the issue}}}\uc2  by ruling 6-3 on procedural grounds that the case had been \u8220\'20\'1Cimprovidently granted\u8221\'20\'1D, indicating they should have never taken it up in the first place.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis court had a chance to bring clarity and certainty to this tragic situation and we have squandered it,\u8221\'20\'1D wrote \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , the supreme court justice, at the time. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd for as long as we refuse to declare what the law requires, pregnant patients in Idaho, Texas and elsewhere will be paying the price.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration\u8217\'20\'19s Tuesday move is not unexpected. In March, the administration {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/mar/04/trump-attacks-abortion-rights-idaho-south-carolina"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 moved to drop out of the case}}}\uc2  over the Idaho \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. A local Idaho hospital later filed its own lawsuit over the ban.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250603el630030d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
