\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 New figures shed light on US  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  travel as Trump cuts tracking research\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 962 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 April 2025\par 14:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Guttmacher report finds 155,000 people crossed state lines for procedure \u8211\'20\'13 double number who did so before Roe\u8217\'20\'19s fall\par \par \uc2 For the second year in a row, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers performed more than 1m abortions in the United States in 2024. About 155,000 people crossed state lines for abortions \u8211\'20\'13 roughly double the number of patients who did so in 2020, before the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade and paved the way for more than a dozen state-level \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans to take effect.\par \par \uc2 These numbers, released earlier this week by the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights-supporting \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  , have not changed much since 2023, when the US also performed more than 1m abortions and 169,000 people traveled for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 This lack of change masks a deep geographical divide in the US, as a handful of states have now become major hubs for people seeking abortions.\par \par \uc2 In 2024, Illinois provided roughly 35,000 abortions to out-of-state patients, while North Carolina provided 16,700. Kansas and New Mexico, which neighbor anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Texas, provided 16,100 and 12,800 abortions \u8211\'20\'13 the vast majority of which were to out-of-state patients.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTravel is so incredibly costly, both for patients and for the broader support network of funds, practical support organizations and providers,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Isaac Maddow-Zimet\uc2  , a Guttmacher data scientist. The sheer magnitude of the travel, he said, is \u8220\'20\'1Ctestament to the great efforts that a lot of people are taking in order to make sure that people could access care that really they should be able to access within their own community \u8211\'20\'13 without necessarily expending this enormous financial and logistical cost\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 As the Guttmacher report focuses on abortions provided through the formal healthcare system, it does not count self-managed abortions, which appear to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/mar/25/self-managed-abortions-avoid-bans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 be on the rise post-Roe }}}\uc2 . (Medical experts widely agree that individuals can safely end their own first-trimester pregnancies using \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.) The report also does not track abortions provided to people living in states with total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, even though providers living in blue states sometimes mail \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills across state lines \u8211\'20\'13 a practice that, in recent months, has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/mar/27/new-york-abortion-pill-lawsuit-texas-fine"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sparked heated litigation }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This does not mean, researchers warned, that everybody who wants a post-Roe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still able to get one. Caitlin Myers, a \uc2 Middlebury College\uc2  economics professor who has researched the impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, estimated that about 20-25% of people who want abortions are blocked from getting them by bans.\par \par \uc2 The Guttmacher report arrived days after the Trump administration {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2025/04/13/abortions-cancer-in-firefighters-and-super-gonorrhea-rfk-jr-s-cuts-halt-data-collection-00284828"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 effectively demolished }}}\uc2  the CDC team responsible for government reports on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provision in the United States, which are known as \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  surveillance\u8221\'20\'1D reports. These cuts have alarmed researchers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf we can\u8217\'20\'19t measure outcomes, we can\u8217\'20\'19t do science,\u8221\'20\'1D Myers said. \u8220\'20\'1CMy concern is that this work fundamentally relies on the ability to track public health outcomes, and if we aren\u8217\'20\'19t tracking them, we don\u8217\'20\'19t know what\u8217\'20\'19s happening to people. We don\u8217\'20\'19t know what\u8217\'20\'19s happening in their lives. We don\u8217\'20\'19t know the effect of policies and interventions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In addition to the CDC and Guttmacher, just one other group \u8211\'20\'13 #WeCount, a research project by the Society of Family Planning \u8211\'20\'13 regularly collects nationwide data on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  incidence. The groups use different methods to collect data, so the CDC\u8217\'20\'19s annual report on the topic has long been less comprehensive than the post-Roe reports issued by Guttmacher and #WeCount.\par \par \uc2 A number of states \u8211\'20\'13 including California, a haven for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights \u8211\'20\'13 do not provide information to the federal government about the abortions performed within their borders. The CDC report also lags behind the Guttmacher and #WeCount reports; its most recent report, issued in November, counted abortions performed in 2022.\par \par \uc2 The CDC report does include information about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients\u8217\'20\'19 demographic backgrounds and the gestational age of their pregnancies, which can serve as a critical fact-check in heated debates around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists, for example, often condemn abortions that take place later on in pregnancy, but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/reproductive-health/data-statistics/abortion-surveillance-findings-reports.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the 2022 CDC report }}}\uc2  found that only about 1.1% of all abortions take place at or after 21 weeks\u8217\'20\'19 gestation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  surveillance can be used to assess changes in clinical practice patterns over time,\u8221\'20\'1D a former employee from the CDC\u8217\'20\'19s Division of \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a text. \u8220\'20\'1CWithout this report, we are losing the ability to track these changes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Notably, the move to ax the researchers behind the CDC report appears to run counter to Project 2025, a famous playbook of conservative policies. Project 2025 urged the federal government to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/aug/05/project-2025-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dramatically expand the CDC\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1C\b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  surveillance\u8221\'20\'1D }}}\uc2  by cutting funds to states that did not provide the CDC with information about \u8220\'20\'1Cexactly how many abortions take place within its borders, at what gestational age of the child, for what reason, the mother\u8217\'20\'19s state of residence, and by what method\u8221\'20\'1D. It also suggested that the CDC collect statistics on miscarriages, stillbirths and \u8220\'20\'1Ctreatments that incidentally result in the death of a child (such as chemotherapy)\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 These proposals alarmed researchers and raised concerns about patients\u8217\'20\'19 data privacy, especially given the fact that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains a deeply controversial procedure. But given the turbulence of the Trump administration, experts are not sure whether another agency will ultimately take up Project 2025\u8217\'20\'19s recommendations.\par \par \uc2 Maddow-Zimet said he doesn\u8217\'20\'19t \u8220\'20\'1Cthink that we\u8217\'20\'19re necessarily any less concerned than we were before about the possibility of these kind of data mandates going into effect and/or using the data in appropriate ways to either make providers ask patients questions they wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have otherwise asked, that are very stigmatizing, or potentially put providers at risk\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250420el4k000xd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
