\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Volunteers rush to send  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  pills to US women in need as \u8216\'20\'18war between the states\u8217\'20\'19 looms\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 2,386 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 April 2025\par 14:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Massachusetts \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  project pushes for access across country as controversial shield laws are put to legal test\par \par \uc2 Seated around a circular table in a nondescript office building just outside {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/boston"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Boston }}}\uc2 , the volunteers pack the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  pills into envelopes with practiced efficiency.\par \par \uc2 Each of the volunteers \u8211\'20\'13 five women and one man \u8211\'20\'13 have a unique role in the assembly line. One volunteer drops slim, orange boxes of mifepristone, the first drug typically used in a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , into the envelopes, while another volunteer adds green-capped bottles of the second drug, misoprostol. A few volunteers add brochures on topics such as how to use \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills or what to do if a woman suspects she has an ectopic pregnancy. Finally, one volunteer drops small purple cards into each envelope. They all bear the same handwritten message: \u8220\'20\'1CWe wish you the best.\u8221\'20\'1D The cards are signed with a swooping heart and a nondescript name: \u8220\'20\'1Cthe Map \u8221\'20\'1D, or the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/massachusetts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Massachusetts }}}\uc2  Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access Project .\par \par \uc2 By the end of the day, dozens of these envelopes will have been dropped off at a \uc2 US Postal Service\uc2  office \u8211\'20\'13 many on their way to people who live in states that have banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Map is one of a handful of organizations operating under a controversial legal innovation known as a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/article/2024/may/14/abortion-bans-shield-laws-telehealth-pills"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shield law }}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D. Enacted by eight states in the years since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v Wade }}}\uc2 , shield laws are designed to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers from red-state prosecutions and legal actions, even if the providers\u8217\'20\'19 patients are located in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/jul/29/abortion-laws-bans-by-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 states that ban \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Providers in shield law states routinely ship \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills across state lines: in spring 2024, they facilitated more than 7,700 monthly abortions in states with total or six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, according to #WeCount, a research project by the Society of Family Planning.\par \par \uc2 But shield laws are now being put to the test. In December, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/dec/13/texas-abortion-pills-lawsuit-ken-paxton"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas sued }}}\uc2  Dr Margaret Carpenter, a New York doctor, over allegations that she violated Texas\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans by mailing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to a Texas woman. Then, in January, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/31/new-york-louisiana-abortion-pill-charges"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a Louisiana grand jury }}}\uc2  criminally indicted Carpenter .\par \par \uc2 The New York governor, \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  , has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/mar/27/new-york-abortion-pill-lawsuit-texas-fine"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 refused to sign an extradition order }}}\uc2  for Carpenter . Citing the state\u8217\'20\'19s shield law, a New York county clerk has also refused to enforce a $113,000 fine, levied by a Texas court, against her. Now, Texas is expected to sue New York over the shield law \u8211\'20\'13 a move that could ultimately land the case in front of the supreme court, dominated 6-3 by conservatives, and tip the balance of power between states that protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and those that do not.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNo one wanted this, but it\u8217\'20\'19s not unexpected,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Angel Foster, the Map \u8217\'20\'19s co-founder, of the state-on-state fight. \u8220\'20\'1CI think we were prepared for this to happen, and we\u8217\'20\'19re waiting for the next shoe to drop.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Since the election, it\u8217\'20\'19s felt even more urgent to have a tangible and literally hands-on impact\par \par \uc2 Map volunteer\par \par \uc2 The legal battle has not slowed down demand at the Map . Before dropping their prices last year, the Map was mailing pills to about 500 patients a month. Now, it provides pills to roughly 2,500 per month.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSince the election, it\u8217\'20\'19s felt even more urgent to have a tangible and literally hands-on impact. A package is going to somebody who needs it, in a place where they are being denied their rights,\u8221\'20\'1D one Map volunteer said as she sealed envelopes. She spread her palm out on a package as if she were placing a hand on a Bible. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s going to a real person.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18War between the states\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 To order pills from the Map , a patient must be within their first trimester of pregnancy and at least 16, the age of consent for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under Massachusetts law. After they fill out an online intake form, a licensed clinician reviews their chart; if a patient has questions, someone from Map will take their call. Because the organization operates on a sliding scale, patients can receive pills for as little as $5.\par \par \uc2 On one recent Tuesday morning, Cheryl, a retired OB-GYN, sat in the Map \u8217\'20\'19s tiny, dimly lit office and quietly clicked through patients\u8217\'20\'19 charts, evaluating the answers to questions about the date of their last period and their past pregnancies. Patients shared why they wanted abortions, but Cheryl rarely lingered over their answers. One, however, struck her: a 25-year-old single mom who felt like another pregnancy would endanger her ability to take care of her child. It reminded Cheryl of the five years she spent providing abortions at Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s last \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, which shuttered shortly after the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2022 }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decision }}}\uc2  in Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization paved the way for state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans to unfurl across the US.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat was a really common refrain: \u8216\'20\'18I just want to do right by the kids I have,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Cheryl recalled. About half of the Map \u8217\'20\'19s patients, she said, already have children. They are also predominantly under 35 and people of color \u8211\'20\'13 similar to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients writ large, at least before Roe fell.\par \par \uc2 After Roe\u8217\'20\'19s collapse forced the Mississippi clinic to close, Cheryl started providing abortions in North Carolina \u8211\'20\'13 until that state banned the procedure after 12 weeks and the clinic where she worked no longer needed her services. \u8220\'20\'1CI was sitting at home, being sad and useless and doing local advocacy stuff,\u8221\'20\'1D Cheryl said. She also grew increasingly angry and frustrated with what she saw as mass complacency with the post-Roe reality.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s when someone told her: \u8220\'20\'1CI have just the group for you.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Working with the Map means assuming a certain level of risk. There is no way to guarantee that a staffer or volunteer won\u8217\'20\'19t get drawn into a lawsuit \u8211\'20\'13 or worse.\par \par \uc2 To diffuse risk, the Map never mails anything that includes clinicians\u8217\'20\'19 names. Foster no longer travels to or through states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, and does not drive outside of Massachusetts; she doesn\u8217\'20\'19t want to run the risk of getting pulled over for speeding and learning that another state has put out a warrant out for her arrest. This is also, in part, why the Map relies on an assembly line to put its packages together: there is no single person to point a finger at.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m feeling like the people that have the power to protect us really aren\u8217\'20\'19t, so we just have to keep moving along and doing what we think is right,\u8221\'20\'1D Cheryl said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s terrifying, but the whole world is terrifying. I feel like just walking down the street these days is terrifying. Someone\u8217\'20\'19s going to whisk you off and accuse you of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/mar/27/judge-explanation-tufts-student-rumeysa-ozturk-ice"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 writing an op-ed }}}\uc2  or something.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She asked to be identified only by her first name to protect her ability to travel in the US, although Cheryl has no plans to enter a state with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 As Cheryl worked, a US map dotted with silver stars glimmered on the wall above her head. Each star represented the location of a patient served in October 2023, the Map \u8217\'20\'19s first month of operation. Although sky-blue areas like the coast of Oregon glinted with stars, most were clustered in the south-eastern US, which is now blanketed in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. Today, a third of the Map \u8217\'20\'19s patients come from Texas, which outlaws virtually all abortions, while another third hail from Florida and Georgia, which both prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  past six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The map\u8217\'20\'19s constellations illuminate a paradox of the post-Roe US: even though 26 million women of reproductive age live under a total or six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, many are still receiving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills in the mail or crossing state lines to visit a brick-and-mortar clinic. In 2023 and 2024, the US saw more than 1m abortions \u8211\'20\'13 some of the highest numbers in a decade, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/apr/20/us-abortion-travel-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to the Guttmacher Institute }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The question is whether this paradox is sustainable. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists consider this kind of interstate networking an existential threat. While Texas and Louisiana have gone after Carpenter , a number of states have attempted to criminalize people who help others cross state lines for abortions. Courts have frozen many of those efforts, but these legal battles are far from over.\par \par \uc2 With different US states now home to fundamentally contradictory reproductive regimes, both sides \u8211\'20\'13 whether they seek to punish out-of-state travel or offer banned healthcare \u8211\'20\'13 are scrambling traditional codes of conduct between states, creating new questions about what the constitution allows. These questions are sure to end up before the supreme court.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere isn\u8217\'20\'19t really much of a precedent for anything like shield laws, and the courts are very conservative,\u8221\'20\'1D warned Mary Ziegler, a University of California, Davis School of Law professor who studies the legal history of reproduction.\par \par \uc2 The US constitution protects people\u8217\'20\'19s right to travel, but also mandates that states honor court rulings from other states \u8211\'20\'13 such as the fine that Texas won against Carpenter (because she and her lawyer did not show up to a court date in the state). In addition, the constitution specifies that, if an individual commits a crime in one state and \u8220\'20\'1Cshall flee from justice\u8221\'20\'1D to another state, that individual must be \u8220\'20\'1Cdelivered up\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 or extradited \u8211\'20\'13 back to the scene of the crime.\par \par \uc2 Yet there\u8217\'20\'19s no evidence that Carpenter and other shield law providers did \u8220\'20\'1Cflee from justice\u8221\'20\'1D; rather, they\u8217\'20\'19re practicing within and obeying the law of their own home states.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNew York is going to say: \u8216\'20\'18She\u8217\'20\'19s not a fugitive. This is not the kind of scenario where a court should get involved,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Ziegler said. \u8220\'20\'1CPrecedent would say they don\u8217\'20\'19t have to extradite her, and the question would become whether Louisiana can find a way around that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ziegler also questioned whether Texas could convince a court to force New York to collect its $113,00 fine. The constitution, she said, forces states to recognize fines levied in lawsuits between individuals \u8211\'20\'13 not necessarily fines that result from a lawsuit by a state against an individual.\par \par \uc2 But Steven Aden, the chief legal officer and general counsel at the powerful anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group \uc2 Americans United for Life\uc2  , is bullish about Texas\u8217\'20\'19s chances.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou can\u8217\'20\'19t go to Reno and incur a gambling debt in a casino and then go back home and raise a defense in court, when the casino comes after you for that gambling debt, by saying: \u8216\'20\'18We don\u8217\'20\'19t have gambling in our state,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Aden said.\par \par \uc2 Ziegler and Aden did agree on one thing, though: not only is the supreme court all but certain to take up Carpenter \u8217\'20\'19s case in one form or another, but the high court will likely see a deluge of similar cases over the next several years.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese are the first shots fired in what we like to call \u8211\'20\'13 what we reluctantly, I guess, call \u8211\'20\'13 a coming war between the states,\u8221\'20\'1D Aden said.\par \par \uc2 Experts {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/made-by-history/2022/07/14/what-pre-civil-war-history-tells-us-about-coming-abortion-battle/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have noted }}}\uc2  that the closest parallels are the pre-civil war battles over how to treat enslaved people who had escaped southern states (which permitted slavery) and fled to northern states (which did not). When these disputes reached the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  , as in the case of Dred Scott v Sandford, the court repeatedly sided with enslavers and lent power to the federal government to enforce pro-slavery laws.\par \par \uc2 The modern-day dispute between states over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is dramatically different from the 19th-century interstate battle over slavery \u8211\'20\'13 but \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s famous warning still seems to resonate: \u8220\'20\'1CA house divided cannot stand.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Can I volunteer?\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Other threats to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills could soon imperil shield-law providers, too. The attorneys general of Idaho, Kansas and Missouri are now {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/17/abortion-pill-mifepristone-lawsuit-texas"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pursuing a lawsuit }}}\uc2  that could roll back providers\u8217\'20\'19 ability to prescribe the pills through the mail. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists are also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/nov/17/anti-abortion-groups-trump-2025"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 trying to }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/nov/17/anti-abortion-groups-trump-2025"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cajole }}}\uc2  the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump administration }}}\uc2  to enforce the Comstock Act, a 19th-century anti-vice law that bans the mailing of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related materials but went dormant under Roe.\par \par \uc2 The Map would close if the law no longer protected its work, Foster said. But that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t necessarily mean she would give up.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe might, as a group of people, decide to pivot and do something that\u8217\'20\'19s around civil disobedience, and create a different kind of entity doing different work,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Even if anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  forces prevail in court, there is likely no way to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills out of US hands. The \uc2 US Postal Service\uc2  already fails, frequently, to detect the illicit drugs that swim through it. In addition to shield law \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, there is a thriving online market for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills that are sent straight from overseas pharmacies, allowing women to end their pregnancies without involving the formal US healthcare system. (Medical experts widely agree that it is safe to end your own pregnancy using pills in the first trimester of pregnancy.)\par \par \uc2 For now, the Map has no shortage of volunteers. As the volunteers stuffed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills into envelopes, a woman working in another part of the office building, who had no connection to the group, walked by the conference room and asked Foster: \u8220\'20\'1CCan I volunteer?\u8221\'20\'1D Another bystander told them: \u8220\'20\'1CThank you guys for what you\u8217\'20\'19re doing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It took less than two hours for the volunteers to package some 200 envelopes. Soon after they departed, another pair of volunteers arrived to add shipping labels and drop off the packages, discretely packed into a bin, at a nearby post office. In January, the Map was mailing roughly 150 packages a day \u8211\'20\'13 in part, Map project manager Andrea suspects, due to fears surrounding {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump }}}\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s inauguration \u8211\'20\'13 but that volume has slowed, to about 65 to 85 packages a day. (Andrea asked to be identified by her first name only.)\par \par \uc2 A woman working at the post office once asked Andrea if she was running a jewelry business, given the number of shipments and the rattling sounds each package made. She had mistaken the pills for beads.\par \par \uc2 Andrea smiled. She did not confirm or deny.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250426el4q001gt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
