\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Meet the new American refugees fleeing across state lines for safety\b0\par\par\uc2 Ed Pilkington \par \uc2 4,670 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 April 2025\par 08:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Americans have often moved between states for opportunities. Now they\u8217\'20\'19re being forced to uproot themselves to escape hostile forces under Trump\par \par \uc2 America is on the move. Hundreds of thousands of people are packing up boxes, loading U-Hauls, and shipping out of state in an urgent flight towards safety.\par \par \uc2 They\u8217\'20\'19re being propelled by hostile political forces bearing down on them because of who they are, what they believe, or for their medical needs.\par \par \uc2 All are displaced within their own country for reasons they did not choose. They are the new generation of America\u8217\'20\'19s internal refugees \u8211\'20\'13 and their ranks are growing by the day.\par \par \uc2 Here, we profile families who have joined America\u8217\'20\'19s swelling class of internal refugees. They represent just five stories among many others which might have been included: librarians fleeing book bans, professors forced out by ideologically-motivated college {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/nov/11/new-college-of-florida-rightwing-takeover"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 education boards}}}\uc2 , and most recently, the thousands of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/apr/01/trump-health-agency-layoffs-cuts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 federal employees}}}\uc2  fired by the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Such is the tide of coerced dislocation, in the land of the free.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 The history teacher\par \par \uc2 John Dube\par \par \uc2 Moved from New Hampshire to Vermont to escape school censorship\par \par \uc2 John Dube, 63, takes great pride in his decades-long public service. He has been a high school history teacher for the past 35 years.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI encourage an inquiry- and evidence-based approach,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a mantra of mine: if you have an understanding of the past, you can understand a lot about the present.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s the sensibility he infused into his classroom at Timberlane regional high school in Plaistow, New Hampshire, where he taught for more than 30 years. The town is also where he brought up his two children, living what he described as a \u8220\'20\'1Cnormal, some would say boring, suburban life\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It stopped being normal around four years ago, when headwinds started to build over what rightwing groups claimed was the victimization of white kids through the teaching of \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/nov/03/republicans-critical-race-theory-winning-electoral-issue"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 critical race theory}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D (CRT). You won\u8217\'20\'19t find CRT mentioned in any of Dube\u8217\'20\'19s courses, and yet it became a battlefield in the culture wars and in his life.\par \par \uc2 New Hampshire\u8217\'20\'19s anti-CRT measure, HB 2, became law in 2021. It banned the teaching of \u8220\'20\'1Cdivisive concepts\u8221\'20\'1D in schools, meaning that teachers would no longer be allowed to discuss race, gender identity or sexual orientation in the classroom. They were explicitly forbidden from promoting ideas suggesting that a person is inherently racist, sexist, or oppressive solely based on their race or sex, whether they are aware of it or not.\par \par \uc2 To aid enforcement, the state set up an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nhpr.org/nh-news/2021-11-10/nh-discrimination-teachers-system"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 online portal}}}\uc2  where parents and students could snitch on teachers, who could then face possible dismissal or being stripped of their teaching licenses. The culture wars group {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.forbes.com/sites/petergreene/2021/11/12/new-hampshire-and-moms-for-liberty-put-bounty-on-teachers-heads/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Moms for Liberty}}}\uc2  backed that up with a $500 bounty for information.\par \par \uc2 Dube initially treated the CRT furor lightly, assuming it would quickly flame out. He was astounded when the bill became law (the legislation was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2024/05/28/new-hampshire-school-civil-rights-ruling-00160238"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blocked by a federal judge}}}\uc2  last May as unconstitutionally vague, and is now under appeal).\par \par \uc2 For a start, how could you avoid \u8220\'20\'1Cdivisive concepts\u8221\'20\'1D when teaching history, a subject that is divisive by its nature? \u8220\'20\'1CHistory isn\u8217\'20\'19t a science. It\u8217\'20\'19s not like the law of gravity. Why did the Japanese bomb Pearl Harbor? You can answer that in many different ways. It\u8217\'20\'19s all in the debate.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He was also profoundly disturbed by the political intentions behind the law; being a history teacher, he saw the implications immediately. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s the old adage: control the past, control the future. It\u8217\'20\'19s how totalitarian regimes take away people\u8217\'20\'19s rights \u8211\'20\'13 by literally rewriting history.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He was angered by the edicts of Orwellian thought police in a state whose motto is \u8220\'20\'1CLive free or die\u8221\'20\'1D. In that spirit, he added his signature to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.zinnedproject.org/news/pledge-to-teach-truth?signature_page=161"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nationwide petition}}}\uc2  called a \u8220\'20\'1Cpledge to teach the truth\u8221\'20\'1D, which vowed to oppose state-sponsored censorship.\par \par \uc2 Dube added a note explaining why he had signed. It said: \u8220\'20\'1CI believe in encouraging my students to think critically.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The backlash was instant. Granite Grok, a local rightwing website, posted the names of all New Hampshire signatories, and within hours of that Dube received a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  message that read: \u8220\'20\'1CWhats up homo? I heard your teaching Marxist commie CRT in your classrooms. You can fuck right off you garbage human.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Dube calmly replied that he would not be intimidated.\par \par \uc2 Within days, police officers turned up at his house, having been dispatched by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . Dube\u8217\'20\'19s name was circulating on obscure chatrooms frequented by violent militia members. He was urged to install security cameras at home, but when he asked why the police didn\u8217\'20\'19t arrest the perpetrators of the threats, he was told that was impossible on free speech grounds.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was like: are you kidding me? My life has been turned upside down in a moment because I pledged to teach the truth, and the police are preaching freedom of speech?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Over the next few months, the attrition became unbearable for Dube and his wife. They were afraid every time a car drove down their cul-de-sac. It was as if the New Hampshire they had fallen in love with years ago was vanishing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt just destroyed it. It wasn\u8217\'20\'19t about partisanship \u8211\'20\'13 we\u8217\'20\'19ve been used to Republicans and Democrats arguing forever. It was about a climate being created where you don\u8217\'20\'19t feel safe.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In the end, they cut their losses. They vacated their home of decades, abandoned family and friends, and headed for the Montpelier area of Vermont.\par \par \uc2 The transition has been tough. Dube traded in his job as a respected long-time history teacher for a role as substitute. He still feels unsettled, like the internal refugee he has become.\par \par \uc2 He thinks of the past \u8211\'20\'13 of the Von Trapp family of Sound of Music fame, who established their lodge in Stowe, Vermont, just 20 miles from his current home, having fled Austria shortly after the Nazi annexation. And he worries about the future. The New Hampshire model of educational intolerance is rapidly spreading nationwide: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  recently signed an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/mar/27/trump-smithsonian-executive-order"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 executive order}}}\uc2  that seeks to impose the same ban on \u8220\'20\'1Cdivisive\u8221\'20\'1D history on the \uc2 Smithsonian Institution\uc2 , the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest museum and education complex.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAmerica is a flawed country. We\u8217\'20\'19ve made tremendous mistakes,\u8221\'20\'1D Dube said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut we\u8217\'20\'19ve always tried to learn from those mistakes to become better. That\u8217\'20\'19s the America that I\u8217\'20\'19ve spent my life teaching about \u8211\'20\'13 and this is not that America.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 The patient and her physician\par \par \uc2 Kayla Smith and Kylie Cooper\par \par \uc2 Left Idaho for Washington and Minnesota to flee a strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban\par \par \uc2 Kayla Smith can pinpoint the exact moment she knew she had to leave. It was early in her third pregnancy, and she was scared.\par \par \uc2 Her first pregnancy had been difficult. She\u8217\'20\'19d had severe pre-eclampsia \u8211\'20\'13 high blood pressure that can be life-threatening \u8211\'20\'13 and her daughter Addison had been born at 33 weeks. Her second pregnancy had ended tragically when she lost her son at 21 weeks due to a fatal heart defect.\par \par \uc2 Now it was third time lucky, and Smith was seeking reassurance from her doctor. Would she and her baby be OK?\par \par \uc2 The physician looked at her and said: \u8220\'20\'1CIdaho is not a safe place to be pregnant right now.\u8221\'20\'1D Then, as the devastating truth sank in, he added: \u8220\'20\'1CIf you were my daughter, I\u8217\'20\'19d have told you not to get pregnant in this state.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 There was little to discuss after that. \u8220\'20\'1CThat pretty much sealed the deal for us,\u8221\'20\'1D Smith said.\par \par \uc2 The number of people internally displaced by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws is startling. A recent study published by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nber.org/papers/w33328"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 National Bureau of Economic Research}}}\uc2  of 13 states with strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans found that, between them, they are losing 36,000 residents every {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/abortion-ban-laws-linked-to-people-leaving-states-research/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 three months}}}\uc2 . Separate research released this month by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://iwpr.org/reproductive-health-care-and-job-decisions/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Institute for Women\u8217\'20\'19s Policy Research}}}\uc2  revealed that one in five people planning to have children within the next decade have moved state, or know someone who has done so, to escape \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Idaho has one of the most coldly unbending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans; the state\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reproductiverights.org/maps/state/idaho/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Defense of Life Act }}}\uc2  is an almost total prohibition.\par \par \uc2 The ban deeply affected how Smith\u8217\'20\'19s second pregnancy unfolded. Two days after the state\u8217\'20\'19s so-called \u8220\'20\'1Ctrigger law\u8221\'20\'1D became active, her baby \u8211\'20\'13 a boy she and her husband James had already named Brooks \u8211\'20\'13 was diagnosed with such a serious cardiac condition that they were told he was unlikely to survive beyond birth.\par \par \uc2 Were Smith to develop complications, her doctors told her they could not protect her: they would only be able to intervene if she were in mortal danger, by which point it might be too late. (The law only makes exception for rape, incest and where a doctor can affirmatively prove they acted to prevent the pregnant woman\u8217\'20\'19s death \u8211\'20\'13 a concept that is so vaguely worded that it encourages doctors to wait until patients become sicker to avoid being prosecuted.)\par \par \uc2 So the Smiths took out a $16,000 personal loan to cover the costs of the procedure, travelled across state lines into neighboring Washington and, grieving their son, had a medically induced termination.\par \par \uc2 Afterwards, they returned home, and eventually tried for another baby. They weren\u8217\'20\'19t yet ready to give up on Idaho. Kayla, 33, had moved there for college, and James, one year her junior, had been to school and trained as a pharmacist in the state. By the time of their third pregnancy, James had been promoted to pharmacy manager, Kayla was caring for Addison at home, and they had built their own home. They were living the Idaho dream.\par \par \uc2 But soon, the couple found themselves locked into a repetitive conversation. \u8220\'20\'1CWe had it nightly for months,\u8221\'20\'1D Kayla said, \u8220\'20\'1Cand we always came to the same decision \u8211\'20\'13 we probably need to leave.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Part of the motivation was discovering that they were expecting another girl, Nora. They started wondering what it would be like raising Addison and Nora in a state that would offer them no bodily autonomy. They knew too that in this third pregnancy, Smith\u8217\'20\'19s life was potentially at risk given her history of pre-eclampsia.\par \par \uc2 And so, in September 2023, the Smith family joined the ranks of America\u8217\'20\'19s internal refugees. They relocated permanently to Washington state, where Kayla gave birth to Nora 16 months ago.\par \par \uc2 They were not alone. Kylie Cooper was the physician specializing in high-risk pregnancies who gave the Smiths the devastating news about their son Brooks\u8217\'20\'19 fetal abnormalities. Smith was the first patient Cooper saw after the Idaho trigger law kicked in.\par \par \uc2 Under the law, doctors face up to five years in prison if they perform abortions not covered by exceptions. The onus of proof is on them \u8211\'20\'13 they must be able to convince a jury that termination was necessary to save the mother\u8217\'20\'19s life, an exceptionally hard standard to meet given often nuanced medical decisions and the vague terminology of Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI felt tremendous fear and sadness for my patients, as my ability to help them had been taken away. And I felt fear for myself, like I had a target on my back,\u8221\'20\'1D Cooper told the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 Cooper, her husband and two young children left Idaho in April 2023 and now live in Minnesota. Six of Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s initial nine maternal fetal medicine specialists have abandoned the state or retired since the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, with almost {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.idahostatesman.com/living/health-fitness/article285692341.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one in four}}}\uc2  of all OB-GYNs leaving.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTo have to move state to be able to practice medicine \u8230\'20\'26 I did not expect that in modern-day America,\u8221\'20\'1D Cooper said.\par \par \uc2 The Smiths\u8217\'20\'19 own displacement has cost them dear. In the move to Washington, where they now live in Olympia, James lost his manager\u8217\'20\'19s position. \u8220\'20\'1CI had a reputation in Idaho, and now I\u8217\'20\'19ve got to prove myself all over again.\u8221\'20\'1D They gave up the house they owned and are renting again, at a premium. To make up the difference, Kayla has gone back to work, which she regrets, as she spends less time with their daughters.\par \par \uc2 Addison, now four, keeps asking where all the snow has gone. She used to love stepping out of their old Idaho home into the winter whiteness.\par \par \uc2 Along with three other women who were also forced to leave Idaho to get abortions necessary to safeguard their lives, Smith {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reproductiverights.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/ID-Complaint-Final-9-12.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 joined a lawsuit}}}\uc2  against the state brought by the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The suit sought to widen the ban\u8217\'20\'19s exceptions to cover fatal fetal diagnoses such as her son\u8217\'20\'19s.\par \par \uc2 A state court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reproductiverights.org/judgment-adkins-v-idaho/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled last week}}}\uc2  that medical exceptions to the ban should be broadened to allow women like Smith who have a health condition or pregnancy complication that puts their own life at risk to obtain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. However, the judge did not go so far as to include pregnant women with lethal fetal conditions who are not in danger themselves, leaving them still in a bind in which they must either carry a doomed pregnancy against their will or flee the state.\par \par \uc2 Despite this partial victory, Smith can feel the mood darkening across the US. Some {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/womens-health-policy/dashboard/abortion-in-the-u-s-dashboard/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 18 states}}}\uc2  have imposed total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans or restrictions on terminations beyond 12 weeks of pregnancy. At the federal level, Trump has issued an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2025/01/enforcing-the-hyde-amendment/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 executive order}}}\uc2  undoing his predecessor \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s efforts to preserve access to reproductive care.\par \par \uc2 In 32 years, James Smith has never been outside the US. Nor has he ever possessed a passport. Now he is applying for one for himself, and for his two girls. \u8220\'20\'1CIf things keep going south, I\u8217\'20\'19d rather be prepared.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 The mom of a trans teen\par \par \uc2 Anonymous\par \par \uc2 Moving her trans daughter from Texas to a west coast state for her protection\par \par \uc2 Sandra, the mother of a 15-year-old trans girl, has also started to probe the logistics of leaving the US. She spends evenings researching how to migrate to other countries, and the various health systems they would encounter.\par \par \uc2 Several of the families who belong to an online community frequented by Sandra have already gone. One family went to Canada, another to Uruguay, a third to New Zealand.\par \par \uc2 For Sandra, 41, though, dreams of leaving the country are not for now. She has a more pressing task: to get her daughter to safety by ushering her out of Texas and resettling her on the west coast (it is an indication of the extent of her fears that she asked the Guardian not to specify their new state, and Sandra is a pseudonym, also at her request).\par \par \uc2 Sandra has, with the help of a friend, set up a \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2  page to cover some of the costs of relocation. It has reached $6,000 \u8211\'20\'13 a fraction of the at least $20,000 she estimates will be needed to move the family and the small business they run to a safe haven.\par \par \uc2 It will be worth it, she thinks, if it buys them and their teenager security and peace of mind. She expressed the urgency of this moment for families like hers when we first exchanged emails.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are refugees and many of us are in hiding,\u8221\'20\'1D she wrote to me. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s immense and lonely.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Sandra\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Ckiddo\u8221\'20\'1D, as she calls her child, identified as a girl from as young as four. They had been at a family gathering with cousins, and in the car on the way home the child began sobbing, imploring her parents to stop calling her a boy.\par \par \uc2 In the more than 10 years since then, Marie (also not her real name) has never wavered in her gender identification. Through on-going therapy, medical visits, and parentally- and medically approved hormonal treatment, she has been categoric that she is a girl. She\u8217\'20\'19s done so while insisting on her privacy, eschewing becoming a poster child of the transgender cause. She wants minimum fuss.\par \par \uc2 Sandra and Marie\u8217\'20\'19s father have followed Marie\u8217\'20\'19s lead. In the decade since the meltdown in the car, Sandra has told nobody that her child is trans: to her friends and acquaintances, Sandra presents as a regular mom to a regular teenaged girl.\par \par \uc2 But then the political climate turned against them. The more the Texas legislature has fallen under the grip of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s Maga Republicans, the more trans families have become the subject of their ire.\par \par \uc2 By 2021, Texas had introduced {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://19thnews.org/2021/09/texas-is-pushing-the-most-anti-trans-bills-in-the-country-advocates-fear-deadly-consequences/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 40 anti-trans bills}}}\uc2 , vastly more than any other state. Since then, the bombardment has only intensified. In 2022 the Republican governor of Texas, Greg Abbott, ordered officials to investigate trans families for child abuse. The move \u8211\'20\'13 a direct threat to Sandra and her family \u8211\'20\'13 is currently on hold pending appeal.\par \par \uc2 The following year, lawmakers banned puberty blockers and hormone therapy for trans kids, forcing Sandra to go out of state at great expense to find care for her daughter.\par \par \uc2 In this year\u8217\'20\'19s legislative session, no fewer than 125 anti-trans bills have been introduced, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://translegislation.com/bills/2025/TX"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trans Legislation Tracker}}}\uc2 , covering almost every aspect of a trans person\u8217\'20\'19s life. In March, arguably the most insidious bill so far was tabled, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.statesman.com/story/news/state/2025/03/11/texas-anti-trans-bill-transgender-gender-identity-fraud-state-felony-tom-oliverson-house-bill-3817/82261151007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 HB 3817}}}\uc2 . It would effectively criminalize being transgender, by creating a new state felony of \u8220\'20\'1Cgender identity fraud\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf that were to pass, someone could arrest my teenager and she would have a felony on her record for the rest of her life,\u8221\'20\'1D Sandra said.\par \par \uc2 That the state is bearing down on her family is hard enough. But what has amplified the terror is how the hostility has spread out from the Texas capitol in Austin into the community. Parents in schools near their home have demanded to be notified of any trans students who might be sharing bathrooms with their children \u8211\'20\'13 effectively baying for the outing of kids like Marie.\par \par \uc2 Sandra had hoped they might have another three or four years in Texas, long enough for Marie to complete high school. But as Trump\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/20/trump-executive-order-gender-sex"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 attacks}}}\uc2  on trans people have come {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/28/trump-executive-order-transgender-transition"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 thick}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/05/trump-trans-athletes-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fast}}}\uc2 , the family is nowscrambling to get the teenager out by the end of summer.\par \par \uc2 The blitz of federal actions has left Sandra angry, sad and confused. She keeps asking herself: why should the US president, who has the world to worry about, be focused on her family?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re just a regular American family doing the laundry, buying groceries, making sure the kid does homework. To have the president obsess on us is bizarre, and very intrusive.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s as though the world has steadily closed in on them, Sandra said. Asked how that felt, she replied: \u8220\'20\'1CI imagine it\u8217\'20\'19s what it\u8217\'20\'19s like being surrounded by a wildfire.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 The climate evacuee\par \par \uc2 Amanda McPhillips\par \par \uc2 Quit California for New York to escape the fires\par \par \uc2 It was 4.30am and Amanda McPhillips had already taken her four egg-laying chickens out of the yard and into the bathtub for their protection. She had a bag with all her essentials \u8211\'20\'13 passports, birth certificates \u8211\'20\'13 packed by the door.\par \par \uc2 Despite the assiduous preparations, she still wasn\u8217\'20\'19t ready for the apocalyptic scene outside.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe air was deep orange. All of a sudden the evacuation zone on my phone was all around us. I was like, \u8216\'20\'18Oh, this isn\u8217\'20\'19t good. We should go.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That was the start of McPhillips\u8217\'20\'19s journey into displacement. In 2022, more than half a million people fled their homes in the US as a result of environmental disasters, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.internal-displacement.org/database/displacement-data/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Internal Displacement Monitoring Center}}}\uc2 . The statistics do not reveal how many permanently relocated, but they do indicate the scale of human upheaval.\par \par \uc2 Look at it one way, and McPhillips, 45, was one of the lucky ones.\par \par \uc2 Her rented home in Pasadena remained standing in January\u8217\'20\'19s devastating Los Angeles fires, while more than 6,000 homes in neighboring Altadena were wiped out. But the smoke damage to their house was intense, and it was within a few hundred yards of the burn area which threatened the family\u8217\'20\'19s long-term health.\par \par \uc2 What really did it, though, was that her six-year-old son\u8217\'20\'19s elementary school burned to the ground. McPhillips was shocked by how profoundly that affected them.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI hadn\u8217\'20\'19t realised how much of an anchor a school is with small kids. Our whole life revolved around it \u8211\'20\'13 pick-up and drop-off, play dates, extracurricular activities. Everything.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Today, McPhillips and her kids find themselves almost 3,000 miles away, sheltered in the East Hamptons hamlet of Amagansett on Long Island, New York. The contrast could not be more stark.\par \par \uc2 Charred embers have been replaced by immaculate white fences, sparkling shingle houses, and topiary. A short drive away from McPhillips\u8217\'20\'19s current home, Main Street offers day spas, organic restaurants and gourmet delis; the Atlantic Ocean is about 200 yards from their door.\par \par \uc2 McPhillips\u8217\'20\'19s friend from her years working in the New York TV industry offered her use of a vacant beach house as a temporary refuge. There is a public elementary school close by, with only 16 students per class.\par \par \uc2 McPhillips is well aware of her good fortune. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m extremely privileged; I know that. People have been so kind. I\u8217\'20\'19ve had a soft landing, and I have choices.\u8221\'20\'1D And yet, being an internally displaced person is not easy. Anything stable in her life is gone, shattered along with the school that held it together.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI feel so disheveled, so discombobulated. It\u8217\'20\'19s like there\u8217\'20\'19s no North Star for recovery \u8211\'20\'13 I don\u8217\'20\'19t know where we\u8217\'20\'19ll be in five months, let alone five years.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Unlike the other internal refugees profiled here, McPhillips has not been forced to relocate because of who she is or what she does. But the way she sees it, she shares with the others the fact that her life has been torn apart as a result of human failings, and worse.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe haven\u8217\'20\'19t fled violence or political oppression, but we have been displaced by decades of failure to deal with climate change. And the climate stuff is the scariest, because you cannot reverse it. You cannot go back and fix it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s actions since returning to the White House have supercharged her anxieties. He {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/17/trump-climate-change-la-fires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blamed the LA fires}}}\uc2  on local Democratic politicians, and is presiding over a massive {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2025/mar/12/epa-trump-climate-rules"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rollback of regulations}}}\uc2  that will hamper the fight against rising temperatures and put hundreds of thousands of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/mar/19/trump-epa-pollution-regulation-cuts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lives at risk}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When she\u8217\'20\'19s at her lowest, McPhillips starts to think that it\u8217\'20\'19s all futile. \u8220\'20\'1CIt feels overwhelming, as though there\u8217\'20\'19s nothing you can do. So many people are hurting, and yet there\u8217\'20\'19s nothing that will change Trump\u8217\'20\'19s mind.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Now she is on borrowed time. She and her boys will have to leave their Amagansett refuge in June ahead of the summer Hamptons rush.\par \par \uc2 Where will she go? She could go back to LA, but the city is still reeling. She\u8217\'20\'19s so sensitized to environmental disasters now that wherever they moved would have to be safe from the effects of the climate crisis. But where is such a place?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI met an extreme climate expert soon after the fires and I asked him where it would be safe to live,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CHe said he had identified two counties in Vermont as the safest in the US. Two months later, both counties were devastated by flooding.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 The scientist\par \par \uc2 Kristy Lewis\par \par \uc2 Abandoned Florida for Rhode Island for academic freedom\par \par \uc2 McPhillips\u8217\'20\'19s craving for reliable information on where to shelter from the climate crisis faces another challenge: even the scientists who produce such intelligence are now being displaced by hostile forces.\par \par \uc2 Five years ago Kristy Lewis, 47, was a climate scientist at the top of her game, running an oceanography lab under her own name at a prestigious public university in Florida. She was researching how to mitigate the effects of sea-level rise on the most vulnerable coastal populations \u8211\'20\'13 a subject particularly pertinent to Florida, the state with the longest coastline in the contiguous US.\par \par \uc2 Ever since she was little, Lewis had dreamed about becoming a marine biologist. She\u8217\'20\'19d spent her summers snorkeling off the coast of Florida, fishing from her dad\u8217\'20\'19s boat. \u8220\'20\'1CThey just couldn\u8217\'20\'19t get me out of the water,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Fast forward to 2019 and she was heading the \u8220\'20\'1CLewis Lab of Applied Coastal Ecology\u8221\'20\'1D at the \uc2 University of Central Florida\uc2 . She raised $8m in research funds, leading a team of six graduate research students.\par \par \uc2 Then \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  was elected Republican governor of Florida. Almost immediately, he began to shift how the state approached climate disasters: he would invest in adapting to the consequences of sea-level rise, but would not address the root causes.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe idea of \u8216\'20\'18climate change\u8217\'20\'19 has become politicised,\u8221\'20\'1D DeSantis said in April 2019. \u8220\'20\'1CMy environmental policy is just to try to do things that benefit Floridians.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The rhetorical chill soon began to affect Lewis. She was disturbed when the state allowed students to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/apr/25/florida-bill-record-professors-universities"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 record their professors\u8217\'20\'19 lectures}}}\uc2  without consent, and inform on them if they showed signs of political \u8220\'20\'1Cbias\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere was a clear and imminent risk. I was talking to students about complex topics like climate change, and it was unnerving to think that someone might record me because they thought I was doing them harm. For me, climate change isn\u8217\'20\'19t a political issue \u8211\'20\'13 I know it is real because I have the data.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 What most upset her was the effect on her scientific research. About 40% of her lab\u8217\'20\'19s income came from state funding, which put it in danger as climate crisis denialism spread.\par \par \uc2 As a queer person, she was also dismayed by the introduction of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/28/dont-say-gay-bill-florida-ron-desantis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cdon\u8217\'20\'19t say gay\u8221\'20\'1D bill}}}\uc2 , which banned classroom discussion on sexual orientation or gender identity. And as the chair of the College of Sciences\u8217\'20\'19 diversity, equity and inclusion committee, she was disheartened by the clampdown on DEI initiatives \u8211\'20\'13 especially as a female scientist in a predominantly male scientific world.\par \par \uc2 She would hear colleagues talk about the need to change the vocabulary they used. They should drop \u8220\'20\'1Cclimate crisis\u8221\'20\'1D and use \u8220\'20\'1Cenvironmental change\u8221\'20\'1D instead.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt became exhausting to think about the politics all the time. It was a big distraction from my science, not knowing what was going to happen next. I just wanted to do my job.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She left Florida in 2023, along with five other professors in UCF\u8217\'20\'19s College of Sciences who also departed that year. It was just in the nick of time. A few months after she left, DeSantis signed into law {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://floridaphoenix.com/2024/05/15/desantis-signs-bill-erasing-the-term-climate-change-from-state-law/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 HB 1645}}}\uc2  erasing the phrase \u8220\'20\'1Cclimate change\u8221\'20\'1D from state statutes.\par \par \uc2 Like McPhillips, Lewis is profoundly conscious of how she was one of the fortunate ones. \u8220\'20\'1CI left because I felt an attack on my personal and professional self, and that was not OK with me. Not everyone has that privilege \u8211\'20\'13 many have no option but to stay.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She has landed well. She is rebuilding her Lewis Lab within the \uc2 University of Rhode Island\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s school of oceanography, a hallowed place from which in 1985 Bob Ballard discovered the wreck of the Titanic.\par \par \uc2 But she, too, has paid a price. The relocation itself cost her $14,000. Her team of researchers has been splintered, and she is having to reassemble it slowly over time.\par \par \uc2 The move set her back three years in her bid for a tenured academic position. That matters, as without tenure she is more exposed to the icy winds that blow.\par \par \uc2 Those winds are certainly blowing now. This month, she received a letter from the Trump administration informing her that two federal grants she had already been awarded for research into offshore wind energy, amounting to $1.5m, had been cancelled.\par \par \uc2 They no longer met \u8220\'20\'1Cfunding priorities\u8221\'20\'1D, she was told.\par \par \uc2 Lewis fled Florida to escape the attack on her science. Now there is nowhere left to hide.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250424el4o00105\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
