\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 How idea of charging women with murder infiltrated the anti- \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  movement\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,400 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 April 2025\par 16:08 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Abolitionists\u8217\'20\'19 have migrated out of the fringes and moved toward the center of movement alongside Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 penchant for punishment\par \par \uc2 So far this year, lawmakers in at least 12 states have introduced legislation that would treat fetuses as people and leave women who have abortions vulnerable to being charged with homicide \u8211\'20\'13 a charge that, in several of these states, carries the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 Once seen as politically toxic, this kind of legislation has become more popular in the years since Roe v Wade fell, erasing the national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This likely comes as no surprise to Mary Ziegler, a professor at the University of California, Davis School of Law and one of the foremost commentators on the US \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars. The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, she writes in her new book Personhood: The New Civil War Over Reproduction, has really \u8220\'20\'1Calways been a fetal-personhood movement\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 one that is so emboldened, it is increasingly unconcerned with public opinion or even democratic norms.\par \par \uc2 Fetal personhood is the belief that embryos and fetuses deserve the legal rights and protections afforded to people. If taken to its logical conclusion in US law, this belief would not only totally outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but also risk pitting the rights of fetuses against the rights of the women carrying them in all kinds of circumstances. In the first year after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/sep/24/abortion-prosecutions-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 at least 200 people were prosecuted}}}\uc2  for conduct relating to their pregnancies. Most of these cases involved charges of child endangerment, abuse or neglect \u8211\'20\'13 language that treats fetuses as children.\par \par \uc2 A Guggenheim fellow who has published seven books on reproduction and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Ziegler has long seen the fight for fetal personhood as the throughline in the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement\u8217\'20\'19s centuries-long history. In recent years, with Roe gone, she grew convinced that the movement was no longer willing to pull punches in that fight.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe trajectory has been more and more punitive, more focused on saying: \u8216\'20\'18Justice for the fetus means punishing a larger group of people that encompasses not just doctors, but people who assist them, and potentially \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers themselves,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D she said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Personhood, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bookshop.org/p/books/personhood-the-new-civil-war-over-reproduction-mary-ziegler/21692901?ean=9780300273045&next=t"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 which was released Tuesday}}}\uc2 , argues that the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement did not have to be so punitive. While anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have never agreed on how to best implement fetal personhood, they over time started to take more cues from Republican politicians, whose own veneration of \u8220\'20\'1Claw and order\u8221\'20\'1D grew alongside its antagonism toward a social safety net. Those developments ultimately paved the way for the more extreme anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists to grab more power within the movement.\par \par \uc2 After Roe legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide in 1973, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists organized in support of an amendment to the US constitution that would recognize embryos and fetuses as people. (The effort never gained the political traction it needed to become a serious possibility.) Around the same time, some activists broke with mainstream anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups to push for policies that would offer more support for pregnant women, rather than just ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Yet the voices advocating for better daycare and housing for mothers faded away as the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement tightened its alliance with the Republican party. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists understood that, by portraying fetuses as defenseless victims of the ultimate crime, they could energize conservative voters \u8211\'20\'13 and open their pockets.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Republican party, from the Reagan era onward, has really equated a kind of justice and equality with punishing wrongdoers,\u8221\'20\'1D Ziegler said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a natural fit for the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement: the more its fortunes relied on Republican politicians, the more it framed things the same way.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 State-level bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  still do not technically punish \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients. (Instead, it\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, long positioned by the movement as coercing or confusing women into the procedure, and sometimes the people who help them, who are at risk of criminal consequences.)\par \par \uc2 But, over the last few years, self-described \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  abolitionists\u8221\'20\'1D have slowly migrated out of the fringes of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement and moved towards its center. Unlike mainstream \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-lifers\u8221\'20\'1D, \u8220\'20\'1Cabolitionists\u8221\'20\'1D believe that, if a fetus is a person and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the murder of that person, women should be punished accordingly: as murderers.\par \par \uc2 Neither \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-lifers\u8221\'20\'1D nor \u8220\'20\'1Cabolitionists\u8221\'20\'1D are particularly popular. After Roe\u8217\'20\'19s collapse enabled states to make their own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, several states \u8211\'20\'13 including GOP strongholds like Ohio and Missouri \u8211\'20\'13 passed ballot measures to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/1576/abortion.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 According to Gallup}}}\uc2 , 85% of Americans think that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or some circumstances, while a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://19thnews.org/2024/09/poll-americans-abortion-ivf-fetal-personhood-rights/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2024 survey from the 19th News and SurveyMonkey}}}\uc2  found that 41% of Americans think \u8220\'20\'1Ca fetus has rights starting at conception that should be protected by the government.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Even people who support fetal personhood can have tangled, even contradictory views on the matter. Only 9% of people think that the government should restrict access to in vitro fertilization, or IVF, even though IVF as it is currently practiced frequently involves the destruction or abandonment of embryos.\par \par \uc2 None of the bills that allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients to be charged with homicide are likely to pass this year, but Ziegler doesn\u8217\'20\'19t think \u8220\'20\'1Cabolitionists\u8221\'20\'1D are going anywhere, for two reasons. First, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement maintains a strong grip in southern Republican-dominated state legislatures, where many of these bills have emerged. Second, Ziegler believes that the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement is embracing \u8220\'20\'1Ca conviction that it matters less what voters think and more what judges think\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you don\u8217\'20\'19t worry about what you\u8217\'20\'19re doing being unpopular, and you believe that ideological consistency, or even logical consistency, is paramount, you would be more inclined to go with an abolitionist argument, and you might worry less about that having political fallout in a politically uncompetitive state,\u8221\'20\'1D Ziegler said. \u8220\'20\'1CYou don\u8217\'20\'19t need to convince most Americans you\u8217\'20\'19re right. You only need to convince five justices on the supreme court.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 With \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans proving deeply unpopular in the US, circumventing popular will has become a necessity for an increasingly hardline movement.\par \par \uc2 And by relying on the courts rather than voters, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement can afford to ignore voters\u8217\'20\'19 misgivings.\par \par \uc2 The movement has abandoned its earlier goal of amending the US constitution to recognize fetal personhood; instead, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement is now seeking to convince courts that the 14th amendment, which guarantees the right of due process and equal protection, already recognizes embryos and fetuses as people from the moment of conception. It is, according to Ziegler, yet another sign of the movement\u8217\'20\'19s drift away from democratic norms.\par \par \uc2 This interest in the 14th amendment is also something of an about-face for the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, which is predominantly white and has had a convoluted relationship with race for more than a century. At the time that the amendment passed, as part of a post-civil war effort to recognize Black people\u8217\'20\'19s rights, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaders \u8220\'20\'1Cdidn\u8217\'20\'19t want to talk about race because they weren\u8217\'20\'19t sure that people of color were persons with rights\u8221\'20\'1D, Ziegler said. Over time, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement began using the language of civil rights and affirmative action \u8211\'20\'13 but primarily to argue that fetuses are the greatest victim of discrimination.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think that\u8217\'20\'19s one of the reasons fetal personhood language has been resonant for so long,\u8221\'20\'1D Ziegler said of the movement\u8217\'20\'19s co-option of civil rights rhetoric. \u8220\'20\'1CIt gives people a way to talk about equality and a way to elevate somebody as a victim of discrimination in a way that makes sense to a largely white group of conservatives.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As Roe recedes further into the rearview mirror, Ziegler expects the movement to become even more committed to some of the most unpopular implications of fetal personhood, such as restrictions on IVF. Although Republican politicians have shied away from limiting access to the treatment, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement has grown more strident on the topic. Some of its leaders even condemned Donald Trump for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/19/trump-ivf-executive-order"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 his recent executive order supporting IVF}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement is going to be still full steam ahead,\u8221\'20\'1D Ziegler said. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s an increasing sentiment in the movement that this is not about pleasing Republicans. This is not about pleasing voters. This is about the importance of this, ideologically, to the people involved.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250423el4n001jl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
