\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We are failing\u8217\'20\'19: doctors and students in the US look to Mexico for basic  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  training\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman with photographs by Mah\u233\'00\'E9 Elipe \par \uc2 1,426 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 April 2025\par 17:29 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Since Roe was overturned, a growing number of would-be \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers have begun to leave the country in search of an education as training in the US dwindles\par \par \uc2 On paper, it should not be difficult for Dr Ramos to learn to perform abortions. As a family medicine doctor, Ramos works in a specialty that frequently provides the procedure. He lives in a deep-blue state where it is still allowed. And the administrators running Ramos\u8217\'20\'19s residency program \u8211\'20\'13 a kind of apprenticeship that US doctors must undergo to become full-fledged physicians \u8211\'20\'13 support Ramos\u8217\'20\'19s desire to learn how to do it.\par \par \uc2 But over the course of his three-year-long residency, Ramos is guaranteed just three days\u8217\'20\'19 worth of training at \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 . Residents get to participate in only a handful of abortions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s just not enough if you want to practice \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ramos, who asked to be identified only by his last name to protect his privacy. \u8220\'20\'1CI knew that if I wanted to do this, I needed more experience.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s why, earlier this month, Ramos traveled to a clinic in Mexico City for two weeks\u8217\'20\'19 worth of training in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provision. During his first week at the clinic, which is run by the global organization \uc2 MSI Reproductive Choices\uc2  and its Mexican arm Fundaci\u243\'00\'F3n MSI, Ramos performed roughly 60 abortions.\par \par \uc2 In the years since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade, paving the way for more than a dozen states to ban virtually all abortions, a small but growing number of would-be \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers have begun to leave the country in search of an education. In 2023, MSI trained nine American doctors to perform abortions at clinics in Mexico. In 2024, it trained 27. So far this year, it is on track to double that number.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COn one hand, it\u8217\'20\'19s a tremendous relief to know that medical students and residents aren\u8217\'20\'19t going to have to forego this very important part of their training in their education,\u8221\'20\'1D said Pamela Merritt, executive director of Medical Students for Choice. Last year, Merritt\u8217\'20\'19s organization helped eight medical students and residents receive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training in Mexico and the UK.\par \par \uc2 Merritt continued: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s also incredibly sad that in the United States, we are failing to train people even to the standard of care indicated by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Every \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in the US permits abortions to save a patient\u8217\'20\'19s life. But without adequate training, doctors may not be skilled enough to perform abortions even in those dire circumstances.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18It\u8217\'20\'19s a shame\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Medical schools and residency programs are run by massive hospitals that are heavily dependent on public funding; such institutions tend to be, by nature, leery of anything as controversial as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \uc2 Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education\uc2  (\uc2 ACGME\uc2 ) has required OB-GYN residencies to teach doctors how to perform abortions since the 1990s, but rather than offer training in-house, hospitals have often farmed their residents out to freestanding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics for training.\par \par \uc2 Even before Roe fell, this system was faulty: a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ajog.org/article/S0002-9378(18)30292-8/fulltext"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2019 study found}}}\uc2  that, despite the \uc2 ACGME\uc2  requirement, just 64% of OB-GYN residency programs offered \u8220\'20\'1Croutine training with dedicated time\u8221\'20\'1D for abortions. Family medicine residents who want to learn to perform abortions face a greater disadvantage, since the \uc2 ACGME\uc2  does not require their residency programs to offer any kind of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training.\par \par \uc2 Even most OB-GYN residents, program directors reported in the 2019 study, did not achieve what doctors call \u8220\'20\'1Ccompetency\u8221\'20\'1D when it came to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Without competency \u8211\'20\'13 a qualification that\u8217\'20\'19s measured through a melange of doctors\u8217\'20\'19 knowledge, skills and attitudes \u8211\'20\'13 doctors may not be able to safely perform abortions on their own.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training and competency is now even harder to come by. Since Roe\u8217\'20\'19s collapse, more than 100 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics have closed. Those that are left are often besieged by patients fleeing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, leaving them without the time and space to teach everybody who wants to learn.\par \par \uc2 If an OB-GYN residency program is located in a state that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 ACGME\uc2  rules currently dictate that the residency \u8220\'20\'1Cmust provide access to this clinical experience in a different jurisdiction where it is lawful\u8221\'20\'1D. The \uc2 ACGME\uc2  declined to respond to a request for information about how many residency programs are currently compliant with its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -training requirement, although records show that no OB-GYN programs have lost their accreditation status in the last year.\par \par \uc2 Patricia Lohr serves as the director of research and innovation for the \uc2 British Pregnancy Advisory Service\uc2  (Bpas), a UK non-profit that provides abortions up until about 24 weeks of pregnancy. Lohr trained to become an OB-GYN in the US. \u8220\'20\'1CHaving been a resident and a medical student in the United States, I could really see the importance of having access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  education that wasn\u8217\'20\'19t entirely reliant on what was being delivered within academic training programs,\u8221\'20\'1D Lohr said. \u8220\'20\'1CBecause often, abortions weren\u8217\'20\'19t being provided in those academic hospitals.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 When Lohr moved to the UK, she quickly moved to create a two-week training program at Bpas where medical students could learn about abortions and observe \u8211\'20\'13 but not perform \u8211\'20\'13 the procedure. In the years since Roe fell, that training program has received a surge of applications from American medical students and residents.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a shame that people would have to travel to learn a basic part of women\u8217\'20\'19s health care,\u8221\'20\'1D Lohr said. \u8220\'20\'1CThere are many trainees out there at the moment who would like to obtain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  skills, but cannot get it locally, and so they get diverted into doing something else.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Lauren Wiener, a New Jersey medical student, had originally planned to travel to Arizona in summer 2022 to learn how to provide abortions. But when Roe\u8217\'20\'19s fall led Arizona \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to temporarily stop working, Wiener had to cancel her trip. Instead, she ended up undergoing a week-long training at Bpas last fall.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is something that you need to know how to do, because there are emergency situations,\u8221\'20\'1D Wiener said of abortions. \u8220\'20\'1CYou might not want to electively perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 24 weeks, but if someone comes in and they\u8217\'20\'19re miscarrying, you need to know how to evacuate that uterus. It\u8217\'20\'19s a skill you need to have to save a life.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We will be there\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 While training in the US dwindles, the country\u8217\'20\'19s increasingly conservative approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also put it at odds with much of the rest of the world. Only four countries \u8211\'20\'13 including the US \u8211\'20\'13 have tightened their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws over the last 30 years, while more than 60 countries and territories have loosened theirs, according to a tally by the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mexico is one of them. In 2023, its supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/sep/08/mexico-abortion-ruling-americans-border"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decriminalized \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  nationwide}}}\uc2  ; the procedure is now available in about half of all Mexican states. And providers aren\u8217\'20\'19t the only people taking advantage of Mexico\u8217\'20\'19s liberalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws: last year, Fundaci\u243\'00\'F3n MSI provided first-trimester abortions to 62 women from the United States.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTraining, training, training \u8211\'20\'13 it is key, to have less danger for actual patients,\u8221\'20\'1D said Araceli L\u243\'00\'F3pez-Nava, managing director of MSI Latin America. \u8220\'20\'1CWe understand how difficult the situation is becoming in the US, so we\u8217\'20\'19re happy to help.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The organization has the capacity to train up to 300 doctors a year to perform abortions, L\u243\'00\'F3pez-Nava estimated.\par \par \uc2 MSI is not, however, a solution for everybody. Would-be trainees need to speak Spanish. And although the organization has in past years trained medical students, MSI\u8217\'20\'19s Mexico clinics have started focusing on teaching residents who have already performed 20 abortions. Because residents have already chosen their specialties and secured berths in residency programs \u8211\'20\'13 which can be highly competitive \u8211\'20\'13 they are more likely to become \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers.\par \par \uc2 Training in Mexico can also be pricey, especially since the program does not pay for travel and lodging. Ramos\u8217\'20\'19s entire trip cost about $5,000, although a scholarship helped him cover most of the costs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a way, at least for me, to be exposed to a different medical system, learn from different providers from a different country, exchange knowledge,\u8221\'20\'1D Ramos said. \u8220\'20\'1CI feel like I\u8217\'20\'19m being adequately prepared to meet the needs of my patients in the US.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article was amended on 10 April 2025 to remove some personal details.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250409el49001s1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
