\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The Christian right has set the US on the road to \uc2 Gilead\uc2 . Without a fight, other nations may follow\b0\par\par\uc2 Deborah Frances-White \par \uc2 1,332 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 April 2025\par 11:58 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Organisations that pumped money into overturning Roe v Wade are making inroads in Europe. Women\u8217\'20\'19s rights are truly at risk\par \par \uc2 With \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as president, there is now a heavy strain of Christian nationalism driving the US political agenda. From draconian \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies to ending birthright citizenship, some of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s first executive orders sound startlingly like something out of Margaret Atwood\u8217\'20\'19s The Handmaid\u8217\'20\'19s Tale, the dystopian novel turned TV show set in \uc2 Gilead\uc2 , a fundamentalist, fascist version of the US where women have no rights. But it is urgent we understand that what is happening in the US could happen here. This road to Atwood\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Gilead\uc2  is charting a course straight through the UK and Europe, and we may well be sleepwalking on to it.\par \par \uc2 In November 2024 I debated with the American conservative lawyer {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.regent.edu/faculty/erin-morrow-hawley/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Erin Hawley}}}\uc2  at the Oxford Union. The motion was \u8220\'20\'1CThis house regrets the overturning of Roe v Wade\u8221\'20\'1D, the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 landmark decision}}}\uc2  that once protected the right to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the federal level. Hawley is vice-president of the \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2  (\uc2 ADF\uc2 ), an \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jun/30/christian-hate-group-funding-us-anti-lgbtq-anti-abortion-organizations"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 anti-LGBTQ+ hate group}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D, according to the \uc2 Southern Poverty Law Centre\uc2 , founded by the US Christian right. She is also a high profile lawyer and supported the state of Mississippi on the Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization case that overturned Roe.\par \par \uc2 During the debate, Hawley argued that Roe was a \u8220\'20\'1Cusurpation of democratic process\u8221\'20\'1D made by an all-male supreme court. She mentioned the constitution a lot but, curiously, I didn\u8217\'20\'19t hear her mention God. I was surprised because I had just spent the afternoon listening to the podcast This is Living, which she hosts with her husband, the senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 , and on which she describes her decision to fight Roe as \u8220\'20\'1Ca calling from God\u8221\'20\'1D, putting her success down to \u8220\'20\'1Cthe prayers of believers\u8221\'20\'1D. On one episode, the Hawleys suggest that where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now illegal their listeners \u8220\'20\'1Cadopt a mom who is unexpectedly expecting\u8221\'20\'1D to help her \u8211\'20\'13 and \u8220\'20\'1Cmost importantly, introduce her to Jesus\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The overt religious agenda of Hawley\u8217\'20\'19s podcast is completely at odds with the legalese she used in court and in the debating chamber. But the \uc2 ADF\uc2  itself is more explicit. The CEO\u8217\'20\'19s statement on its website reads: \u8220\'20\'1CWe will do \u8211\'20\'13 as we have done \u8211\'20\'13 the hard things to which God has called us with the expectation that He will accomplish His purposes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This kind of language is no longer solely the preserve of organisations of the Christian right \u8211\'20\'13 it has infiltrated the highest offices. The White House\u8217\'20\'19s new \u8220\'20\'1Cfaith office\u8221\'20\'1D webpage proudly declares: \u8220\'20\'1CIn his first week, President Trump pardoned Christians and pro-life activists who were persecuted by the weaponized Biden Administration for praying and peacefully living out their faith.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The tragic reality is that the \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-life\u8221\'20\'1D Dobbs ruling is killing people. In states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal unless the life of the woman or other pregnant person is at stake, patients are suffering and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/nevaeh-crain-death-texas-abortion-ban-emtala"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 even dying}}}\uc2 . Doctors are frightened to give them care for fear of being struck off and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953624003563?via%3DihubAs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 even sent to jail}}}\uc2 . In South Carolina, legislators are trying to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.salon.com/2024/12/12/south-carolina-legislators-try-again-on-bill-that-would-classify-abortion-as-homicide/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pass a bill}}}\uc2  that would classify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as homicide, punishable by the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the \uc2 National Science Foundation\uc2  (NSF) has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/science/2025/02/04/national-science-foundation-trump-executive-orders-words/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reportedly issued}}}\uc2  a list of keywords that trigger staff to review science research projects to determine if they violate Trump-issued executive orders. The words \u8220\'20\'1Cfemale\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cwoman\u8221\'20\'1D are on that list, but \u8220\'20\'1Cmale\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cman\u8221\'20\'1D are not.\par \par \uc2 But this isn\u8217\'20\'19t exclusively a US problem \u8211\'20\'13 women\u8217\'20\'19s rights are increasingly at risk in the UK and Europe, too. Last year, while doing research for my book Six Conversations We\u8217\'20\'19re Scared to Have, I discovered that from 1861 to November 2022 only three women had ever been prosecuted for terminating a pregnancy in the UK. But in the past two years alone, at least six women have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/jan/22/illegal-abortions-prosecutions-uk-police-royal-college"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 appeared in court}}}\uc2  and dozens more have been investigated.\par \par \uc2 It is a criminal offence to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 24 weeks in the UK or without approval from two doctors, carrying a maximum sentence of life imprisonment. While it is true that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs mifepristone and misoprostol became available to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/apr/21/pills-in-the-post-how-covid-reopened-the-abortion-wars"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 use at home}}}\uc2  for women before 10 weeks of pregnancy during the pandemic, this change in procedure does not sufficiently explain such a radical increase in prosecutions.\par \par \uc2 Dr Jonathan Lord, a co-chair of the \uc2 Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists\uc2  (\uc2 RCOG\uc2 ) \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  taskforce, has warned that when an unexplained pregnancy loss is suspected of being an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , police in some areas are seizing women\u8217\'20\'19s phones to search for period tracking apps and their internet history. There have also been reports that police are drug-testing distressed women who have suffered late-stage pregnancy loss and subjecting them to lengthy and distressing investigations.\par \par \uc2 Between 2008 and 2017, about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.opendemocracy.net/en/5050/revealed-trump-linked-us-christian-fundamentalists-pour-millions-of-dark-money-into-europe-boosting-the-far-right/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $50m of funding}}}\uc2  flowed into Europe from 12 of the most influential Christian rightwing foundations in the US, according to research by OpenDemocracy in 2019. Neil Datta, the executive director of the European Parliamentary Forum for Sexual and \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , told me there has been a creeping expansion of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Christian nationalist groups in the UK since 2013. When same-sex marriage was legalised in the UK and France, these groups went into overdrive and were triggered to hire lawyers and political scientists to learn how to draft laws and bills to fight progressive movements.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey style themselves as NGOs, thinktanks and, in some cases, political parties,\u8221\'20\'1D Datta told me. \u8220\'20\'1CBut when you scratch the surface, you see religious connections in almost all of them.\u8221\'20\'1D Many of these groups originated in the US Christian right, but have now opened offices in Europe, including the \uc2 ADF\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s international subsidiary, operating since 2010, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://corporateeurope.org/en/2024/05/alliance-attacking-freedom#:~:text=Today%20the%20Alliance%20Defending%20Freedom%20has%205%20offices%20in%20Europe%2C%20most%20of%20which%20are%20placed%20close%20to%20international%20institutions%20they%20lobby."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 now has five}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 including in Brussels, Strasbourg and London. Just this week, an activist in the UK {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/apr/04/abortion-campaigner-livia-tossici-bolt-buffer-zone-clinic"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was convicted}}}\uc2  of breaching a buffer zone outside an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic. Her case was funded by the UK branch of the \uc2 ADF\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It doesn\u8217\'20\'19t feel like much of a stretch to suggest that their dream seems to be reflected in the growing popularity of the tradwives of TikTok: white women in gingham baking pies for their six children, bragging about financial dependence and recommending raw milk for fertility. Coincidentally, if you watch enough tradwife content, as I did while researching my book, you start to see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/ng-interactive/2024/jul/24/tradwives-tiktok-women-gender-roles#:~:text=After%20I%20watched,within%20an%20afternoon%E2%80%9D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 advertisements for the ADF}}}\uc2 . The road to \uc2 Gilead\uc2  is circular \u8211\'20\'13 once you\u8217\'20\'19re on it, it\u8217\'20\'19s hard to get off.\par \par \uc2 In late 2024, Nigel Farage was reported to be linking up with the \uc2 ADF\uc2 , calling for a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abortionrights.org.uk/press-release-no-rollbacks-abortion-rights-chair-slams-farages-call-to-cut-time-limits/#:~:text=%E2%80%98Farage%20is%20linking%20up%20with%20the%20anti%2Dabortion%20group%20Alliance%20Defending%20Freedom%20(ADF).%20Their%20rhetoric%20is%20straight%20out%20of%20the%20anti%2Dchoice%20US%20playbook."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 parliamentary debate}}}\uc2  to cut the time limit for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the UK. Elon Musk recently responded to a suggestion from Andrew Tate that he would run to be British prime minister by saying, \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/news/2025/feb/27/andrew-tate-tristan-romania-us#:~:text=Elon%20Musk%20responded%20to%20a%20suggestion%20from%20Andrew%20Tate%20that%20he%20would%20%E2%80%9Crun%20for%20prime%20minister%20of%20the%20UK%E2%80%9D%20by%20saying%20%E2%80%9Che%E2%80%99s%20not%20wrong%E2%80%9D%2C%20while%20one%20of%20Tate%E2%80%99s%20lawyers%2C%20Paul%20Ingrassia%2C%20is%20the%20White%20House%20liaison%20official%20for%20the%20US%20Department%20of%20Justice."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 He\u8217\'20\'19s not wrong}}}\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D Last year I would have laughed at the very idea that we could be under his eye. This year, I\u8217\'20\'19m not so sure.\par \par \uc2 It is time to fight for what we have. France made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a constitutional right in 2024. In January, more than 30 groups in the UK, including the \uc2 British Medical Association\uc2  and the Faculty of Public Health, representing 800,000 health care professionals, demanded \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.rcog.org.uk/news/leading-authorities-call-for-urgent-action-by-parliament-to-protect-women-s-essential-reproductive-rights/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 urgent action}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D to safeguard \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If we don\u8217\'20\'19t take action now, we could be sleepwalking into a future where neither Erin Hawley nor I may have the luxury of debating in public again. In the meantime, Margaret Atwood {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vanityfair.com/hollywood/2017/05/handmaids-tale-nolite-te-bastardes-carborundorum-origin-margaret-atwood?srsltid=AfmBOoonxbcKp6nN-ZgHLAmbuIeHk2DrEqKFHmEN-i1oQ_ojeKJOEjCn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 put it best}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rfgez4nC2PQ"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nolite te bastardes carborundorum}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 don\u8217\'20\'19t let the bastards grind you down.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Deborah Frances-White is a bestselling author and the host of the Guilty Feminist podcast. Her new book, Six Conversations We\u8217\'20\'19re Scared to Have, is out now.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250405el45000gq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
