\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Supreme court weighs South Carolina effort to defund \uc2 Planned Parenthood\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,095 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 April 2025\par 20:32 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Court hears arguments on whether state can block clinics that provide abortions from receiving Medicaid funds\par \par \uc2 In its first \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related case since {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  retook control of the White House, the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  on Wednesday heard oral arguments in a case challenging South Carolina\u8217\'20\'19s attempt to effectively \u8220\'20\'1Cdefund\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  because the \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  giant performs abortions.\par \par \uc2 The case, Medina v Planned Parenthood South Atlantic, deals with a 2018 executive order from the South Carolina governor, \uc2 Henry McMaster\uc2  that blocked clinics that provide abortions from receiving reimbursements via Medicaid, the \uc2 US government\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s healthcare program for low-income people, despite the fact that those reimbursements don\u8217\'20\'19t actually cover abortions. \u8220\'20\'1CPayment of taxpayer funds to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, for any purpose, results in the subsidy of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the denial of the right to life,\u8221\'20\'1D McMaster said at the time.\par \par \uc2 However, federal law bans Medicaid from covering the vast majority of abortions, which only comprised about 4% of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s activities in fiscal year 2022. Instead, people use Medicaid to cover \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s other services. In the same year, the organization performed nearly half a million Pap tests and breast exams as well as 4.6m STI tests and treatments. It also provided birth control services to more than 2.2 million people.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood South Atlantic, a \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  affiliate that operates two clinics in South Carolina, teamed up with a patient who sought birth control, Julie Edwards, to sue over McMaster\u8217\'20\'19s order. Lower courts have since kept the order from going into effect.\par \par \uc2 The supreme court is currently controlled 6-3 by conservatives. If the court sides with South Carolina, it could pave the way for other states to exclude \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  from their Medicaid programs as well as devastate South Carolinians\u8217\'20\'19 ability to access reproductive healthcare and family planning services.\par \par \uc2 Of the 2.4 million people treated at \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  nationwide each year, nearly half are on Medicaid. About four in 10 women who have sought care at family-planning clinics {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whijournal.com/article/S1049-3867(12)00073-4/fulltext"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 say those clinics}}}\uc2  are their only recent source of healthcare.\par \par \uc2 At the heart of the case is a federal provision that guarantees that people insured by Medicaid can freely choose their own providers as long as they accept the program and are qualified to provide care. The case hinges on a technical argument: South Carolina \u8211\'20\'13 which is being represented by the powerhouse Christian legal group \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2  (\uc2 ADF\uc2 ) \u8211\'20\'13 says Medicaid beneficiaries have no right to sue if they believe that guarantee has been violated.\par \par \uc2 Oral arguments zeroed in on this technicality, as \uc2 ADF\uc2  senior counsel \uc2 John Bursch\uc2  argued that the guarantee lacks \u8220\'20\'1Cclear rights-creating language\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt does not use the word \u8216\'20\'18right\u8217\'20\'19 or its functional equivalent,\u8221\'20\'1D Bursch said. \u8220\'20\'1CNor does it use word with a deeply rooted, rights-creating pedigree, like the fifth amendment\u8217\'20\'19s \u8216\'20\'18no person shall\u8217\'20\'19.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The three liberal justices on the supreme court \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2 , \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  and \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 appeared ready to reject that argument. It\u8217\'20\'19s impossible to describe Medicaid\u8217\'20\'19s guarantee, Kagan said, without calling it a \u8220\'20\'1Cright\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe obligation is to ensure that individuals can choose their doctor,\u8221\'20\'1D Kagan said. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a correlative right. There\u8217\'20\'19s an obligation, there\u8217\'20\'19s a right. And the right is the right to choose their doctor.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The conservative wing of the court seemed more split. Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  appeared particularly worried about leaving patients unable to seek help if their preferred qualified provider was kicked out of Medicaid. \u8220\'20\'1CIf I want to go see Dr Jones and that\u8217\'20\'19s the provider of my choice and the state has disqualified Dr Jones \u8230\'20\'26 there\u8217\'20\'19s no mechanism, am I right, for the beneficiary to say: \u8216\'20\'18Well, you\u8217\'20\'19re depriving me of my ability\u8217\'20\'19 \u8211\'20\'13 we won\u8217\'20\'19t call it right, we won\u8217\'20\'19t use the loaded word \u8211\'20\'13 \u8216\'20\'18but the ability to see the provider of my choice\u8217\'20\'19?\u8221\'20\'1D she asked.\par \par \uc2 During arguments, Bursch suggested that, rather than suing, patients could ask the government to intervene administratively.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, these seemingly narrow arguments, legal experts warn, conceal the broader consequences of the case. If people can\u8217\'20\'19t sue when they believe a state is violating Medicaid, then it will become far more difficult to keep states from discriminating against certain kinds of care, said Nicole Huberfeld, a health law professor at Boston University\u8217\'20\'19s School of Public Health.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEven though the state is trying to claim that it has sole authority to decide who\u8217\'20\'19s a qualified provider, this isn\u8217\'20\'19t really about whether \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  is a qualified provider. It\u8217\'20\'19s about a political calculation on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D Huberfeld said. \u8220\'20\'1CReally, what\u8217\'20\'19s happening here is states making politically driven decisions about access to medical care.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A decision in the case is expected in June.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA medical home\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 More than 1 million people in South Carolina use Medicaid and the Children\u8217\'20\'19s Health Insurance Program (Chip) \u8211\'20\'13 which is closely linked to Medicaid \u8211\'20\'13 for their insurance. Almost 40% of South Carolina counties are believed to be \u8220\'20\'1Ccontraceptive deserts\u8221\'20\'1D, where there are not enough providers to meet the needs of the people who live there. South Carolina bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  past six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Because Texas terminated \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  from its state Medicaid program in 2021, it offers a window into what happens when people lose access to medical provider. The termination {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2021/03/10/planned-parenthood-medicaid-texas/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 affected}}}\uc2  the healthcare of some 8,000 Texans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPlanned Parenthood, we heard again and again, was a medical home for people with very few options for providers,\u8221\'20\'1D said Anna Chatillon, a research scientist at Resound Research for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2 , who led a study that interviewed Texans about how the termination affected their lives. \u8220\'20\'1CIn several different interviews, we heard people talking about showing up for an appointment, finding out that they couldn\u8217\'20\'19t use Medicaid any longer, not having any idea where else to go, and experiencing real disruptions to their care based on that very quickly, but also serious emotional ramifications.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The case is part of a broader, longstanding anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign against \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , which includes an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/oct/22/maga-courts-planned-parenthood-case"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ongoing lawsuit}}}\uc2  that could bankrupt the organization over allegations that it defrauded Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  announced that the Trump administration \u8211\'20\'13 which helped South Carolina make its case in oral arguments \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/mar/31/trump-planned-parenthood"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 had notified nine of its affiliates}}}\uc2  that they would lose funding from Title X, the nation\u8217\'20\'19s oldest and largest family-planning program. Politico {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2025/03/31/trump-admin-cuts-tens-of-millions-from-planned-parenthood-00261763"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  the funds were being withheld as punishment for the organization\u8217\'20\'19s diversity, equity and inclusion efforts.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250402el42001p5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
