\par\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Kemi Badenoch\uc2  gives credence to race-swap conspiracy theory about Adolescence but admits she hasn\u8217\'20\'19t watched it \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Andrew Sparrow \par \uc2 9,455 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 April 2025\par 08:36 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Conservative party leader says she believes show \u8216\'20\'18is based on real story\u8217\'20\'19, a claim described as wholly untrue by writer and co-creator of series\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 Afternoon summary\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  has rejected claims that the expected imposition of US tariffs on Britain means he has been \u8220\'20\'1Cplayed\u8221\'20\'1D by President Trump. Starmer said that the UK and the US were at an \u8220\'20\'1Cadvanced stage\u8221\'20\'1D in negotiating an economic deal, and he suggested that this could lead to the UK getting exemptions from US tariffs in future. (See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/apr/01/uk-politics-labour-conservatives-trump-tariffs-trade-deal-keir-starmer-latest-news?page=with%3Ablock-67eba8098f08bb7e41c3727b#block-67eba8098f08bb7e41c3727b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 9.53am}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/apr/01/uk-politics-labour-conservatives-trump-tariffs-trade-deal-keir-starmer-latest-news?page=with%3Ablock-67ebd7b38f08a46a40e92e95#block-67ebd7b38f08a46a40e92e95"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1.15pm}}}\uc2 .) According to Steven Swinford from the Times, a deal is ready to sign. \u8220\'20\'1CBut US is refusing to sign it until after hitting UK with tariffs on April 2, aka Liberation Day,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/Steven_Swinford/status/1906975138127425854"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Swinford says}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Richard Hughes, the chair of the \uc2 Office of Budget Responsibility\uc2 , has told MPs that the \uc2 OBR\uc2  is likely to downgrade its growth forecast in the autumn. As {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/business/2025/04/01/ftse-100-markets-latest-trump-tariffs-liberation-day-gold/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Telegraph reports}}}\uc2 , he said the \uc2 OBR\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s most recent report, which downgraded the UK growth forecast for 2025 from 2% to 1%, was based on \uc2 IMF\uc2  projections from January, before Trump\u8217\'20\'19s policies kicked in. Hughes told the Treasury committee:\par \par \uc2 Putting in place more restrictions on global trade around the world is not very good for global output, and it\u8217\'20\'19s pretty reasonable to say the direction of that is going to be down.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2025/apr/01/uk-business-secretary-denies-free-speech-issue-featured-in-us-tariff-talks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jonathan Reynolds, the business secretary, has denied that the issue of free speech has featured in tariff negotiations with the US after reports a deal could be jeopardised by the outcome of a criminal case in Dorset.}}}\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2025/apr/01/lawyers-justice-secretary-shabana-mahmood-sentencing-council-guidelines"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Shabana Mahmood\u8217\'20\'19s intervention to halt new guidelines on sentencing is \u8220\'20\'1Cdangerous\u8221\'20\'1D and a \u8220\'20\'1Cdeliberate step backwards\u8221\'20\'1D, according to senior legal figures and prison campaigners.}}}\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Kemi Badenoch\uc2  has given credence to a conspiracy theory about the \uc2 Netflix\uc2  hit Adolescence being based on a real story, but involving a black boy not a white boy \u8211\'20\'13 despite also admitting she has not seen the show. (See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/apr/01/uk-politics-labour-conservatives-trump-tariffs-trade-deal-keir-starmer-latest-news?page=with%3Ablock-67ebf5008f08a46a40e93037#block-67ebf5008f08a46a40e93037"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 3.59am}}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 Lawyers attack \u8216\'20\'18dangerous\u8217\'20\'19 decision to halt Sentencing Council guidelines\par \par \uc2 Shabana Mahmood\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s intervention to halt new guidelines on sentencing is \u8220\'20\'1Cdangerous\u8221\'20\'1D and a \u8220\'20\'1Cdeliberate step backwards\u8221\'20\'1D, according to senior legal figures and prison campaigners, Rajeev Syal reports.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2025/apr/01/lawyers-justice-secretary-shabana-mahmood-sentencing-council-guidelines"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Lawyers attack \u8216\'20\'18dangerous\u8217\'20\'19 decision to halt Sentencing Council guidelines}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.23pm BST\par \par \uc2 Andy Burnham\uc2  calls for jobcentres to be renamed Live Well centres\par \par \uc2 This morning the Commons work and pensions committee held a session in Manchester town hall, where it took evidence from witnesses in relation to jobcentres and pensioner poverty.\par \par \uc2 Andy Burnham\uc2 , the Greater Manchester mayor, gave evidence, and, as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mirror.co.uk/news/politics/andy-burnham-says-dwp-jobcentres-34973606"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Daily Mirror reports}}}\uc2 , he argued that jobcentres should be renamed live well centres, in line with Greater Manchester\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.greatermanchester-ca.gov.uk/news/10m-funding-boost-for-greater-manchester-s-live-well-plan-to-tackle-inequalities-improve-health-and-help-people-back-into-work/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Live Well initiative}}}\uc2  to promote wellbeing that links healthcare with employment support.\par \par \uc2 As Ashley Cowburn reports, Burnham said:\par \par \uc2 I would like \uc2 Jobcentre Plus\uc2  to be renamed Live Well centres. In the name it should be positive - it should say to the public \u8216\'20\'18you are going to be helped when you come in here.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not just the name. It\u8217\'20\'19s what happens when you go inside. What\u8217\'20\'19s the atmosphere like? What\u8217\'20\'19s it like to be in there? I just think there\u8217\'20\'19s a huge opportunity.\par \par \uc2 As Steve Robson reports {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://inews.co.uk/news/politics/andy-burnham-benefit-cut-wont-work-3616765?srsltid=AfmBOoolPB0Wn_l7fc1eyud0eCukzvzS-idtFv1QsRWScDWPtjPdC-XL"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in the i}}}\uc2 , Burnham also also said that Westminster governments put too much emphasis on punitive sanctions when reforming welfare. He said:\par \par \uc2 From my time in Westminster, and I was probably guilty of it at the time, I think benefits policy in this country has too much, under all governments, been written to create headlines to please certain newspapers and not actually to do the job of encouraging the recovery of people back to a better position in their lives.\par \par \uc2 I think once you leave as a minister and become a mayor, you just don\u8217\'20\'19t think \u8216\'20\'18will this please that paper? It\u8217\'20\'19s a completely different, bottom-up offer that you give to people\par \par \uc2 If you really want to help people, if you really want to save money and get more people into work, I think you have to come at it a completely different way.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.41pm BST\par \par \uc2 Green party say it is 'sickening' Labour is boasting about deportation flights on campaign leaflets\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, in his speech to the Organised Immigration Crime conference, \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  announced that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/mar/31/keir-starmer-immigration-yvette-cooper-labour-uk-politics-latest-live-news?page=with%3Ablock-67ea50a88f08167d07b35e47#block-67ea50a88f08167d07b35e47"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 24,000 migrants have been removed from the UK }}}\uc2  since Labour took office. This included the four biggest return flights ever, he said.\par \par \uc2 If you are a Labour activists, you can now {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://shop.labour.org.uk/product/illegal-migration-leaflet"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 buy leaflets}}}\uc2  from party HQ making the same point.\par \par \uc2 The Green party says it is horrified. \uc2 Carla Denyer\uc2 , the Green\u8217\'20\'19s co-leader, said:\par \par \uc2 Labour\u8217\'20\'19s recent leaflet boasting about deporting more people than the Tories is sickening.\par \par \uc2 Labour are failing to provide safe and legal routes into the UK while seemingly revelling in turning people\u8217\'20\'19s lives upside down.\par \par \uc2 Any claim the Labour party may have once had to be a party of compassion or principle has well and truly gone.\par \par \uc2 The controversy is reminiscent of what happened in 2015, when the party produced {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newstatesman.com/politics/2015/03/labours-anti-immigrant-mug-worst-part-it-isnt-gaffe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 official mugs }}}\uc2  with the slogan \u8220\'20\'1CControls on immigration\u8221\'20\'1D. This was one of the party\u8217\'20\'19s five election pledges, and so it was understandable that the party wanted to publicise it. But the mugs were very unpopular with some Labour activists who felt they sounded rightwing.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.42pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.21pm BST\par \par \uc2 Chagos Islands deal now being finalised with Mauritius, No 10 says, after Trump gives it approval\par \par \uc2 A deal to hand the Chagos Islands to Mauritius is being \u8220\'20\'1Cfinalised\u8221\'20\'1D after winning approval from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s US, Downing Street has said. \uc2 PA Media\uc2  says:\par \par \uc2 The plan will see the UK give up sovereignty over the British Indian Ocean Territory but pay to lease back the strategically important Diego Garcia military base, which is used by the US.\par \par \uc2 Discussions are ongoing between the UK and the Mauritian government over the terms of the deal.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe finalisation of the deal is ongoing,\u8221\'20\'1D the PM\u8217\'20\'19s spokesperson said.\par \par \uc2 US President Trump indicated his backing for the deal during \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s visit to Washington in February, saying: \u8220\'20\'1CI have a feeling it\u8217\'20\'19s going to work out very well\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The PM\u8217\'20\'19s spokesperson said: \u8220\'20\'1CYou will have seen from the president that he recognised the strength of the deal.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think we are now working with the Mauritian government to finalise the deal and sign the treaty.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy understanding is it\u8217\'20\'19s now between us and the Mauritian government to finalise the deal, following the discussions with the US.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Shadow foreign secretary \uc2 Priti Patel\uc2  said: \u8220\'20\'1CLabour should have the courage to come to the House of Commons and face scrutiny over their deal to surrender the Chagos Islands and pay for the privilege.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis remains a very bad deal for British taxpayers \u8211\'20\'13 and we now know money is going to be frontloaded to Mauritius, at a time when Labour is stripping vulnerable pensioners of their winter fuel payments and whacking family farms and businesses with punishing taxes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 Badenoch gives credence to race-swap conspiracy theory about Adolescence (which she hasn't watched)\par \par \uc2 Kemi Badenoch\uc2  has given credence to a conspiracy theory about the \uc2 Netflix\uc2  hit Adolescence being based on a real story, but involving a black boy not a white boy.\par \par \uc2 This has been described as wholly untrue by Jack Thorne, the writer and co-creator of the series, which has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gov.uk/government/news/downing-street-opens-doors-to-adolescence-creators-for-vital-discussion-on-protecting-our-children"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  praised by Keir Starmer}}}\uc2  for the way it has opened up a debate about the radicalisation of young men.\par \par \uc2 Starmer met Thorne, and some of the shows other creators, in Downing Street this week, along with charities engaged in child protection, and the PM said he would like as many pupils as possible to watch the series.\par \par \uc2 The four-part drama is about a 13-year-old boy arrested for the murder of a girl at his school. \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s reviewer {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2025/mar/13/adolescence-review-the-closest-thing-to-tv-perfection-in-decades"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  described it}}}\uc2  as \u8220\'20\'1Cthe most devastating and immaculately scripted and played series I have ever seen \u8211\'20\'13 as close to televisual perfection as you can get\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with GB News, asked if she has seen it, Badenoch replied:\par \par \uc2 Well, I think Adolescence is a fictional story. It\u8217\'20\'19s based on a real story, but my understanding is that the boy who committed that crime was not white.\par \par \uc2 So, people can do whatever they like in fiction. The prime minister should not be building policy on fiction. He should be building policy on reality. What is the reality? Phones are disrupting schools and not enough schools have effective bans.\par \par \uc2 Badenoch went on to talk about the Tory policy to ban mobile phones in schools. In an earlier LBC interview this morning she said that she had not actually watched the programme, because \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t have time to watch anything these days, to be honest.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Speaking to LBC, Badenoch also stated her belief that Adolescence was based on a true story. She said:\par \par \uc2 The story which it is based on has been fundamentally changed and so creating policy on a work of fiction rather than on reality is the real issue.\par \par \uc2 But, in an interview last week on the News Agents podcast, Thorne said there was no truth in the claim that he had adapted a real story involving a black boy. He said:\par \par \uc2 They\u8217\'20\'19ve claimed that Stephen [Graham] and I based it on a story and so they\u8217\'20\'19re saying that we race swapped it, because we were basically here and then ended up there, and everything else, and nothing is further from the truth.\par \par \uc2 I have told a lot of real-life stories in my time. I know the harm that can come when you take elements of a real-life story, and you put it on screen, and the people aren\u8217\'20\'19t expecting it. There is no part of this that\u8217\'20\'19s based on a true story, not one single part.\par \par \uc2 Asked about critics who complained about the main character being white, Thorne said:\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s absurd to say that this is only committed by black boys, it\u8217\'20\'19s absurd, it\u8217\'20\'19s not true. And history shows a lot of cases of kids from all races committing these crimes.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re not making a point about race with this. We are making a point about masculinity. We\u8217\'20\'19re trying to get inside a problem. We\u8217\'20\'19re not saying this is one thing or another. We\u8217\'20\'19re saying this is about boys.\par \par \uc2 The claim that Adolescence was based on a real story involving a black boy has been circulated on social media, including by people claiming that the colour of the main character was changed to conform with an anti-white agenda.\par \par \uc2 Ian Miles Cheong, a prominent rightwing commentator on X, floated this argument last month in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/stillgray/status/1902584599676412288"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a post}}}\uc2  that has attracted 4.8m views saying:\par \par \uc2 Netflix\uc2  has a show called Adolescence that\u8217\'20\'19s about a British knife killer who stabbed a girl to death on a bus and it\u8217\'20\'19s based on real life cases such as the Southport murderer.\par \par \uc2 So guess what. They race swapped the actual killer from a black man/migrant to a white boy and the story has it so he was radicalized online by the red pill movement.\par \par \uc2 Just the absolute state of anti-white propaganda.\par \par \uc2 This attracted a comment from \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , the X owner, billionaire Trump ally and far-right provocateur, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/elonmusk/status/1902586606575313345"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 saying}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CWow.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Badenoch has been accused, including by \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  at PMQs, of spending too much time reading social media.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 Lisa Smart, the Lib Dem foreign affairs spokesperson, says she is in the unusual position of agreeing with much of what the Conservative spokesperson said. She also asks about China, and in particular about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.standard.co.uk/news/london/chinese-super-embassy-london-mint-court-plans-inquiry-b1209889.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 China\u8217\'20\'19s application }}}\uc2  to build a new super embassy in China.\par \par \uc2 Jarvis says national security has been the core priority for the government in dealing with the embassy application. But he says there is a limit to what he can say because the final decision will be taken by \uc2 Angela Rayner\uc2 , as housing secretary, in a quasi-judicial capacity.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.54pm BST\par \par \uc2 Jarvis refuses to rule out China being included in Firs enhanced tier in future\par \par \uc2 Jarvis is responding to Philp.\par \par \uc2 He says he can understand why Philp is asking about China.\par \par \uc2 Countries will be considered separately, he says.\par \par \uc2 He says he won\u8217\'20\'19t speculate on what countries may or may not be included in the Firs (foreign influence registration scheme) enhanced tier in future.\par \par \uc2 On China, the government\u8217\'20\'19s policy is to \u8220\'20\'1Ccoperate where we can, compete where we need to, and challenge where we must\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 Tories demand to know why China not included in enhanced tier of foreign influence registration scheme\par \par \uc2 Chris Philp\uc2 , the shadow home secretary, is responding to Jarvis.\par \par \uc2 He welcomes the decision about Russia.\par \par \uc2 But he says Jarvis ignored \u8220\'20\'1Cthe elephant in the room\u8221\'20\'1D, China. Why was he silent?\par \par \uc2 Philp goes on:\par \par \uc2 We know China engages in industrial scale espionage, seeking to steal technology from government, universities and from industry.\par \par \uc2 They repress Chinese citizens here, and have sought to infiltrate our political system. In 2022 it was exposed by MI5 that China sought to infiltrate this very parliament via their agent, Christine Lee.\par \par \uc2 China has set up undeclared and illegal police stations in the UK and in December China placed a bounty on the head of three Hong Kong dissidents living in the UK.\par \par \uc2 He says MI5 and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  have both warned about the \u8220\'20\'1Cepic threat\u8221\'20\'1D posed by China.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 Russia to be in enhanced tier of foreign influence registration scheme, security minister \uc2 Dan Jarvis\uc2  tells MPs\par \par \uc2 Dan Jarvis\uc2 , the security miniser is now making a Commons statement about the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/national-security-bill-factsheets/foreign-influence-registration-scheme-factsheet"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 foreign influence registration scheme}}}\uc2 , an initiative to allow the government to monitor people lobbying on behalf of foreign governments in the UK. It is part of the National Security Act 2023.\par \par \uc2 Jarvis says it will go live in July.\par \par \uc2 The scheme imposes routine requirements for people lobbying on behalf of foreign governments, but it also has an enhanced tier, covering hostile countries, where the rules are much tighter. If a country is in this tier, anyone carrying out activity in the UK on behalf of the country, or an entity linked to it, will have to register. Failure to comply will be an offence.\par \par \uc2 The government has already said that Iran will be in the enhanced tier.\par \par \uc2 Today Jarvis says Russia will also be in the enhanced tier.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.36pm BST\par \par \uc2 Green party claim Sentencing Council bill based on 'wilful misunderstanding' and will lead to more unjust punishments\par \par \uc2 The Green party has also criticised the government\u8217\'20\'19s decision to introduce the sentencing guidelines (pre-sentence reports) bill. In a statement, the Green MP Si\u226\'00\'E2n Berry said:\par \par \uc2 It has been chilling to witness the government pile pressure on an independent body which has been nothing but clear about the importance of these guidelines for passing effective sentences.\par \par \uc2 The new guidelines, which were publicly consulted on, would have helped create safer sentencing for many women, parents, pregnant and young people, for whom extreme custodial sentences can compound the harm that led to their offending in the first place. This delay will condemn too many people to unjust punishment while we wait for new plans.\par \par \uc2 Pre-sentencing reports offer judges information, they do not determine sentences. Politicians\u8217\'20\'19 wilful misunderstanding of these processes is dangerous and will cause irrevocable harm for many if this bill is passed.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 In the Commons the Labour MP \uc2 Nadia Whittome\uc2  said she was alarmed by the implication that, if \uc2 the Sentencing Council\uc2  guidelines are withdrawn, judges would not be advised to get pre-sentence reports before sentencing pregnant women.\par \par \uc2 Mahmood said the bill would not affect policy relating to pregnant offenders, because there is already court of appeal precedent saying pre-sentence reports should be obtained before pregnant women are sentenced.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.20pm BST\par \par \uc2 Diane Abbott\uc2  says government should not be interfering with independence of Sentencing Council\par \par \uc2 Diane Abbott\uc2 , the Labour MP and a former shadow home secretary, told Mahmood she did not know why she was so \u8220\'20\'1Ctriumphant\u8221\'20\'1D about introducing legislation to interfer with the independence of \uc2 the Sentencing Council\uc2 . She said there have been many reports saying black and brown people are treated unfairly by the courts, and she said the judiciary was independent for a good reason.\par \par \uc2 There is a reason why \uc2 the Sentencing Council\uc2  is independent. It was made a statutory independent body to avoid even the appearance of ministerial interfering in sentencing. This is not the United States. Our political system, our judicial system, are entirely separate.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.12pm BST\par \par \uc2 In response to Jenrick, \uc2 Shabana Mahmood\uc2  said she would not comment on the two Sentencing Council members he criticised. (See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/apr/01/uk-politics-labour-conservatives-trump-tariffs-trade-deal-keir-starmer-latest-news?page=with%3Ablock-67ebe3b38f08a46a40e92f27#block-67ebe3b38f08a46a40e92f27"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2.10pm}}}\uc2 .) She said, unlike like Jenrick, she did not want to \u8220\'20\'1Cmake it personal\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 Robert Jenrick\uc2  suggests chair of Sentencing Council should resign\par \par \uc2 The emergency legislation introduced by the govenrment is a direct result of \uc2 Robert Jenrick\uc2 , the shadow justice secretary, denouncing \uc2 the Sentencing Council\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s guidelines as \u8220\'20\'1Ctwo-tier justice\u8221\'20\'1D. Until then, the guidelines had been accepted as reasonable by the government and almost everyone else (including the last Tory government).\par \par \uc2 In response to Mahmood, Jenrick claimed she had \u8220\'20\'1Ccompletely lost control of the justice system\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He said there were other guidelines in the legal system that amounted to two-tier justice.\par \par \uc2 And he suggested the chair of \uc2 the Sentencing Council\uc2 , Lord Justice William Davis, and another council member, Johanna Robinson, should resign. He claimed Davis had called for short sentences to be abolished, and he said Robinson has described the Tory Illegal Immigration Act as appalling.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.12pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.01pm BST\par \par \uc2 Mahmood confirms new bill will stop Sentencing Council making pre-sentence report guidelines relating to race\par \par \uc2 Mahmood says how to make the justice system fairer is a policy matter for parliament. It is for politcians to decide policy, she says, and for judges to apply policy.\par \par \uc2 She says today she is introducing a sentencing guidelines (pre-sentence reports) bill. It is a tightly-focused bill, she says.\par \par \uc2 The bill adopts a targeted approach. It does not prevent [Sentencing Council] guidance from advising in general terms that pre-sentence reports should be requested when judges need more information about an offender\u8217\'20\'19s personal circumstances.\par \par \uc2 But it does prohibit the council from making guidelines about pre-sentence reports with specific reference to the offender\u8217\'20\'19s personal characteristics, such as their race, religion or belief or cultural background.\par \par \uc2 Mahmood says this raises further question about what the role of \uc2 the Sentencing Council\uc2  should be. She says this is something the government will consider going ahead. If further legislation is needed, the government will include it in the forthcoming sentencing bill, she says.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.53pm BST\par \par \uc2 Mahmood says, despite 'noble' intentions behind controversial Sentencing Council guidelines, they went too far\par \par \uc2 Mahmood explains how the new guidelines were drawn up.\par \par \uc2 She says she is in favour of the use of pre-sentence reports.\par \par \uc2 But the new guidances would have encouraged judges to get them for some offenders, but not others. Judge would have been told that they would normally have been needed for people from ethnic, cultural or faith minorities.\par \par \uc2 She says pre-sentence reports can lead to people getting lower sentences, and so this would have created the \u8220\'20\'1Cperception of differential treatment before the law\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 She says the intention behind the guidelines was to address real inequalities that exist in sentencing. She goes on:\par \par \uc2 There is no doubt that more must be done to understand the problem we face and to address it. There are some measures already taking place across our justice system to make it more representative of the public that it serves, such that it can deliver outcomes in which we can all have confidence.\par \par \uc2 And I note that the proportion of ethnic minorities within the judiciary has risen from just 7% 10 years ago to 11% today.\par \par \uc2 While change can feel slow and must accelerate, my view is that despite the noble intentions behind them in attempting to address inequalities in our justice system, these guidelines sacrifice too much.\par \par \uc2 They raise a serious question of policy; in the pursuit of equality of outcome for different religions and races, should we treat them differently before the eyes of the law, and move so far away from an ideal that has underpinned justice in this country for centuries? On this I am clear all must be equal before the law.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.47pm BST\par \par \uc2 Shabana Mahmood\uc2  makes statement to MPs about Sentencing Council\par \par \uc2 In the Commons \uc2 Shabana Mahmood\uc2 , the justice secretary, is making a statement to MPs about \uc2 the Sentencing Council\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She says the recent guidelines proposed by the council raise the issue of whether everyone is equal before the law.\par \par \uc2 Following constructive talks with the council, the guidelines have been put on hold, she says.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 At the Downing Street lobby briefing, like \uc2 Angela Rayner\uc2  (see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/apr/01/uk-politics-labour-conservatives-trump-tariffs-trade-deal-keir-starmer-latest-news?page=with%3Ablock-67ebcb978f08a46a40e92ded#block-67ebcb978f08a46a40e92ded"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 12.23pm}}}\uc2  ), the PM\u8217\'20\'19s spokesperson ruled out cancelling President Trump\u8217\'20\'19s state visit to the UK as retaliation for his tariffs. The spokesperson claimed the two issues were not linked. He said:\par \par \uc2 I wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t draw any any link between the two. Obviously, the state visit is a matter for the Palace, as you know.\par \par \uc2 You\u8217\'20\'19ll have seen that the prime minister was delighted to extend His Majesty the king\u8217\'20\'19s invitation for a historic state visit during his visit to to the White House.\par \par \uc2 But when it comes to these talks, we\u8217\'20\'19ll obviously continue to have these conversations. We\u8217\'20\'19ll obviously continue them in the national interest, and we\u8217\'20\'19ll obviously provide an update as and when we have one.\par \par \uc2 In the past \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  has also claimed that the state visit is a matter for the king, not the government. While technically true in a very narrow sense (the king issues the actual invitation), this argument is thoroughly bogus, because the king only issue a state visit invitation on the advice of Downing Street. It was Starmer\u8217\'20\'19s decision, not \uc2 King Charles\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.36pm BST\par \par \uc2 In an interview with Times Radio, Lord Darroch, a former UK ambassador to Washington, said he was sceptical about claims that the government is close to signing a trade deal with the US.\par \par \uc2 In an interview recorded before the Downing Street lobby briefing where No 10 said \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  told cabinet that talks were at an \u8220\'20\'1Cadvanced stage\u8221\'20\'1D, Darroch was asked how he responded to claims that an agreement was close. He replied:\par \par \uc2 The honest answer is quite cynical, because at various points when I was in government, we were negotiating, trying to negotiate a free trade deal. The EU also tried to do a US-EU free trade deal when we were in the EU. So I\u8217\'20\'19ve seen this picture play several times.\par \par \uc2 And the truth of it is that the Americans, whoever is in government, always, they try and strike a very tough deal on trade.\par \par \uc2 Darroch also said that the government should be wary of giving President Trump concessions to get exemptions from his tariffs. He explained:\par \par \uc2 I just think you need to be careful about, as it were, giving the Trump administration, giving the president a win on all of this, because he already thinks tariffs are a great idea. And if he starts getting concessions offered by the rest of the world, to keep them away, he\u8217\'20\'19ll keep using them.\par \par \uc2 And every time there is some grievance or some dispute in relations, he\u8217\'20\'19ll come back and threaten tariffs or impose them and then say, do what I want you to do to get them lifted.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.18pm BST\par \par \uc2 David Lammy\uc2 , the foreign secretary, told MPs that the government would \u8220\'20\'1Cprepare for the worst\u8221\'20\'1D over US tariffs. Speaking during Foreign Office questions, he said:\par \par \uc2 We are an open-trading nation. We have been that under successive governments. It\u8217\'20\'19s hugely important at this time that we continue the intense conversations we\u8217\'20\'19re having with the US administration on getting an economic agreement but of course we prepare for the worst \u8211\'20\'13 all options remain on the table, as the prime minister indicated again just yesterday.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.15pm BST\par \par \uc2 Starmer tells cabinet talks with US on economic deal are at 'advanced stage'\par \par \uc2 For the record, here is the extract from the Downing Street readout from cabinet this morning, saying what \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2 , \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2  and Jonathan Reynolds told colleagues about the impending US tariffs. Starmer said talks with the US on an economic deal were at an \u8220\'20\'1Cadvanced stage\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A No 10 spokesperson said:\par \par \uc2 The prime minister then turned to US tariffs and trade. He set out the latest position to cabinet on the announced US plans on steel, aluminium, and automotive tariffs, with further details of \u8216\'20\'18reciprocal\u8217\'20\'19 global tariffs expected this week. He said the UK\u8217\'20\'19s approach is to progress ongoing talks with the US on an economic deal, which are at an advanced stage, while keeping all options on the table.\par \par \uc2 He said a calm and pragmatic approach best served UK national interests, not a knee-jerk reaction. The UK has a balanced trading relationship with the US, supporting millions of jobs both sides of the Atlantic, as well as a deeply important security and defence relationship.\par \par \uc2 He said nobody wants to see global tariffs, but the UK would continue negotiations, continue engaging with British industries, prepare for all scenarios, and double down on delivering a modern industrial strategy that supports jobs and grows the economy.\par \par \uc2 The chancellor said that global tariffs will have an impact on the UK as an open trading economy, that securing a deal could mitigate some of those effects, and updated on discussions she had with the \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  yesterday.\par \par \uc2 The business secretary updated on the progress of his discussions in recent days and weeks, and said that the UK was well placed to agree an economic deal with the US and that those talks would continue beyond tomorrow\u8217\'20\'19s announcement. He underlined that the business community wants to see the government take a calm, cool-headed, and pragmatic approach to discussions with the US, and that would guide our approach.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.01pm BST\par \par \uc2 Conservation groups criticise Starmer over 'spiders blocking housing' claims\par \par \uc2 Helena Horton is a Guardian environment reporter.\par \par \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  has been asked to retract false claims he made about spiders by a group of leading conservation charities.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister claimed in a recent article that plans to build over 15,000 homes near the Swanscombe Peninsula in Kent were blocked due to the presence of the distinguished jumping spider.\par \par \uc2 However, the nature charities have said this is \u8220\'20\'1Cfake news\u8221\'20\'1D in a scandal they are branding \u8220\'20\'1Cspidergate\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Starmer has been asked to visit the peninsula to see the nature for himself, in the letter signed by the Wildlife Trusts, Campaign for Protecting Rural England, Buglife and Wildlife and Countryside Link.\par \par \uc2 The letter reads:\par \par \uc2 The vast majority of the 15,000 homes are still progressing, with thousands already built. The only area affected by wildlife concerns is part of the Swanscombe Peninsula, a designated Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI), where 1,300 homes were proposed.\par \par \uc2 Contrary to your claim, the distinguished jumping spider was not found in the area earmarked for these homes but in other parts of the SSSI. The Swanscombe Peninsula supports an extraordinary range of rare and endangered species, including over 1,700 species of invertebrates, nationally scarce botanical species such as the man orchid, and UK Red List bird species like the nightingale, grasshopper warbler, cuckoo, and breeding marsh harrier. It was designated an SSSI for its national importance in terms of plants, geology, birds, and invertebrates - a wealth of biodiversity that deserves protection.\par \par \uc2 The suggestion that conservation protections are blocking necessary housing development is misleading and unhelpful. We must stop framing nature and growth as opposing forces. Sustainable development can and should integrate environmental considerations, ensuring that new communities are liveable and ecologically sustainable.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.03pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.40pm BST\par \par \uc2 Asylum system risks \u8216\'20\'18damaging social cohesion\u8217\'20\'19, \uc2 Glasgow city council\uc2  warns\par \par \uc2 The asylum system risks \u8220\'20\'1Cdamaging social cohesion\u8221\'20\'1D with homeless refugees putting \u8220\'20\'1Cunprecedented pressure\u8221\'20\'1D on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk/glasgow"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Glasgow}}}\uc2  services, the city council has warned. Libby Brooks has the story.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2025/apr/01/asylum-system-risks-damaging-social-cohesion-glasgow-city-council-warns"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Asylum system risks \u8216\'20\'18damaging social cohesion\u8217\'20\'19, Glasgow city council warns}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 There are two urgent questions in the Commons after 12.30pm, on \uc2 NHS\uc2  pensions and on the Adoption and Special Guardianship Support Fund.\par \par \uc2 Then, after about 1.30pm, \uc2 Shabana Mahmood\uc2 , the justice secretary, will make a statement on \uc2 the Sentencing Council\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That will be followed, about an hour later, by a statement from \uc2 Dan Jarvis\uc2 , the security minister, about the implementation of the foreign influence registration scheme. Potentially this could be the most controversial of all four announcements, because any announcement that does not involve China being placed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hongkongwatch.org/all-posts/2025/12/2/human-rights-groups-urge-uk-home-secretary-to-strengthen-foreign-influence-registration-scheme"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in the enhanced tier}}}\uc2  will outrage the China hawks on the Tory benches.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.23pm BST\par \par \uc2 Rayner says it would be 'ridiculous' to cancel Trump's state visit in retaliation for tariffs\par \par \uc2 Vine says the UK is not exempt from the US tariffs.\par \par \uc2 Q: All that charm offensive hasn\u8217\'20\'19t worked. We have not got leverage. We will have to change our approach?\par \par \uc2 Rayner says the government has put \u8220\'20\'1Cfoundations\u8221\'20\'1D in place that will help relations in the future.\par \par \uc2 Q: Why don\u8217\'20\'19t we cancel the state visit?\par \par \uc2 Rayner says this is not about being nice to President Trump. It is about recognising that the UK and the US have a fair and balanced trading relationship.\par \par \uc2 Q: Should we cancel the state visit?\par \par \uc2 Rayner replies:\par \par \uc2 I think that\u8217\'20\'19s ridiculous. I don\u8217\'20\'19t think that\u8217\'20\'19s where we\u8217\'20\'19re at at all.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not about who visits the king or not. It\u8217\'20\'19s about putting the British interest first, and that\u8217\'20\'19s what our government is doing.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.18pm BST\par \par \uc2 Angela Rayner\uc2 , the deputy PM, is being interviewed by Jeremy Vine {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sounds/play/live/bbc_radio_two"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 on Radio 2.}}}\par \par \uc2 She is there to talk about the cost of living, and the impact of the national living wage rise.\par \par \uc2 Vine reads out figures for how utility bill increases, and says these rises are \u8220\'20\'1Cunsustainable\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Mabel Banfield-Nwachi has an equivalent Guardian list here.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2025/mar/31/awful-april-bill-rises-council-tax-energy-tv-licence-car-tax"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Awful April\u8217\'20\'19: bill rises Britons face, from council tax to energy and cars}}}\par \par \uc2 Rayner lists various meausures being taken by Labour to help, including: the pension triple lock, the winter fuel payments staying for the poorest pensioners, the rise in the national living wage, measures to give workers more job security, breakfast clubs, and more support for council budgets.\par \par \uc2 But Labour is dealing with the \u8220\'20\'1C14 years of the mess that the Conservatives left behind\u8221\'20\'1D, she says.\par \par \uc2 Q: Do you think putting up employer NICs was a mistake?\par \par \uc2 Rayner ignores the question, and returns to the point about the national living wage going up. That will mean workers have more money to spend on the high street, which will help firms.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.05pm BST\par \par \uc2 Q: Would you like to see the UK laws around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  buffer zones reviewed to help get a trade deal?\par \par \uc2 Badenoch says she supports free speech. But she says she does not want to see laws over-interpreted.\par \par \uc2 She says she does not want to see US \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars coming to the UK.\par \par \uc2 She says she does not know the full details of the case cited by the US state department. She says she voted against the buffer zone law, because she thought it was too strict. But she says parliament passed the law, and it should be respected.\par \par \uc2 Here is Jamie Grierson \u8217\'20\'19s story about the claim that the UK\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  buffer zone laws might be an obstacle to a trade deal with the US.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2025/apr/01/uk-business-secretary-denies-free-speech-issue-featured-in-us-tariff-talks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK business secretary denies free speech issue featured in US tariff talks}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.55am BST\par \par \uc2 Badenoch rejects claims differing food standards might be obstacle to US trade deals, saying quotas more important\par \par \uc2 Q: Have you encouraged JD Vance or any senior Republicans to spare the UK from tariffs?\par \par \uc2 No, says Badenoch. She says it is not right for her to cut across what the government is doing.\par \par \uc2 Q: Would you support a trade deal allowing US chlorinated chicken to be imported into the UK?\par \par \uc2 Badenoch says she does not believe the obstacles to a US trade deal are standards. They are quotas, she says. She goes on:\par \par \uc2 Nobody is going to be forced to eat chlorinated chicken. I don\u8217\'20\'19t even think most chicken in the US is actually washed with chlorine, because it\u8217\'20\'19s a myth that has expanded quite a lot over the years. Things have changed. We need to focus on what\u8217\'20\'19s actually in a trade deal.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.51am BST\par \par \uc2 Q: You say Labour\u8217\'20\'19s decisions have broken voters\u8217\'20\'19 trust. But do you accept that the cost of living crisis started under the Tories, and that the \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  mini-budget destroyed your party\u8217\'20\'19s credibility.\par \par \uc2 Badenoch says the Tory reputation was built over decades and centuries, not just over the past few years.\par \par \uc2 She says Tory politicians have worked in business. They understand it. Labour ministers don\u8217\'20\'19t, she claims.\par \par \uc2 She says the Covid crisis had to be paid for.\par \par \uc2 Labour has now done something destructive, she says.\par \par \uc2 Q: What you say on tariffs sounds very similar to what \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  is saying. Is that right?\par \par \uc2 Badenoch says it will be \u8220\'20\'1Cexcellent\u8221\'20\'1D if Starmer avoids retaliatory tariffs.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.36am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.48am BST\par \par \uc2 Q: How would you be responding to the Trump tariffs?\par \par \uc2 Badenoch says at her first PMQs as Tory leader she called for a trade deal with the US.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.47am BST\par \par \uc2 Badenoch rejects claim she has told her MPs not to criticise Trump\par \par \uc2 Q: Why have you {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dailymail.co.uk/news/article-14551239/Tory-MPs-bemoan-Kemi-Badenochs-near-ban-criticising-Trump.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reportedly told your MPs not to criticise President Trump?}}}\par \par \uc2 I haven\u8217\'20\'19t, says Badenoch.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.45am BST\par \par \uc2 Badenoch and Stride are now taking questions.\par \par \uc2 Asked what the Tories would do instead, Badenoch says the Tories would not have introduced this measure.\par \par \uc2 But she says the party is not going to produce its budget plans now.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.43am BST\par \par \uc2 Mel Stride\uc2 , the shadow chancellor, is speaking now.\par \par \uc2 He is setting out how the Tories have calculated that the \u8220\'20\'1Cjobs tax\u8221\'20\'1D will cost every family \u163\'00\'A33,500 over the course of this parliament.\par \par \uc2 He says the \uc2 OBR\uc2  estimated how much money would be raised by the jobs tax.\par \par \uc2 From that, the Tories subtracted how much would be used to compensate public services needing to pay higher employer NICs.\par \par \uc2 He says the \uc2 OBR\uc2  has estimated that 76% of the cost of the higher employer NICs will ultimately be paid by firms paying lower wages.\par \par \uc2 So the Tories have taken the figures for the revenue from the tax rise, minus the amount being used to compensate the public sector, calculated what 76% of it would come to, and then divided that by the number of families in Britain.\par \par \uc2 That has produced the \u163\'00\'A33,500 per household figure, he says.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.40am BST\par \par \uc2 Here is a live feed of the Tory press conference.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.38am BST\par \par \uc2 Badenoch claims Labour's 'jobs tax' will cost average families \u163\'00\'A33,500 by end of this parliament\par \par \uc2 Kemi Badenoch\uc2  is holding a press conference now.\par \par \uc2 She is criticising the government over the impact of the rise in employer national insurance contributions (NICs), which the Tories call the \u8220\'20\'1Cjobs tax\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 She says the average family will lose \u163\'00\'A33,500 by the end of this parliament as a result, because firms will respond to this by reducing wages (or not increasing them by as much as they otherwise would, to be more precise, although Badenoch does not spell that out).\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.30am BST\par \par \uc2 Lib Dems call for Cobra meeting to discuss US tariffs, saying 'we can't kowtow to Trump any longer'\par \par \uc2 It is hard for small parties to get in the papers, but the Liberal Democrats have had many years of practice and one of their solutions is to respond to a crisis by either calling for the recall of parliament (during recess), or calling for a meeting of Cobra (the Whitehall emergency committee). Their requests are normally ignored, but they do count as news (sort of) and trend has become a Westminster lobby in-joke.\par \par \uc2 So, when a press release arrived this morning from the Lib Dems calling for a Cobra meeting to discuss the US tariffs, there was a debate in the office about whether this was a self-deprecatory April fool\u8217\'20\'19s day joke.\par \par \uc2 But it\u8217\'20\'19s not, the Lib Dems tell us. They really do want Cobra (which normally deals with natural disasters and terrorist incidents) to discuss the Trump tariffs. Calum Miller, the Lib Dem foreign affairs spokersperson, said:\par \par \uc2 The prime minister should call a Cobra meeting today to coordinate Britain\u8217\'20\'19s response to Trump\u8217\'20\'19s trade war \u8211\'20\'13 including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/mar/31/keir-starmer-immigration-yvette-cooper-labour-uk-politics-latest-live-news?page=with%3Ablock-67ea998b8f08167d07b361fe#block-67ea998b8f08167d07b361fe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 plans for Tesla tariffs }}}\uc2  and emergency measures to boost demand in the hardest hit sectors.\par \par \uc2 The government needs to pull out all the stops to protect businesses and families from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s destructive and needless trade war.\par \par \uc2 We can\u8217\'20\'19t kowtow to Trump any longer. That is why we\u8217\'20\'19re urging the government to urgently start talks on a new trade deal with our European neighbours, so we can negotiate with Washington from the strongest position possible.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.21am BST\par \par \uc2 Limited global trade war, with Britain exempt from US tariffs, could have 'mildly positive' impact for UK, MPs told\par \par \uc2 Prof David Miles, a member of the \uc2 OBR\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s budget responsibility committee, told the Treasury committee that a very limited trade war triggered by US tariffs could have positive effects for the UK economy. He explained:\par \par \uc2 Let\u8217\'20\'19s suppose there\u8217\'20\'19s a very limited tariff war. So it\u8217\'20\'19s China, Mexico, Canada, countries [where] already it\u8217\'20\'19s pretty clear what Trump is going to do, and that\u8217\'20\'19s it. And the UK stays out of it, so does most of the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 It wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be unreasonable to think that could be, if anything, very, very mildly positive. There\u8217\'20\'19s a bit of trade that will get diverted to the UK. And some of the exports from China, for example, that would have gone to the US, they\u8217\'20\'19ll be looking for a home for them in the rest of the world. And stuff will be available in the UK a bit cheaper than otherwise would have been.\par \par \uc2 Miles said this was not the most probable scenario.\par \par \uc2 All the other scenarios, which did involve the UK facing tariffs, would be negative for the economy, to different degrees, he said.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.11am BST\par \par \uc2 OBR\uc2  did not include US tariff policy in its forecasts partly because it was 'changing every day', its chair says\par \par \uc2 At the Treasury committee Richard Hughes is now being asked about tariffs.\par \par \uc2 John Glen (Con) says the US announced car tariffs last week. If that had come before the \uc2 OBR\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s report was written, would that have used up all the chancellor\u8217\'20\'19s headroom?\par \par \uc2 Hughes says there were elements of that included in the \u8220\'20\'1Cdownside forecasts\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Q: Why did you not explicitly include tariffs in your forecasts?\par \par \uc2 Hughes says the \uc2 OBR\uc2  is not as big as the \uc2 IMF\uc2  or the \uc2 OECD\uc2 . It does not have the capacity to produce its own economic model for the global economy. So it relies on the \uc2 IMF\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s, he says.\par \par \uc2 And he says there is a second reason why tariffs were not included.\par \par \uc2 The other reason to not take account of us tariff policy is it was changing every day, depending on what phone call the president had with one or another head of state.\par \par \uc2 And so in that sense, it was very hard to choose what day\u8217\'20\'19s tariff policy in the US to really reflect on our forecast.\par \par \uc2 But he also points out that the \uc2 OBR\uc2  report included a section giving {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/live/2025/mar/26/spring-statement-rachel-reeves-budget-labour-benefit-cuts-pmqs-keir-starmer-kemi-badenoch-uk-politics-latest-news?page=with%3Ablock-67e3ff148f084978da0320de#block-67e3ff148f084978da0320de"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 three scenarios}}}\uc2  for how US tariff policy could affect the UK.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.51am BST\par \par \uc2 Starmer says firms needs 'calm' not 'knee-jerk' response to US tariffs, playing down prospect of retaliatory measures\par \par \uc2 In his Sky News interview this morning (see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/apr/01/uk-politics-labour-conservatives-trump-tariffs-trade-deal-keir-starmer-latest-news?page=with%3Ablock-67eba8098f08bb7e41c3727b#block-67eba8098f08bb7e41c3727b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 9.53am}}}\uc2  ) it was put to \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  that, even though he says all options are on the table in relation to retaliatory tariffs, his reference to not wanting a trade war implies he will not impose tariffs on US imports.\par \par \uc2 As {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.sky.com/story/politics-latest-live-reeves-spring-statement-welfare-defence-cuts-starmer-economy-reform-farage-12593360"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sky reports}}}\uc2 , Starmer replied:\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m not going to go ahead of myself. But I am talking to the sectors most impacted, and I think what they want most is a calm and collected response to this, not a knee-jerk response.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.41am BST\par \par \uc2 Badenoch says Tories would oppose retaliatory tariffs against US because they would just make 'everyone poorer'\par \par \uc2 In her LBC interview this morning \uc2 Kemi Badenoch\uc2  said she would be opposed to the UK imposing retaliatory tariffs on the US. She said:\par \par \uc2 A trade deal is the best way but it has to be something significant and comprehensive, not just a deal that tinkers at the margins in easy areas.\par \par \uc2 Some people will want us to have trade retaliation \u8211\'20\'13 that just makes everyone poorer.\par \par \uc2 This is a time for significant diplomacy and showing that actually, if you put tariffs on, the people who will suffer aren\u8217\'20\'19t just our exporters but also the American consumer who will have to pay more.\par \par \uc2 Unlike the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , the UK has not yet announced any retaliatory measures in response to the tariffs President Trump has already unveiled, and \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  does not seem keen to go down this route. But he has not ruled it out, saying all options are on the table.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.42am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.21am BST\par \par \uc2 Starmer says he accepts cost of living crisis 'ongoing', despite wages going up\par \par \uc2 As Rupert Jones reports, today is when millions of people face price rises because 1 April is the day when many routine bills go up.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2025/apr/01/bill-rises-in-awful-april-energy-water-car-tax-tv-licences"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Millions of Britons brace for across-the-board bill rises in \u8216\'20\'18awful April\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 Speaking to Sky News this morning, \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  said he accepted that the cost of living crisis was ongoing. But he insisted that government policies, such as the rise in the national living wage coming into effect today, would provide people with help. He said:\par \par \uc2 I think for most people, they would say the cost-of-living crisis is ongoing, and they feel the pressure financially.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s why it\u8217\'20\'19s so important we make good on our pledge that people would feel better off and the national living wage going up today by an average of \u163\'00\'A31,400 is going to affect millions of people, so in their pay packet this month, and obviously for months to come, they will now be getting more money,\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s alongside the other work we\u8217\'20\'19ve done \u8211\'20\'13 interest rates have been cut three times since we\u8217\'20\'19ve had a Labour government, so anybody with a mortgage knows that the rates are coming down, and of course, on average, wages going up now more quickly than prices.\par \par \uc2 I acknowledge that with bills coming in, people see that rise and that is a pressure. That is why it\u8217\'20\'19s so important we deliver on the national living wage, to make sure people are better off \u8211\'20\'13 \u163\'00\'A31,400, quite a significant amount of money for millions of workers.\par \par \uc2 Making sure that interest rates are coming down not going up, that makes a big difference, wages going up higher than prices.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.09am BST\par \par \uc2 OBR\uc2  chair Richard Hughes gives evidence to Treasury committee\par \par \uc2 Richard Hughes, chair of the \uc2 Office for Budget Responsibility\uc2 , is giving evidence to the Treasury committee. There is a live feed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.parliamentlive.tv/Event/Index/6ded3d58-80d3-484e-9e4f-fa9dbbb5fb42"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here.}}}\par \par \uc2 Hughes started by telling the committee that he wrote to the chancellor earlier this year to say that, when his five-year term ends later this year, he would like to have a second term in office.\par \par \uc2 He said he had not yet had a reply.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.53am BST\par \par \uc2 Starmer dismisses claims he's been 'played' by Trump, and says future trade deal could lessen impact of tariffs\par \par \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  has dismissed claims that he has been \u8220\'20\'1Cplayed\u8221\'20\'1D by President Trump over tariffs.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with Sky News this morning, echoing what Jonathan Reynolds said in his morning interview round (see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/apr/01/uk-politics-labour-conservatives-trump-tariffs-trade-deal-keir-starmer-latest-news?page=with%3Ablock-67eb8da48f08e6b4b7c08372#block-67eb8da48f08e6b4b7c08372"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  8.58am}}}\uc2  ), Starmer said that a future trade deal with the US might lead to the UK getting some exemptions from the tariffs coming tomorrow. He said:\par \par \uc2 We are of course negotiating an economic deal which will, I hope \u8230\'20\'26 mitigate the tariffs.\par \par \uc2 Asked if he had been \u8220\'20\'1Cplayed\u8221\'20\'1D by US President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Starmer replied:\par \par \uc2 The US is our closest ally. Our defence, our security, our intelligence are bound up in a way that no two other countries are.\par \par \uc2 So it\u8217\'20\'19s obviously in our national interest to have a close working relationship with the US, which we\u8217\'20\'19ve had for decades, and I want to ensure we have for decades to come.\par \par \uc2 He said talks on an economic deal would normally take \u8220\'20\'1Cmonths or years\u8221\'20\'1D but \u8220\'20\'1Cin a matter of weeks we have got well advanced in those discussions\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Starmer also confirmed that it was likely the UK would be affected by the tariffs being announced tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 We are obviously working with the sectors most impacted at pace on that.\par \par \uc2 Nobody wants to see a trade war but I have to act in the national interests.\par \par \uc2 That means that \u8220\'20\'1Call options remain on the table\u8221\'20\'1D in response, he added.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.53am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.44am BST\par \par \uc2 Badenoch says local elections will be 'very difficult' for Tories\par \par \uc2 Q: Nigel Farage says you are lazy, and that you only work hard for a few hours in the afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Badenoch asks how he would know. She says he has never met her. He throws abuse at politicians to get attention, she says.\par \par \uc2 Q: Since you became leader, the Tory poll ratings have gone down. Are you failing?\par \par \uc2 Badenoch says polls are just a snapshot. She says it took the opposition 14 years to get back to power last time a party lost office, 13 years the time before that, and 18 years the time before that. She has just been in office for five months, she says.\par \par \uc2 But she says the local elections will be hard for Tories.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re going to have very difficult local elections. These local elections are not going to be fun for the Conservative party, because the last time we fought them was in 2021 with \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 , when there was a vaccine bounce just after Covid. So we are going to have a tough time.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.37am BST\par \par \uc2 Q: What have you learned from the \uc2 Netflix\uc2  drama Adolescence that \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  was talking about yesterday?\par \par \uc2 Badenoch says she is aware of this, but she says she has not watched it. She does not have time, she says.\par \par \uc2 When it is put to her that she should be aware of what people are talking about, she replies:\par \par \uc2 I pay attention, but I\u8217\'20\'19m not going to watch every single thing that everybody\u8217\'20\'19s watching on \uc2 Netflix\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.34am BST\par \par \uc2 Q: Do you agree with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thesun.co.uk/news/34174454/dinghy-migrants-free-hotels-household-bills/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sun splash story }}}\uc2  saying the UK is allowing Albanian migrants to stay in comfortable hotel rooms?\par \par \uc2 Badenoch says that is the reality of the migration system now.\par \par \uc2 She says the last Conservative government did not get everything right.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  opposed almost everything that government was doing to try to deal with the problem, she says.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.31am BST\par \par \uc2 Q: Do you agree with Jordan Bardella\u8217\'20\'19s claim that the conviction of \uc2 Marine Le Pen\uc2  for embezzlement in France means democracy has been executed?\par \par \uc2 Badenoch says she does not agree with that. She says she respects the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 But it would be different if \uc2 Le Pen\uc2  were being prosecuted for her politics, she says.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.28am BST\par \par \uc2 Q: Is it true that you have banned shadow ministers from criticising \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , as a paper reported at the weekend?\par \par \uc2 No, says Badenoch. She says that is not true.\par \par \uc2 But she says politicians from one country should not endlessly be criticsing the internal politics of another country. It is better to make criticisms in private, she says.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.26am BST\par \par \uc2 Kemi Badenoch\uc2  interviewed on LBC\par \par \uc2 Kemi Badenoch\uc2  is being interviewed on LBC now.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the Telegraph story (see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/apr/01/uk-politics-labour-conservatives-trump-tariffs-trade-deal-keir-starmer-latest-news?page=with%3Ablock-67eb9f778f08167d07b36cde#block-67eb9f778f08167d07b36cde"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 9.20am}}}\uc2  ), she says she thinks we do have free speech in this country. But the scope of laws is being expanded.\par \par \uc2 She says she thinks the UK has \u8220\'20\'1Cthe right balance\u8221\'20\'1D on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws. She says she does not want \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to become as divisive an issue in the UK as it is in the US.\par \par \uc2 Asked if the prosecution of the woman referred to in the Telegraph story should have been prosecuted, Badenoch say she does not know the details of the case. But she is opposed to draconian interpretions of the law on this, she says.\par \par \uc2 UPDATE: Badenoch said:\par \par \uc2 I think that we\u8217\'20\'19ve found a right balance. I do, however, think that we should not be persecuting for people for expressing themselves.\par \par \uc2 I remember voting against certain bits of legislation that would penalise people like that woman \u8230\'20\'26 but I still think that, on balance, we are in a good place in our country.\par \par \uc2 We should not talk the UK down. We do have freedom of expression but it is at risk in some places if we\u8217\'20\'19re not paying attention.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.33am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.20am BST\par \par \uc2 Reynolds rejects claim prosecution of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigners in UK could block trade deal with US\par \par \uc2 This morning the Daily Telegraph has splashed on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/us/news/2025/03/31/free-speech-row-threatens-starmers-us-trade-deal/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a story}}}\uc2  claiming that the prosecution of an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigner in the UK could be an obstacle to a trade/tariff deal with the US. The paper reports:\par \par \uc2 In a highly unusual step on Sunday night, the US state department issued a statement saying it was \u8220\'20\'1Cconcerned about freedom of expression in the United Kingdom\u8221\'20\'1D in relation to the case of an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigner.\par \par \uc2 It said it was \u8220\'20\'1Cmonitoring\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://archive.is/o/YzkxK/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2025/03/07/pro-life-activist-prosecuted-holding-sign-abortion-clinic/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the case of Livia Tossici-Bolt}}}\uc2 , who was prosecuted for holding a sign near a Bournemouth \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic reading: \u8220\'20\'1CHere to talk if you want.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A verdict in the case is due on Friday \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Asked about the comments, a source familiar with trade negotiations told The Telegraph there should be \u8220\'20\'1Cno free trade without {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://archive.is/o/YzkxK/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2025/01/18/starmers-hatred-free-speech-trigger-economic-war-with-us/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 free speech}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D, a stance thought to have become a point of contention between the two nations.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the story in his interviews this morning, Jonathan Reynolds, the business secretary, said this was not an issue that had been raised with him in his talks with the Trump administration about a trade deal, which could be linked to potential tariff exemptions. He told Times Radio:\par \par \uc2 Obviously, there are things from different people in the administration that they\u8217\'20\'19ve said in the past about this, but it\u8217\'20\'19s not been part of the trade negotiations that I\u8217\'20\'19ve been part of.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.58am BST\par \par \uc2 UK in \u8216\'20\'18best possible position\u8217\'20\'19 to negotiate future exemptions from Trump tariffs, business secretary Jonathan Reynolds says\par \par \uc2 Good morning. So much for the \u8220\'20\'1Cunprecedented\u8221\'20\'1D state visit invite. The real spring statement, the one that is likely to have most impact on the UK tomorrow, is coming tomorrow, when President Trump announces global tariffs, and the government expects that the UK will not get an exemption. As Nick Robinson put it to Jonathan Reynolds, the business secretary, in the opening question of his Today programme interview this morning: \u8220\'20\'1CSucking up to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  didn\u8217\'20\'19t work, did it?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On the Today programme, and in his other interviews this morning, Reynolds\u8217\'20\'19s response was essentially: Not yet. He argued that the UK still has a good chance of winning tariff exemptions, but just not tomorrow. Or that the sucking up might still pay off \u8211\'20\'13 not that Reynolds put it quite like that.\par \par \uc2 Instead, Reynolds told Robinson:\par \par \uc2 We have engaged with the US on the potential for a deal, because that is in the UK\u8217\'20\'19s national interest, and actually would be mutually beneficial to the US and the UK \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Only the president will himself know exactly how the US is going to take tomorrow. And you\u8217\'20\'19re right to say it might not be possible for any country in the world to be exempted from the initial announcements.\par \par \uc2 But I do believe the work we have done means the UK is in the best possible position of any country to potentially reach an agreement.\par \par \uc2 I do believe UK businesses support our approach. They support the calm-headed approach, the desire to engage, to remain at the table, while we can potentially secure an agreement.\par \par \uc2 In the interview Reynolds also did not challenge the assertion that the tariff announcement tomorrow will have more impact on the British economy than last week\u8217\'20\'19s spring statement. Tomorrow would be \u8216\'20\'18\u8220\'20\'1Ca very serious and significant moment\u8221\'20\'1D, Reynolds said.\par \par \uc2 I will post more from his interviews soon.\par \par \uc2 Here is the agenda for the day.\par \par \uc2 Morning: \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  is on a visit to promote the rise in the national living wage coming into effect today.\par \par \uc2 9am: \uc2 Andy Burnham\uc2 , the mayor of Greater Manchester, gives evidence to the Commons work and pensions committee on reforming jobcentres.\par \par \uc2 9.20am: \uc2 Kemi Badenoch\uc2  is interviewed on LBC.\par \par \uc2 Morning: \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  chairs cabinet\par \par \uc2 10am: Richard Hughes, chair of the \uc2 Office for Budget Responsibility\uc2 , and colleagues give evidence to the Commons Treasury committee about the spring statement.\par \par \uc2 11.30am: \uc2 David Lammy\uc2 , the foreign secretary, takes questions in the Commons.\par \par \uc2 11.30am: \uc2 Kemi Badenoch\uc2  and \uc2 Mel Stride\uc2 , the shadow chancellor, hold a press conference on \u8220\'20\'1CLabour\u8217\'20\'19s jobs tax\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Noon: Downing Street holds a lobby briefing.\par \par \uc2 Also, at some point today \uc2 Shabana Mahmood\uc2 , the justice secretary, is set to unveil the emergency bill she is introducing to block \uc2 the Sentencing Council\uc2  guidelines that she described as implementing \u8220\'20\'1Ctwo-tier justice\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 If you want to contact me, please post a message below the line or message me on social media. I can\u8217\'20\'19t read all the messages BTL, but if you put \u8220\'20\'1CAndrew\u8221\'20\'1D in a message aimed at me, I am more likely to see it because I search for posts containing that word.\par \par \uc2 If you want to flag something up urgently, it is best to use social media. You can reach me on Bluesky at @andrewsparrowgdn. \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2024/nov/13/the-guardian-no-longer-post-on-x-twitter-elon-musk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 given up posting from its official accounts on X}}}\uc2  but individual Guardian journalists are there, I still have my account, and if you message me there at @AndrewSparrow, I will see it and respond if necessary.\par \par \uc2 I find it very helpful when readers point out mistakes, even minor typos. No error is too small to correct. And I find your questions very interesting too. I can\u8217\'20\'19t promise to reply to them all, but I will try to reply to as many as I can, either BTL or sometimes in the blog.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.21am BST\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250401el410010a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
