\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 A Wisconsin state supreme court race will test whether  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  can still tip an election\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,059 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 March 2025\par 14:17 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Two years after the fall of Roe v Wade, it is no longer clear whether voters will show up for the singular issue\par \par \uc2 The United States on Tuesday will face its first major election of the Trump 2.0 era: a race for a single state supreme court seat in Wisconsin that could not only rewrite \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the state, but test whether the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights can still drive voters to the polls.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Wisconsin residents will decide whether to elect liberal Susan Crawford or conservative \uc2 Brad Schimel\uc2 , two county judges, to the state supreme court, which is poised to weigh in on two major \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates are trying to use the threat to the procedure to convince people to head to the polls, but, more than two years after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade, it is no longer clear whether \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can tip an election. Democratic candidates talked about outrage over Roe constantly during the 2024 election \u8211\'20\'13 and fell short, losing both the White House and the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Ben Wikler, leader of the Democratic party of Wisconsin, remains convinced that the issue can be Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 silver bullet.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the single issue that most motivates Democratic voters and persuades independent, moderate voters to cast a ballot for Susan Crawford and against \uc2 Brad Schimel\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D Wikler said. Although the candidates are nominally non-partisan, the Democratic party is backing Crawford, while \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has endorsed Schimel. If Schimel wins, conservatives will regain control of the state supreme court.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDemocrats are fired up and they\u8217\'20\'19re angry,\u8221\'20\'1D Wikler continued. \u8220\'20\'1CThey want a channel to create change at this moment, and they do not want to go back to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 After Roe collapsed in June 2022, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers across Wisconsin stopped offering the procedure out of fear that they could be prosecuted under an 1849 law declaring that \u8220\'20\'1Cany person, other than the mother, who intentionally destroys the life of the unborn child\u8221\'20\'1D is guilty of a felony. Under the law \u8211\'20\'13 which was first enacted only a year after Wisconsin became a state and before women had the right to vote \u8211\'20\'13 abortions are only permitted to save a patient\u8217\'20\'19s life. That kind of exception, doctors across the country have said, is so narrow as to be unworkable.\par \par \uc2 Wisconsin\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic attorney general, \uc2 Josh Kaul\uc2 , filed a lawsuit arguing that the 1849 ban only applies in instances of feticide, or when someone ends a pregnancy against a pregnant person\u8217\'20\'19s will. A court sided with Kaul, suspending the ban, but the state supreme court heard arguments in a case over the ban late last year. A ruling in that case is set to be released before Crawford or Schimel would join the state supreme court bench.\par \par \uc2 However, the court is also expected to hear arguments in a separate case \u8211\'20\'13 filed by \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 over whether the Wisconsin constitution protects \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. If it does, the 1849 ban could be struck down completely.\par \par \uc2 Schimel, who personally opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has previously {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://archive.is/bxKCD#selection-1694.0-1696.0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 called the 1849 ban \u8220\'20\'1Cvalid\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThere is not a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in our state constitution,\u8221\'20\'1D Schimel said on a campaign stop, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2025/01/27/us/politics/wisconsin-supreme-court-race.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to the New York Times}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThat will be a sham if they find that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Crawford has avoided speaking directly about the 1849 ban. She has, however, released multiple ads slamming Schimel for his stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Brad Schimel\uc2  wants to make sure women don\u8217\'20\'19t have the right to make their own healthcare decisions. If he wins, that right is gone,\u8221\'20\'1D Crawford said in a recent ad. \u8220\'20\'1CI trust women to make their own healthcare decisions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/nov/02/state-supreme-court-abortion-ballot"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fall of Roe turbocharged}}}\uc2  the public\u8217\'20\'19s attention on state supreme court elections, once a relatively sleepy corner of US politics, as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights catapulted to the top of many liberal voters\u8217\'20\'19 priorities. Without a federal guarantee of the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , lawsuits over the procedure are increasingly waged in state courts, and state supreme courts often have the final word on whether it remains legal.\par \par \uc2 Spending on such races has skyrocketed. In 2023, when another seat on the Wisconsin state supreme court was up for grabs, campaigns and outside groups spent an eye-popping $51m \u8211\'20\'13 at the time, a record amount for a state supreme court election.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s election has smashed that record. As of last week, more than $80m had been poured into the race. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , the richest person on the planet, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/mar/29/wisconsin-supreme-court-race-elon-musk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the groups he funds}}}\uc2  have spent more than $20m in support of Schimel; the billionaire \uc2 George Soros\uc2  has given $1m to the Democratic party of Wisconsin, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/analysis-opinion/wisconsin-supreme-court-race-breaks-spending-record-fueled-out-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to the Brennan Center for Justice}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s just an entirely new world when it comes to judicial elections,\u8221\'20\'1D said Douglas Keith, a senior counsel in the Brennan Center for Justice\u8217\'20\'19s Judiciary Program, who tracks supreme court races. \u8220\'20\'1CThese look so much more like a top-tier \uc2 US Senate\uc2  race than they do the judicial elections from even just a few years ago.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 One anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  political action committee, Women Speak Out Pac, has dropped more than $500,000 into the race, campaign finance records show. The Pac, which is partnered with the powerful anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organization SBA Pro-Life America, has over the last seven years received more than $20m in funding from the Republican megadonor Dick Uihlein and a Pac he uses to fund his pet causes, which include {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/uline-uihlein-election-denial"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 opposition to \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 , unions and taxes}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Parties\u8217\'20\'19 investment in these races has also increased \u8211\'20\'13 over the last few weeks, Wikler said, Wisconsin Democrats had reached \u8220\'20\'1Chundreds of thousands\u8221\'20\'1D of voters, more than doubling their record from 2023.\par \par \uc2 This single race has cost more than all state supreme court races in 2018 \u8211\'20\'13 a development that Keith calls \u8220\'20\'1Cdeeply troubling\u8221\'20\'1D for democracy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CVoters are not receiving reliable information from all this money. They\u8217\'20\'19re receiving a barrage of ads, many of which are misleading and do nothing to actually inform the electorate,\u8221\'20\'1D Keith said. The candidates have debated just once.\par \par \uc2 He added: \u8220\'20\'1CIt just makes it so hard for the public to view courts as anything different than the other branches, and to have any reason to trust the decisions that come out of the courts.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250331el3v001gv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
