\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Key court hearing as Alabama threatens prosecutions over  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  support\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 991 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 March 2025\par 15:07 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Experts say victory for state could give green light for other states to attack those who help women travel for procedure\par \par \uc2 A bellwether test of states\u8217\'20\'19 ability to prosecute people over {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 abortions}}}\uc2  that take place across state lines faces a critical hearing on Wednesday, when Alabama \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters square off against the state attorney general over his threats to prosecute groups that help women travel for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 In the months after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade in 2022, clearing the way for Alabama to ban virtually all abortions, Alabama attorney general \uc2 Steve Marshall\uc2  repeatedly suggested that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists who help people go out of state for abortions could be charged as participants in an illegal conspiracy. The Yellowhammer Fund, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund that helped people pay for the procedure, and the West Alabama Women\u8217\'20\'19s Center, a former \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic that pivoted to providing services like miscarriage management, joined with other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates to sue Marshall over his comments.\par \par \uc2 Now, experts worry that a victory for Alabama could serve as a green light for other states\u8217\'20\'19 efforts to attack people who want to end their pregnancies but live in states that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you go to Las Vegas to gamble, but your state doesn\u8217\'20\'19t permit it, you don\u8217\'20\'19t expect for your AG to suggest that anybody who helped you gamble in another state is going to be prosecuted, fined, and jailed,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rachel Rebouch\u233\'00\'E9, an expert in \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  law and the dean of \uc2 Temple University\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s law school.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a real encroachment on what we take for granted about how states treat each other \u8211\'20\'13 but also within the state, that the state will turn its law enforcement power against somebody who has done something that is not illegal.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Since Marshall\u8217\'20\'19s threats, the Yellowhammer Fund has stopped paying for people\u8217\'20\'19s legal, out-of-state abortions, while the West Alabama Women\u8217\'20\'19s Center is unable to help patients looking for out-of-state abortions, according to court documents. The plaintiffs in the case collectively receive about 95 questions each week from people looking for abortions outside Alabama.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe majority of our clients\u8217\'20\'19 patients are poor or low income. They are people who may depend on financial assistance and support in figuring out how to get the resources they need to pay for travel,\u8221\'20\'1D said Meagan Burrows, a senior staff attorney at the \uc2 ACLU\uc2 , which is representing West Alabama Women\u8217\'20\'19s Center (now known as WAWC Healthcare).\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAll of these patients are coming to our clients very distressed, very confused about the legal landscape and their legal options, and are rightly contacting local healthcare providers in Alabama that they trust, who they know have all of the information and resources at their fingertips by virtue of being providers of reproductive healthcare, and former \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care providers. And our clients have to turn them away.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The attorney general\u8217\'20\'19s office did not immediately respond to a request for comment. But in court filings, it doubled down on Marshall\u8217\'20\'19s claims. \u8220\'20\'1CAn elective \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  performed in Alabama would be a criminal offense; thus, a conspiracy formed in the state to have that same act performed outside the state is illegal,\u8221\'20\'1D one brief read.\par \par \uc2 The hearing on Wednesday, in federal court in Montgomery, will deal with requests from both sides for summary judgment, or to move forward without a full trial.\par \par \uc2 Despite the downfall of Roe, which unleashed a wave of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans across much of the US south and midwest, US abortions have increased in recent years. That rise, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/nov/27/post-roe-abortion-rate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights supporters say}}}\uc2 , is due in large part to travel, as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics on the coasts have performed a growing number of procedures on women fleeing states with bans.\par \par \uc2 In response, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have begun to test out various ways to attack out-of-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  travel, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/dec/27/speech-anti-abortion-movement"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 including by limiting talk of it}}}\uc2 . In Texas, an activist has drawn \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds, which help people travel out of state for abortions, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/sep/28/texas-lawyer-abortion-ban-procedure-patient-information"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 into litigation and asked them}}}\uc2  to turn over information about past abortions. Idaho and Tennessee have passed laws that ban \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  trafficking\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 which they define as transporting a minor for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without parental consent \u8211\'20\'13 as well as \u8220\'20\'1Crecruiting\u8221\'20\'1D minors for abortions. A court has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/dec/02/idaho-abortion-trafficking-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blocked the \u8220\'20\'1Crecruiting\u8221\'20\'1D provision}}}\uc2  in Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s law, citing first amendment concerns, while another court has paused the enforcement of Tennessee\u8217\'20\'19s entire law.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith criminal penalties for helpers, penalties for providers, what you see are anti-[\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] rights politicians attempting to stop anyone who is helping a pregnant person or pregnant people from seeking care,\u8221\'20\'1D Elisabeth Smith, the director of state policy and advocacy for the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , told the Guardian last year. \u8220\'20\'1CThe pregnant person, essentially, would be isolated and unable to seek the care that they want and need.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Abortions have also risen thanks to the emergence of blue-state \u8220\'20\'1Cshield laws\u8221\'20\'1D, which aim to protect providers who dispense medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills across state lines. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents have, in recent weeks, targeted those providers, too. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/31/new-york-louisiana-abortion-pill-charges"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Louisiana has indicted}}}\uc2  a New York doctor for allegedly dispensing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill, while {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/dec/13/texas-abortion-pills-lawsuit-ken-paxton"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas has filed a lawsuit}}}\uc2  against the same doctor.\par \par \uc2 Mary Ziegler, who studies the legal history of reproduction, sees all of these efforts as intertwined. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s part of a broader set of issues about when states can project their power across their borders,\u8221\'20\'1D Ziegler said.\par \par \uc2 Regardless of the outcome of Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s hearing, red states appear to already be on a warpath. \u8220\'20\'1CAttorneys general are just going full speed ahead, even when it comes to out-of-state defendants,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ziegler, a professor at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 , School of Law. \u8220\'20\'1CI think the gloves came off after the election was over.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A ruling in the requests for summary judgment is expected in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250305el35001jm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
