\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  pill manufacturer asks to join suit that could rewrite US  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  access\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 539 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 February 2025\par 22:34 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 GenBioPro seeks to defend access to mifepristone in suit brought by attorneys general of Idaho, Missouri and Kansas\par \par \uc2 The manufacturer of the generic version of a major \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill has asked to join a federal lawsuit over the pill\u8217\'20\'19s future, marking a new step forward in litigation that could rewrite US {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 The manufacturer, GenBioPro, filed court papers on Monday to become a defendant in the lawsuit over mifepristone, one of two drugs typically used in a US medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That lawsuit, which was brought by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republican}}}\uc2  attorneys general of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/idaho"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Idaho}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/missouri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Missouri}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/kansas"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kansas}}}\uc2 , seeks to reverse \uc2 FDA\uc2  regulations that dramatically expanded access to the pill, such as provisions that permit providers to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/mar/24/hawaii-mifepristone-abortion-pill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prescribe it through telehealth}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Medication abortions account for more than 60% of US abortions. Last year, tens of thousands of them were provided through telehealth, including to women living in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, according to #WeCount, a research project by the Society of Family Planning.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDespite the overwhelming majority of Americans \u8211\'20\'13 including constituents of these attorneys general \u8211\'20\'13 supporting the legal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , these politicians remain determined to severely restrict access to a critical drug that women across America depend on,\u8221\'20\'1D Skye Perryman, the president and CEO of Democracy Forward, said in a statement. Lawyers from Democracy Forward are serving on GenBioPro\u8217\'20\'19s legal team.\par \par \uc2 Perryman continued: \u8220\'20\'1CTheir baseless attacks not only jeopardize the availability of mifepristone, but also threaten the integrity of our nation\u8217\'20\'19s drug regulation system.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit is a continuation of a case that reached the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  last year. It was first brought by an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  coalition known as the Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine. In a 9-0 ruling, the justices found that the coalition {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/jun/13/supreme-court-abortion-pill-access"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lacked the legal standing to bring the case}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/article/2024/jun/13/supreme-court-abortion-pill-mifepristone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sent it back to a lower court}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 However, Judge Matthew Kacsmaryk of the northern district of Texas, ruled in January that the attorneys general from Idaho, Missouri and Kansas {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/17/abortion-pill-mifepristone-lawsuit-texas"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 could continue the case}}}\uc2  for now. Kacsmaryk, who was appointed by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2 , had in the earlier version of the case issued a court order that would yank mifepristone from the market entirely. That order was frozen by higher courts.\par \par \uc2 Under {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/joebiden"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Joe Biden}}}\uc2 , the \uc2 FDA\uc2  defended itself against the lawsuit and argued in favor of maintaining the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s current regulation of the drug. Under Trump, however, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is widely expected to drop out of the case.\par \par \uc2 Danco Laboratories, which manufactures mifepristone under the brand name Mifeprex, is already a defendant in the suit.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2023/04/01/health/abortion-pill-safety.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 More than 100 studies}}}\uc2 , conducted across decades and dozens of countries, have found that mifepristone and misoprostol, the second drug normally used in US abortions, are safe.\par \par \uc2 Other states, meanwhile, are taking aim at providers\u8217\'20\'19 ability to dispense \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills across state lines. Texas has filed a civil lawsuit against a New York doctor who allegedly prescribed pills to a woman in Texas, while Louisiana is pursuing a criminal case against the same doctor. New York, which has a shield law in place to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers who operate in other states, has declined to sign Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s extradition order.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250226el2q003s6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
