\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Prayer and prosecutions: the US \u8216\'20\'18hate group\u8217\'20\'19 waging war over Britain\u8217\'20\'19s  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  clinic buffer zones\b0\par\par\uc2 Shanti Das \par \uc2 1,842 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 February 2025\par 18:47 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigners cheer as JD Vance brands safe zones an attack on \u8216\'20\'18liberties of religious Britons\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Rachael Clarke remembers life before buffer zones. Almost every day, the head of staff at the UK\u8217\'20\'19s biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider would get emails from staff worried about protesters outside clinics \u8211\'20\'13 and women crying in the waiting room.\par \par \uc2 Some of the protesters had huge placards with graphic images of foetuses. Others held candlelit vigils and said prayers. One scattered baby clothes in the bushes. \u8220\'20\'1CWe had every\u173\'00\'ADthing from people telling women that having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was putting their baby in a meat grinder to people following nurses down the road in the dark telling them they were killing babies,\u8221\'20\'1D says Clarke.\par \par \uc2 Since {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/oct/11/buffer-zone-to-be-set-up-outside-bournemouth-abortion-clinic"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 buffer zones were rolled out}}}\uc2  nationally late last year \u8211\'20\'13 building on public space protection orders that were already in place outside some clinics \u8211\'20\'13 she says things have drastically improved.\par \par \uc2 Reports of alleged harassment outside \uc2 British Pregnancy Advisory Service\uc2  clinics have stopped almost completely. So when she heard \uc2 JD Vance\uc2 , the US vice-president, decrying buffer zone laws as an attack on the \u8220\'20\'1Cliberties of religious Britons\u8221\'20\'1D in a speech on Friday at the Munich Security Conference \u8211\'20\'13 and condemning the conviction of a man, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/feb/14/thought-and-cancelled-elections-how-do-jd-vances-europe-claims-stand-up"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Adam Smith-Connor}}}\uc2 , who he said had been targeted for \u8220\'20\'1Cjust silently praying on his own\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 she wasn\u8217\'20\'19t impressed. \u8220\'20\'1CYou can\u8217\'20\'19t see these things in isolation,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 Rather than being a one-off, Clarke sees the Smith-Connor case as part of a wider effort by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigners to test the new law to the limits \u8211\'20\'13 and shift the focus away from the true reason for buffer zones to a debate about freedom of speech.\par \par \uc2 Hers is a view shared by reproductive healthcare professionals, legal experts and campaigners who believe buffer zones \u8211\'20\'13 intended to protect service users and staff \u8211\'20\'13 are being targeted in an orchestrated campaign by conservative Christian groups that are fuelling the spread of misinformation and seeking to shift the terms of the debate.\par \par \uc2 At the centre of the efforts is the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jun/19/alliance-defending-freedom-lgbtq-rights-america"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom}}}\uc2  (\uc2 ADF\uc2 ), a prominent conservative Christian group which opposes gay marriage as well as wanting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be banned \u8211\'20\'13 and has been labelled an extreme rightwing \u8220\'20\'1Chate group\u8221\'20\'1D by critics including the \uc2 Southern Poverty Law Center\uc2  in the US.\par \par \uc2 The Observer has found that the group\u8217\'20\'19s UK branch has coordinated publicity and funded legal costs in a string of cases of alleged \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  buffer zone breaches. In that of Smith-Connor, who was convicted in October, it has written blog posts, launched a fundraising campaign and is paying for a legal appeal.\par \par \uc2 Using almost identical language to that used by Vance, it has circulated statements claiming Smith-Connor is the victim of a \u8220\'20\'1Cthought crime\u8221\'20\'1D. In reality, he had been prosecuted for breaching a public space protection order which had been put in place to protect staff and service users following issues with protests. The new buffer zone law that Vance referred to, which has since come into force, makes it illegal under the Public Order Act 2023 for anyone to do anything that intentionally or recklessly influences someone\u8217\'20\'19s decision to use \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, obstructs them, or causes harassment or distress, within 150 metres of a clinic. The law doesn\u8217\'20\'19t explicitly mention prayer, or silent prayer, but criminalises behaviour likely to intimidate staff or service users.\par \par \uc2 At the time of his arrest in November 2022, Smith-Connor had been partially standing behind a tree near an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in Bournemouth, which was protected by a public space protection order following anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activity. He had been praying, which he said was because he and his former girlfriend had once aborted a pregnancy. But he had also been asked repeatedly to move on, by a community officer who had spoken to him for an hour and 40 minutes. Ordering him to pay \u163\'00\'A39,000 costs, district judge Orla Austin said he had breached a public space protection order and that his actions had been \u8220\'20\'1Cdeliberate\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 After Vance\u8217\'20\'19s comments on Friday, the \uc2 ADF\uc2  celebrated online. CEO and president \uc2 Kristen Waggoner\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 who was in Europe this week in a visit that coincided with Vance\u8217\'20\'19s \u8211\'20\'13 posted on X: \u8220\'20\'1CVery grateful to Vice President @JDVance for highlighting Adam\u8217\'20\'19s unjust and illiberal conviction for silent prayer in the UK.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ADF\uc2  is also funding legal help for Isabel Vaughan-Spruce, the founder of the March for Life anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, who has been arrested twice, but not convicted over alleged buffer zone violations, and was paid \u163\'00\'A313,000 by West Midlands police.\par \par \uc2 A prosecutor had said the case had not met the full code test, which assesses whether prosecutions are in the public interest and if there is sufficient evidence. A West Midlands spokesperson said Vaughan-Spruce had made a civil claim for unlawful arrest, assault and a breach of human rights and it had settled the claim without any admission of liability.\par \par \uc2 This weekend, the \uc2 ADF\uc2  was sharing videos of Vaughan-Spruce outside an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, being asked to move on by an officer and declining.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ADF\uc2  also backed Father Sean Gough, a Catholic priest who faced charges claiming he intimidated service users near an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, which were later dropped for the same reason as Vaughan-Spruce\u8217\'20\'19s - and is supporting a fourth woman who is expected to appear in court next month over her alleged failure to pay a fixed penalty notice after she was accused of breaching a buffer zone.\par \par \uc2 The cases raise questions about the growing influence of US anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups in Britain. The Observer previously reported that the UK branch of the \uc2 ADF\uc2  had more than doubled its spending since 2020 and been appointed a stakeholder in a parliamentary group on religious freedoms in a role that grants it direct access to MPs.\par \par \uc2 In all the cases, at the centre of the \uc2 ADF\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s publicity is the idea that silent prayer is being criminalised \u8211\'20\'13 and people\u8217\'20\'19s right to freedom of religion is being eroded.\par \par \uc2 But Clarke says this is a distraction. \u8220\'20\'1CThe thing they try to push is that it\u8217\'20\'19s just \u8216\'20\'18silently praying\u8217\'20\'19: you might be walking down the road minding your business and a police officer leaps out. But that\u8217\'20\'19s not how it works. These campaigners seek out \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and they stand directly outside,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 Pam Lowe\uc2 , senior sociology lecturer at \uc2 Aston University\uc2 , who has researched the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, said: \u8220\'20\'1CFrom my research the intimidation comes from people standing there. Most people are not immersed in what is or isn\u8217\'20\'19t happening outside \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics. So all they see is someone they think is going to stop them. It doesn\u8217\'20\'19t matter what that person intends or doesn\u8217\'20\'19t intend to do, it\u8217\'20\'19s the presence rather than the action that\u8217\'20\'19s the problem. I\u8217\'20\'19ve seen people climb over walls, put their hood up or try to run past these people, because they don\u8217\'20\'19t know and have no idea what their intentions are.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She said the attempt to focus on \u8220\'20\'1Csilent prayer\u8221\'20\'1D and freedom of speech and religion appeared to be a \u8220\'20\'1Cdeliberate strategy by the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CThey are still free to pray and say what they want about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They are just asking them to move 100m down the street.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ADF\uc2  says it wants buffer zones to be abolished altogether. Lois McLatchie Miller, the ADF\u8217\'20\'19s UK spokesperson, said they did \u8220\'20\'1Cnot exist to protect women from harassment (as this, rightfully, was already illegal). Rather, they violate fundamental freedoms by criminalising people for peaceful, consensual conversations, and even prayers\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The group says it\u8217\'20\'19s proud to back the legal defences of Smith\u8211\'20\'13Connor, Vaughan-Spruce and others it says have been \u8220\'20\'1Ctargeted for their Christian faith\u8221\'20\'1D. It denies it is a hate group \u8211\'20\'13 a claim it says is part of a \u8220\'20\'1Csmear campaign\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 and says Smith-Connor\u8217\'20\'19s prosecution is \u8220\'20\'1Cone of the most extreme examples of censorship in a free society\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CThe UK is on show to the world for \u8220\'20\'1Cthought policing,\u8221\'20\'1D which is incompatible with democracy,\u8221\'20\'1D Miller said.\par \par \uc2 But from a legal standpoint, academics say the current law is robust \u8211\'20\'13 and strikes the delicate balance between the rights of campaigners, and the rights of the people it is intended to protect.\par \par \uc2 Prof George Letsas, from the faculty of law at UCL, said the buffer zones law \u8211\'20\'13 which came into effect in England and Wales in October - was already \u8220\'20\'1Cvery clear\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 and effectively balanced the human rights of all parties. \u8220\'20\'1CFrom a legal perspective it\u8217\'20\'19s clear that the right of women to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services prevails over the right to protest, including silent protest,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 He feels efforts by campaign groups to influence UK legal processes are \u8220\'20\'1Cvery problematic\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CBecause where the money spent is very substantial, and there are deep pockets, you worry about the outcomes and being one-sided. What\u8217\'20\'19s better is if these cases come to the courts more organically as opposed to in an orchestrated way where they\u8217\'20\'19re trying to test the limits of the law. The problem is these appear to be orchestrated, deliberate efforts.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Emily Ottley, lecturer in the law department at the \uc2 University of Winchester\uc2 , said challenges to arrests and convictions were to be expected, given the buffer zones law is new and has not been tested. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s important to not completely eradicate the views and feelings of the people who are protesting,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. But she said the law had been formulated in a way that balanced the human rights of both parties - adding that the true test would be in how it was treated by police, prosecutors and the courts in months to come.\par \par \uc2 For Dr Jonathan Lord, the medical director at \uc2 MSI Reproductive Choices\uc2  and co-chair of the \uc2 Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  taskforce, the \uc2 ADF\uc2  funding of legal cases shows how \u8220\'20\'1Cthe radical American right wing\u8221\'20\'1D had been \u8220\'20\'1Cempowered\u8221\'20\'1D and was attempting to push its \u8220\'20\'1Cextreme anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  views in the UK and around the world\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He said he hoped the focus of the conversation would return to the true reason why the law was brought in. \u8220\'20\'1CFor the terrified woman who knows she faces abuse if their partner discovers she is pregnant, it is irrelevant whether the protester is praying, thinking about their shopping list or has an empty mind. It is simply that they are there that is the problem,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt has nothing to do with freedom of speech or of banning prayer, but of preventing harm to women, girls and their families.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article was amended on 16 February 2025. Adam Smith-Connor was prosecuted for breaching a public space protection order, not \u8220\'20\'1Cunder the Public Order Act 2023\u8221\'20\'1D, which has since come into force, as an earlier version said. Comments by Emily Ottley regarding the buffer zones law were also clarified.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250219el2i002v3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
