\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s IVF order: a PR move that gives pronatalists cause for cheer\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,103 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 February 2025\par 18:40 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The order doesn\u8217\'20\'19t fulfill campaign pledges, but does cement the administration\u8217\'20\'19s reputation for promoting childbearing\par \par \uc2 During {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s}}}\uc2  2024 presidential campaign, as support for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  mounted, the president promised to force insurance companies to pay for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/ivf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in vitro fertilization}}}\uc2  (IVF) treatments. On Tuesday, Trump signed an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/fact-sheets/2025/02/fact-sheet-president-donald-j-trump-expands-access-to-in-vitro-fertilization-ivf/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 executive order}}}\uc2  that, the White House said, \u8220\'20\'1Cexpands access to in vitro fertilization\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPROMISES MADE. PROMISES KEPT,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/28/trump-press-secretary-karoline-leavitt"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Karoline Leavitt}}}\uc2 , the White House press secretary, posted in all-caps on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 /X.\par \par \uc2 Resolve, the country\u8217\'20\'19s leading infertility association, also applauded the order. \u8220\'20\'1CIn our decades of advocacy, we have never seen an administration prioritize IVF as an issue impacting millions of Americans,\u8221\'20\'1D Barbara Collura, its president and CEO, said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CFor this we are grateful.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The order, on its own, does not fulfill any campaign pledges. It does not change policy, much less make IVF free to people who want to grow their families. Instead, it is soliciting \u8220\'20\'1Cpolicy recommendations to protect IVF access and aggressively reduce out-of-pocket and health plan costs for such treatments\u8221\'20\'1D. It is, in many respects, a PR move \u8211\'20\'13 one that may alienate the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, which largely opposes IVF, but further cements the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump administration}}}\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s reputation for pronatalism.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe order recognizes the importance of family formation and that our nation\u8217\'20\'19s public policy must make it easier for loving and longing mothers and fathers to have children,\u8221\'20\'1D the order reads.\par \par \uc2 Although Americans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2024/05/13/americans-overwhelmingly-say-access-to-ivf-is-a-good-thing/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 widely support}}}\uc2  access to IVF, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement tends to oppose it on the grounds that it creates unused or discarded embryos (which they consider to be children). Predictably, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists revolted at news of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s Tuesday order. \u8220\'20\'1CIVF turns children into a product to be created, sold, and discarded \u8211\'20\'13 violating their basic human rights,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Lila Rose\uc2 , the leader of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group Live Action, posted on X. In another post, she added: \u8220\'20\'1CIVF is NOT pro-life.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But Trump is not as dependent on the support of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement as he once was. Although the movement\u8217\'20\'19s deep pockets and nationwide coordination were critical to his victory in the 2016 presidential election, Trump repeatedly wavered on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights throughout his 2024 run. Although he took credit for the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s 2022 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/jan/10/donald-trump-abortion-election-republicans-iowa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturning of Roe v Wade}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 which all three of his appointed justices supported \u8211\'20\'13 Trump also declined to support a nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban and, at one point, suggested that he would vote to restore \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Florida. (His {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/article/2024/aug/30/trump-ivf-anti-abortion-groups"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 campaign later walked back}}}\uc2  that promise.)\par \par \uc2 Clearly, Trump\u8217\'20\'19s lack of commitment to the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cause didn\u8217\'20\'19t hurt his campaign. If anything, it may have helped, given the mass outpouring of support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the years after Roe fell. Trump may have taken note: although anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists had hoped that he would curb access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills or use a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/aug/05/project-2025-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 19th-century anti-vice law}}}\uc2  to in effect ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide, Trump has done neither in the weeks since his return to the White House.\par \par \uc2 But if they are disappointed in what he hasn\u8217\'20\'19t done on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they can take heart in mounting expressions of support from inside the administration on pronatalism, or the belief that declining birthrates are a threat to society and having babies contributes to the greater good. In his first speech as vice-president, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/jd-vance"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 JD Vance}}}\uc2  told the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/24/trump-vance-march-for-life"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March for Life}}}\uc2  in January that he wants \u8220\'20\'1Cmore babies in the United States of America\u8221\'20\'1D. Billionaire {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/elon-musk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Elon Musk}}}\uc2 , whose so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cdepartment of government efficiency\u8221\'20\'1D (Doge) is now reportedly hacking away at the \uc2 US government\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s funding and staffing, has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://19thnews.org/2025/02/elon-musk-son-oval-office/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 long championed pronatalism}}}\uc2  and is believed to have more than a dozen children.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration\u8217\'20\'19s pronatalist devotion to \u8220\'20\'1Cfamily formation\u8221\'20\'1D, however, seems to extend only so far \u8211\'20\'13 or, perhaps, only to specific kinds of parents. Stressing that IVF should be used by \u8220\'20\'1Clonging mothers and fathers\u8221\'20\'1D leaves out LGBTQ+ families, who regularly rely on IVF. His administration\u8217\'20\'19s plan to cut the funding of the National Institutes of Health and Science, the planet\u8217\'20\'19s leading public funder of biomedical and behavioral research, threatens the future of efforts to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/15/trump-medical-research-funding-maternal-mortality"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 curb maternal mortality}}}\uc2 , which is disproportionately high among Black and Indigenous women. (Those planned cuts are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2025/02/12/trump-universities-funding-cuts-016328"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reportedly Doge\u8217\'20\'19s work}}}\uc2 .) And the lack of direct action on IVF in Trump\u8217\'20\'19s Tuesday order is striking, especially in the context of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s other executive orders, which have issued sweeping \u8211\'20\'13 and potentially illegal \u8211\'20\'13 mandates to eliminate birthright citizenship and roll back the rights of transgender people. The order does not, for example, mandate private employers cover IVF in the way that they are required to cover contraception.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn the wake of all these other executive orders that do such dramatic things so fast, this one still leaves you anticipating what\u8217\'20\'19s really the policy change gonna be,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sean Tipton, chief advocacy and policy officer for the \uc2 American Society for Reproductive Medicine\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CIf they start putting the pressure on private companies to include IVF that they put on them to dump DEI, maybe we see some change.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Still, Tipton added: \u8220\'20\'1CI would much rather have a policy that was thought out. I\u8217\'20\'19m hopeful that they\u8217\'20\'19re going to talk to the stakeholders, the experts, about what that policy should look like. And that\u8217\'20\'19s going to take a little time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 American Society for Reproductive Medicine\uc2  would like to see the federal government mandate that employers cover IVF treatment. That kind of requirement , however, is probably only possible through legislation \u8211\'20\'13 and that\u8217\'20\'19s unlikely to pass anytime soon. After an Alabama supreme court order last year declared that frozen embryos were \u8220\'20\'1Cextrauterine children\u8221\'20\'1D, leading IVF providers in the state to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2024/feb/24/us-infertility-patients-ivf-alabama-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 halt their work}}}\uc2 , Republicans in Congress {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jun/13/right-to-ivf-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shut down}}}\uc2  a bill that would enshrine a federal right to IVF. While Trump\u8217\'20\'19s alliance with the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement may be up in the air, many Republicans in Congress still depend on it to corral funding and votes.\par \par \uc2 Still, Tipton said, there are changes that Trump could make immediately to expand IVF access \u8211\'20\'13 like, for example, providing all federal employees coverage for fertility treatments. Although the healthcare program that covers most civilian federal employees currently offers options for IVF coverage, Tricare \u8211\'20\'13 the healthcare program for the US military \u8211\'20\'13 does not, according to Resolve.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COne big thing the president can do is role model for other employers,\u8221\'20\'1D Tipton said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe federal government ought to do that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250219el2j002pg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
