\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I won\u8217\'20\'19t regret this\u8217\'20\'19: young women turn to sterilization as Trump intensifies war on  \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 Alaina Demopoulos \par \uc2 1,862 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 February 2025\par 18:11 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Women under 30 are sharing information about permanent contraception \u8211\'20\'13 including lists of doctors who won\u8217\'20\'19t give them a hard time about getting the procedure\par \par \uc2 Madison Clark did not celebrate when {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  won the 2024 election. But one thought gave her solace: \u8220\'20\'1CAt least I don\u8217\'20\'19t have to worry about having a baby.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In September, Clark, a 24-year-old nursing student from Battle Creek, Michigan, underwent a bilateral salpingectomy, a sterilization procedure that removed her fallopian tubes, ensuring she will never get pregnant. Clark considers the procedure her fail-safe against any further {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/jan/26/us-anti-abortion-movement-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rollbacks of \b  \highlight22reproductive rights\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  that might occur under the new {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump administration}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Clark had always known she didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to have children. \u8220\'20\'1CI just don\u8217\'20\'19t personally see myself on that path,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. In 2022, when she learned she was pregnant, she got an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But that same year, the supreme court overturned Roe v Wade with its decision in Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, leading Clark to seriously consider {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hopkinsmedicine.org/health/treatment-tests-and-therapies/salpingectomy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 permanent contraception}}}\uc2 . She\u8217\'20\'19s not the only one.\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/06/permanent-contraception-abortion-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study}}}\uc2  published this month in the Health Affairs journal found that among young adults aged 19 to 26, tubal sterilization visits increased 70% after May 2022 \u8211\'20\'13 when the Dobbs decision leaked \u8211\'20\'13 in states likely to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Tubal ligation, known as getting one\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Ctubes tied\u8221\'20\'1D, involves cutting or blocking the fallopian tubes with clamps. A salpingectomy for permanent birth control, like Clark received, removes both fallopian tubes altogether.\par \par \uc2 The study also found that vasectomy procedures, a form of male birth control, increased 95% \u8211\'20\'13 but were still not as popular as tubal sterilizations. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/12/sterilizations-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 previous study}}}\uc2 , published last spring, found the number of tubal ligations among women ages 18 to 30 shot up after Dobbs, at a rate of increase double that of vasectomies.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPatients are scared of losing access to all kinds of reproductive care,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Sarah K Horvath, associate professor of obstetrics and gynecology at Penn State College of Medicine. \u8220\'20\'1CThere are people who had the idea of permanent contraception hovering somewhere on their to-do list, and now they\u8217\'20\'19re pushing it up to number one.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On TikTok, sterilization content has swelled. In one {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tiktok.com/t/ZP8FWD5ne/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 video}}}\uc2  with more than 73,000 likes, a creator posted footage of herself driving to an appointment and waiting on a hospital bed, with the caption: \u8220\'20\'1CGetting sterilized because y\u8217\'20\'19all couldn\u8217\'20\'19t act right in the voting booth.\u8221\'20\'1D One popular TikTok doctor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tiktok.com/@pagingdrfran/video/7462360820568182047?_r=1&_t=ZP-8tJeWIFimKt"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shared a \u8220\'20\'1Chow to\u8221\'20\'1D video}}}\uc2  about tubal sterilization a week after Trump\u8217\'20\'19s inauguration. \u8220\'20\'1CI go on Thursday to get this procedure done,\u8221\'20\'1D a user wrote in the comments. \u8220\'20\'1CI was so worried the current administration would prevent this option so we got it scheduled Asap.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Krysten Stein, an assistant professor at the University of Cincinnati Blue Ash College who studies doctor influencers on TikTok, said that conversations on social media about sterilization reflect gen Z\u8217\'20\'19s current political anxieties.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have this rapidly changing landscape around \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and seeking information about it or posting on social media feels like a way to take back control,\u8221\'20\'1D Stein said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt feels empowering to claim authority and to normalize these life choices.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In some states \u8211\'20\'13 including Michigan, where Clark lives \u8211\'20\'13 voters have enshrined \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections in state constitutions, and according to a recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/1576/abortion.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Gallup poll}}}\uc2 , a majority (54%) of Americans consider themselves \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-choice\u8221\'20\'1D. That has not stopped Trump from cozying up to an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/jan/23/abortion-homicide-bills"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 invigorated anti-\b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  movement}}}\uc2 . His first week in office saw him {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-trump-executive-order-pardon-817774b21d32a4edf6d39ee43cbc18f4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pardon activists}}}\uc2  who illegally blockaded the entrances to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/24/trump-order-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 limit funding}}}\uc2  for overseas groups that provide or advocate for abortions, and sign an executive order declaring gender begins \u8220\'20\'1Cat conception\u8221\'20\'1D, a tenet of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/23/trump-executive-order-fetal-personhood"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cfetal personhood\u8221\'20\'1D doctrine}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Robert F Kennedy Jr\uc2 , Trump\u8217\'20\'19s nominee for secretary of health and human services, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/29/rfk-jr-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  at his confirmation hearing on Thursday that he believed that \u8220\'20\'1Cevery \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a tragedy\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Trump {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/trump-contraception-birth-control-abortion-2024-8f73bb1b3a5864b24157f15eb272a3e6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 indicated}}}\uc2  that he was also open to regulating contraception last May \u8211\'20\'13 though the president later {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://truthsocial.com/@realDonaldTrump/posts/112480398507581671"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 posted}}}\uc2  on Truth Social that he \u8220\'20\'1CWILL NEVER ADVOCATE IMPOSING RESTRICTIONS ON BIRTH CONTROL\u8221\'20\'1D. This month, the supreme court, stacked with anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  justices, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.democracydocket.com/opinion/the-supreme-court-could-gut-access-to-birth-control-this-year/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 agreed}}}\uc2  to hear a case that threatens the Affordable Care Act\u8217\'20\'19s coverage of preventative care such as birth control and pre-exposure prophylaxis for HIV (PrEP).\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe news cycle was a huge, huge reason that I took sterilization seriously and decided to spring into action,\u8221\'20\'1D Clark said. \u8220\'20\'1CNow, it\u8217\'20\'19s a common conversation between my boyfriend and me: \u8216\'20\'18God, at least we don\u8217\'20\'19t have to worry about having to travel to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or having to leave the country for one, or even being forced to carry a child, which is the most scary option in all of this.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Nineteen states currently restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/jul/29/abortion-laws-bans-by-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bans}}}\uc2  for nearly all circumstances present in a dozen states. Even though Julia Wolf, 29, lives in Pennsylvania, a state with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, the fall of Roe planted the idea of sterilization in her mind. Like Clark, Wolf always knew she didn\u8217\'20\'19t want children. After she learned that the US has the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/article/2024/jun/05/maternal-mortality-rate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 highest maternal mortality rate}}}\uc2  among high-income countries, even the thought of becoming pregnant frightened her.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhether you agree with a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose or not, it\u8217\'20\'19s naive to think that other things aren\u8217\'20\'19t on the line, like contraceptives or sterilization,\u8221\'20\'1D said Wolf, who works in social media.\par \par \uc2 After the 2024 election results rolled in, Wolf scheduled her bilateral salpingectomy procedure as soon as she could \u8211\'20\'13 the first Monday in December. Her gynecologist told her that she performed three other sterilization procedures for women that same week.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t worry that I am going to regret this,\u8221\'20\'1D Wolf said. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m 100% confident in this decision, and I\u8217\'20\'19m just really glad I did it, especially because everything\u8217\'20\'19s moving so fast since Trump\u8217\'20\'19s been president.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This month, the congressman Andy Biggs of Arizona introduced a House resolution aiming to set a new standard for women\u8217\'20\'19s healthcare that \u8220\'20\'1Cshould also address the needs of men, families and communities as they relate to women\u8217\'20\'19s healthcare\u8221\'20\'1D. The line scared Wolf.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI just know for a fact that women will never have any input on men\u8217\'20\'19s health, and so for it to be the other way around is just crazy,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19d change doctors, get denied again\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Not every person who wants a sterilization will receive one \u8211\'20\'13 or else they might find the process to get one needlessly arduous.\par \par \uc2 Some doctors are hesitant to sterilize women under the age of 30, especially if they are unmarried or do not already have children. Though studies show that most women {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35115436/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 do not regret}}}\uc2  getting permanent contraception, those who do tend to be between the ages of 21 and 30 at the time of their procedure. (Sterilization is the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2492586/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most common form}}}\uc2  of contraception for married couples, with 700,000 performed on women annually, half of which are performed postpartum.)\par \par \uc2 The United States has an ugly history of forced sterilizations. In the 20th century, they were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pbs.org/independentlens/blog/unwanted-sterilization-and-eugenics-programs-in-the-united-states/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 performed}}}\uc2  under eugenics programs aimed at controlling \u8220\'20\'1Cundesirable\u8221\'20\'1D populations such as minorities, poor people, unmarried women or the mentally ill. Today, people on Medicaid are legally required to wait 30 days after signing a consent form to be sterilized \u8211\'20\'13 in theory, to prevent vulnerable people from being manipulated into undergoing forced sterilization as they were in the past. But {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.statnews.com/2024/03/26/medicaid-sterilization-waiting-period-harmful/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 activists say}}}\uc2  this practice is outdated and unfair. For one, there is no such restriction for people on private insurance. Thirty-day waiting periods also recall delaying tactics used in red states to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (ACOG){\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-statement/articles/2024/02/permanent-contraception-ethical-issues-and-considerations"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 says}}}\uc2  doctors should advise patients about \u8220\'20\'1Creversible alternatives\u8221\'20\'1D such as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://stanfordhealthcare.org/medical-treatments/v/vasectomy-reversal.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 vasectomy}}}\uc2  or other forms of birth control, and emphasize the permanence of tubal ligation or salpingectomy. But the \uc2 ACOG\uc2  also notes that doctors should \u8220\'20\'1Cavoid paternalism\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cnot [impose] thresholds based on age or parity or both for permanent contraception\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRespect for an individual patient\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive autonomy should be the primary concern guiding permanent contraception provision and policy,\u8221\'20\'1D the \uc2 ACOG\uc2  says.\par \par \uc2 In her own practice, Horvath, the OB-GYN, doesn\u8217\'20\'19t care so much why a person wants the procedure. She just wants them to be sure they\u8217\'20\'19re making the best decision. \u8220\'20\'1CThere are 18 different contraceptive methods, and no one is perfect,\u8221\'20\'1D Horvath said. \u8220\'20\'1CI ask that people think through all of the choices. If they\u8217\'20\'19re just really worried about getting through the next four years, have they thought about an IUD? That might leave the door open for you, if permanent sterilization doesn\u8217\'20\'19t feel like the right thing yet, or if you feel like you\u8217\'20\'19re really just having this reaction out of fear.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Some young people say they\u8217\'20\'19ve been turned away from the procedure for sexist reasons. Kasey Peterson, a 25-year-old property manager who lives in Oceanside, California, remembers playing with Barbies as a child and hearing her father say: \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19re going to make a really great mother one day.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI distinctly remember looking at him and going, \u8216\'20\'18Oh, I don\u8217\'20\'19t want kids,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Peterson said. As soon as she became a legal adult, she asked for a sterilization at every annual check-up. \u8220\'20\'1CEvery single doctor said, \u8216\'20\'18You\u8217\'20\'19re too young,\u8217\'20\'19 or laughed it off. I\u8217\'20\'19d change doctors, get denied again, change doctors, get denied again.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The doctors\u8217\'20\'19 reasoning for delaying the procedure seemed straight out of the 1950s: her \u8220\'20\'1Cfuture husband\u8221\'20\'1D might want children; she wasn\u8217\'20\'19t mature enough to make a permanent decision. They said to come back in a couple of years, and then maybe they\u8217\'20\'19d talk.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe overall tone was that I didn\u8217\'20\'19t know what I wanted as an individual,\u8221\'20\'1D Peterson said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was irritating. They were basically telling me that if I was pregnant right now, they would want me to keep the pregnancy, that I was emotionally mature enough for that, but I wasn\u8217\'20\'19t emotionally mature enough to decide that I don\u8217\'20\'19t want children.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Peterson\u8217\'20\'19s partner considered getting a vasectomy, which his doctors were more than happy to perform. \u8220\'20\'1CBut I decided I would rather do it for myself, for insurance reasons, but also because if I were to get assaulted by someone else, his vasectomy wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t cover that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Young people navigating the serpentine process of finding OB-GYNs willing to perform sterilizations come together in forums on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and Reddit, where they vent their frustrations, cheer each other on and share a \uc2 Google\uc2  doc of vetted doctors. Peterson eventually found her doctor through that list, and she now serves as an administrator for the Childfree and Sterile/Seeking Sterilization \uc2 Facebook\uc2  group.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen Roe v Wade was overturned, and when Trump got elected, our group got absolutely flooded,\u8221\'20\'1D Peterson said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s great that people are finding a community and a resource, but I hate how it happened. People are worried about protecting themselves in the future.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250130el1u001uq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
