\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 New York doctor indicted in Louisiana over alleged  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  pill prescription\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman and agencies \par \uc2 796 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 January 2025\par 21:17 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dr Margaret Carpenter faces felony charges in state that has had near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban since fall of Roe v Wade\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/new-york"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York}}}\uc2  doctor was indicted by a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/louisiana"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Louisiana}}}\uc2  grand jury on Friday for allegedly prescribing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill online in the deep south state, which has one of the strictest near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the country.\par \par \uc2 Grand jurors at the district court for the parish of West Baton Rouge issued an indictment against Dr Margaret Carpenter; her company, Nightingale Medical PC; and the mother of a minor who was allegedly provided with the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wwno.org/public-health/2025-01-31/louisiana-mother-ny-doctor-indicted-for-giving-minor-abortion-pills"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to a New Orleans public radio station}}}\uc2 . All three were charged with criminal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by means of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -inducing drugs, a felony.\par \par \uc2 The case appears to be the first instance of criminal charges against a doctor accused of sending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to another state \u8211\'20\'13 at least since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade in 2022 and opened the door for states to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Carpenter was also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/dec/13/texas-abortion-pills-lawsuit-ken-paxton"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sued by the Texas attorney general}}}\uc2  in December under similar allegations of sending pills to that state. That case did not involve criminal charges.\par \par \uc2 Carpenter did not immediately return a message, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Coalition of Telemedicine, an organization she co-founded, issued a statement condemning the Louisiana case as \u8220\'20\'1Cthe latest in a series of threats that jeopardizes women\u8217\'20\'19s access to reproductive healthcare throughout this country.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMake no mistake, since Roe v Wade was overturned, we\u8217\'20\'19ve witnessed a disturbing pattern of interference with women\u8217\'20\'19s rights,\u8221\'20\'1D the statement continued. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s no secret the United States has a history of violence and harassment against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, and this state-sponsored effort to prosecute a doctor providing safe and effective care should alarm everyone.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The indictment comes just months after Louisiana became the first state with a law to reclassify both mifepristone and misoprostol \u8211\'20\'13 pills commonly used in medical abortions \u8211\'20\'13 as \u8220\'20\'1Ccontrolled dangerous substances\u8221\'20\'1D. The drugs are still allowed, but medical personnel have to go through extra steps to access them.\par \par \uc2 Under the legislation, if someone knowingly possesses mifepristone or misoprostol without a valid prescription for any purpose, they could be fined up to $5,000 and sent to jail for one to five years. The law carves out protections for pregnant women who obtain the drug without a prescription to take on their own.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI have said it before and I will say it again: we will hold individuals accountable for breaking the law,\u8221\'20\'1D the Louisiana attorney general, Liz Murrill, a Republican, said in a statement on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Since the fall of Roe v Wade, Louisiana has had a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, without any exceptions for rape or incest. Under the law, physicians convicted of performing an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including one with pills, face up to 15 years in prison, $200,000 in fines and the loss of their medical license.\par \par \uc2 In an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wrkf.org/show/talk-louisiana/2025-01-31/billy-nungesser-tony-clayton-jill-craft"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 interview with Talk Louisiana}}}\uc2 , the district attorney Tony Clayton, a prosecutor on the case, said the minor\u8217\'20\'19s mother gave her the pill. \u8220\'20\'1CThe minor child was home alone, felt that she had to take the pill because of what her mother told her,\u8221\'20\'1D Clayton said.\par \par \uc2 Friday\u8217\'20\'19s indictment could be the first direct test of the ability of New York\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cshield law\u8221\'20\'1D to defend doctors from criminal prosecution. In the years after Roe fell, New York became one of a handful of blue states that passed such shield laws, which are intended to protect prescribers who use telehealth to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to patients in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis cowardly attempt out of Louisiana to weaponize the law against out-of-state providers is unjust and un-American,\u8221\'20\'1D Letitia James, the New York attorney general, who would enforce the shield law that could protect Carpenter from out-of-state prosecution, said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CWe will not allow bad actors to undermine our providers\u8217\'20\'19 ability to deliver critical care.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In the Talk Louisiana interview, Clayton acknowledged the New York shield law but said he thought he would \u8220\'20\'1Cget over\u8221\'20\'1D it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf that doctor were to travel to a state that has reciprocity with Louisiana, then that presents an issue for the doctor,\u8221\'20\'1D Clayton said. \u8220\'20\'1CI will seek a warrant and use whatever laws we have in place to effectively have a warrant.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Pills have become the most common means of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US, accounting for nearly two-thirds of them by 2023. They are also at the center of political and legal action over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In January, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/17/abortion-pill-mifepristone-lawsuit-texas"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one judge let three states}}}\uc2  continue to challenge federal government approvals for how one of the drugs usually involved can be prescribed.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250131el1v003e9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
