\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 As Trump returns, state lawmakers pursue bills that would treat  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  as homicide\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,154 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 January 2025\par 17:26 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Red state legislators have also introduced a slew of bills targeting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills and minors\u8217\'20\'19 access to the procedure\par \par \uc2 Legislators in at least four states have introduced bills this year that would change the legal definition of \u8220\'20\'1Chomicide\u8221\'20\'1D to include \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 proposals that pave the way for states to charge \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients with murder.\par \par \uc2 Pregnancy Justice, a group that tracks these kinds of efforts, says it has recorded more \u8220\'20\'1Chomicide\u8221\'20\'1D bills this year than ever before. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans have typically penalized providers, rather than patients.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat raises significant alarm \u8211\'20\'13 both that we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen more than we have in the past in a single legislative session already, and that they\u8217\'20\'19re not generating the level of outrage or attention or scrutiny that they have in years past,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dana Sussman, senior vice-president at Pregnancy Justice. \u8220\'20\'1CThe more of these kinds of bills that get introduced, people get numb to the idea of them, and they seem less and less radical.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 With Donald Trump returning to power in Washington DC, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters and opponents are waiting to see how the new president will handle one of the most divisive issues in US life. But red-state lawmakers have already filed a host of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  proposals over the last few weeks.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the \u8220\'20\'1Chomicide\u8221\'20\'1D bills, many of these bills target access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills \u8211\'20\'13 which account for more than 60% of US abortions \u8211\'20\'13 and seek to limit minors\u8217\'20\'19 ability to obtain abortions. In at least two states, Montana and Missouri, lawmakers are also seeking to implement legislation that would in effect roll back the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections guaranteed by recent ballot measures.\par \par \uc2 Last year, when elections loomed over legislators\u8217\'20\'19 heads, states passed fewer \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related laws than they had in the five years prior, according to the the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But this year, Kimya Forouzan, principal state policy adviser at the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , suspects that lawmakers will take Trump\u8217\'20\'19s victory in 2024 as a green light to pursue anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies more aggressively.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s generally going to be an emboldening of state legislators who are opposed to reproductive and sexual health and rights, given the new administration,\u8221\'20\'1D Forouzan said.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Homicide\u8217\'20\'19 and \u8216\'20\'18fetal personhood\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Bills that redefine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as \u8220\'20\'1Chomicide\u8221\'20\'1D strengthen a legal doctrine known as \u8220\'20\'1Cfetal personhood\u8221\'20\'1D, which seeks to grant embryos and fetuses legal rights and protections \u8211\'20\'13 sometimes at the expense of the women carrying them. If fully enacted, fetal personhood would not only ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but would rewrite entire arenas of US law, from traffic regulations to taxes. So far in 2025, lawmakers in at least six states have introduced bills to strengthen fetal personhood, while {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/23/trump-executive-order-fetal-personhood"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump has tucked fetal personhood language}}}\uc2  into one of his executive orders.\par \par \uc2 The \u8220\'20\'1Chomicide\u8221\'20\'1D bills have been introduced in Indiana, North Dakota, Oklahoma and South Carolina. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://webserver1.lsb.state.ok.us/cf_pdf/2025-26%20INT/SB/SB456%20INT.PDF"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Oklahoma bill}}}\uc2  not only explicitly strips away protections that keep \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients from being prosecuted, but also separately prohibits anything that may \u8220\'20\'1Cdirect, advise, encourage or solicit\u8221\'20\'1D an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That language, Pregnancy Justice worries, could have implications for free speech rights. It may be used to attack \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds and supporters sharing information with people in need.\par \par \uc2 The Oklahoma bill was co-authored by the Republican state senators Dusty Deevers and Warren Hamilton, who both belong to a far-right movement known as \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/dec/14/abortion-abolitionist-oklahoma-fringe-victory-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  abolitionism}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D. While the mainstream \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-life\u8221\'20\'1D movement shies away from the idea of punishing women for having abortions, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  abolitionists believe that if \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is murder, then \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients should be treated like murderers and punished accordingly.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think we\u8217\'20\'19re going to start to see those exceptions for pregnant people start to be repealed, so that pregnant people can also be criminalized under states\u8217\'20\'19 total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans,\u8221\'20\'1D said Elisabeth Smith, director of state policy and advocacy at the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CAll of this, I think, for most people, feels pretty hypothetical \u8211\'20\'13 unless, of course, they or a loved one or a friend are living in a banned state and has needed access to care. But it\u8217\'20\'19s critical to say anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  folks have made their ultimate goals clear.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills\par \par \uc2 In the years since Roe fell, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have lobbed attack after attack on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, or medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which are increasingly facilitated by the mail. In spring 2024, more than 19,000 abortions were provided each month through telehealth appointments, which rely on the mail to deliver the pills, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://societyfp.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/WeCount-Report-8-June-2024-data.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to #WeCount}}}\uc2 , a research project by the Society of Family Planning.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMailed medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is an existential threat to the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rachel Rebouch\u233\'00\'E9, the dean of \uc2 Temple University\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s law school. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s hard to police.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Red states, however, are going to try. Indiana, which bans virtually all abortions, has introduced {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://iga.in.gov/pdf-documents/124/2025/senate/bills/SB0245/SB0245.01.INTR.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 three}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://iga.in.gov/legislative/2025/bills/senate/245/details"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 separate}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://iga.in.gov/legislative/2025/bills/senate/171/details"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bills}}}\uc2  that would ban people from sending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to individuals in the state. One of those bills also outlaws the possession of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 Over in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://webserver1.lsb.state.ok.us/cf_pdf/2025-26%20INT/SB/SB989%20INT.PDF"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Oklahoma}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.capitol.tn.gov/Bills/114/Bill/HB0026.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tennessee}}}\uc2 , which ban almost all abortions, lawmakers have introduced similar bills that would also ban people from mailing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills \u8211\'20\'13 and, in the case of the Tennessee bill, hold them liable for $5m if those pills were used in an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 One of the Indiana bills would also reclassify mifepristone and misoprostol, the drugs typically used in US abortions, as controlled substances. That bill mirrors a similar proposal in Texas, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned and where {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/14/texas-anti-abortion-bills-filed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lawmakers have also introduced}}}\uc2  a bevy of bills to stop the flow of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. Both efforts also follow in the footsteps of Louisiana, which last year became the first state in the country to categorize mifepristone and misoprostol as controlled substances. That move, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/oct/01/louisiana-abortion-pills"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 doctors warned}}}\uc2 , could endanger women\u8217\'20\'19s lives, since the drugs can treat postpartum hemorrhage and other conditions.\par \par \uc2 Limits on minors\u8217\'20\'19 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access\par \par \uc2 In 2023, Idaho became the first state in the country to ban people from taking minors across state lines for abortions without parental consent \u8211\'20\'13 a practice lawmakers dubbed \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  trafficking\u8221\'20\'1D. Lawmakers in Mississippi, Missouri and New Hampshire are taking up Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s mantle with similar legislation that restricts people from helping minors get abortions through pills or by helping them travel to a state where the procedure is legal. (Mississippi bans virtually all abortions, but it is currently permitted in New Hampshire and Missouri.)\par \par \uc2 Another bill in South Carolina \u8211\'20\'13 which permits \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up until six weeks of pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 proposes eliminating a legal process known as judicial bypass. For decades, minors have used this process to ask courts to grant them the right to undergo the procedure without involving their parents.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters warn that restrictions on minors\u8217\'20\'19 access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  typically foreshadow wider restrictions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEvery ban that we have seen on adult access to care started with a ban on youth access to care,\u8221\'20\'1D Smith {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/dec/27/speech-anti-abortion-movement"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said last month}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250123el1n002p9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
