\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Democratic states train non-doctors on providing abortions to expand US access\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,149 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 January 2025\par 12:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 From Washington to Connecticut, pharmacists and healthcare workers pioneer efforts to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  barriers\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democratic}}}\uc2  states across the country are embarking on a pioneering effort to increase access to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  by teaching people who are not doctors to offer and perform the procedure.\par \par \uc2 In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/washington-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Washington}}}\uc2  state, a first-of-its-kind pilot program called the Pharmacist \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access Project announced this week that it trained 10 pharmacists to prescribe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills; so far, they have prescribed abortions to 43 people.\par \par \uc2 With Roe v Wade gone and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  now all but eliminated in a dozen or so states, the project is the latest attempt to expand access to the procedure in the parts of the country that still allow it. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cga.ct.gov/asp/cgabillstatus/cgabillstatus.asp?selBillType=Bill&which_year=2022&bill_num=5414"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Connecticut}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/10/delaware-access-abortion-care"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Delaware}}}\uc2  have in recent years passed legislation to permit physician assistants, midwives and some nurses to perform abortions, while Oregon, Maryland and Illinois are now devoting millions of state dollars to programs that train similar professions in the procedure.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEven in Washington state, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal, people are facing barriers to accessing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care \u8211\'20\'13 especially people who are struggling to make ends meet, who live in rural areas or don\u8217\'20\'19t have easy access to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care,\u8221\'20\'1D said Beth Rivin, the Pharmacist \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access Project\u8217\'20\'19s managing director and president of Uplift International, which partnered with the online pharmacy Honeybee Health to dispense the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. \u8220\'20\'1CThis expands \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access by bringing a new profession into \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provision.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Of the states that still allow abortions, 14 say that only physicians may perform the procedure, according to a tally by the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , which tracks \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. But in the years since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe}}}\uc2  in the 2022 decision Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, physicians \u8211\'20\'13 already in short supply in the US \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/article/2024/jun/24/abortion-training-doctors-ban-roe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have struggled to get training}}}\uc2  in how to provide abortions.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters have long said that non-physicians should be permitted to provide abortions earlier on in pregnancy if they involve pills or simpler procedures. The \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 , the preeminent membership organization for OB-GYNs in the US, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.acog.org/advocacy/abortion-is-essential/trending-issues/issue-brief-advanced-practice-clinicians-and-abortion-care-provision"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 supports}}}\uc2  allowing certified nurse-midwives, nurse practitioners and physician assistants to perform abortions, since studies have found no \u8220\'20\'1Csignificant difference\u8221\'20\'1D in the complication rates of medication abortions performed by doctors compared to other trained clinicians.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLet\u8217\'20\'19s free up providers who can do the complex cases to do complex cases,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kylea Laina Liese, assistant professor at the Department of Human Development Nursing Science at the \uc2 University of Illinois\uc2 . In 2024, the University of Illinois College of Nursing became one of three organizations to receive $2m from the state\u8217\'20\'19s public health department to expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training \u8211\'20\'13 money that Liese is now using to train the first cohort of physicians and non-physicians.\par \par \uc2 Midwives, nurse practitioners and physician assistants, Liese said, \u8220\'20\'1Care much more likely to represent the communities that they work in. That\u8217\'20\'19s something that\u8217\'20\'19s really missing in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. Being able to have Bipoc communities get their reproductive and sexual health services from providers that look like them and that they trust \u8211\'20\'13 it lowers those burdens.\u8221\'20\'1D More than 60% of all practicing US physicians {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aamc.org/media/63371/download?attachment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 identify as white}}}\uc2 , while far smaller fractions identify as Black, Indigenous or people of color.\par \par \uc2 In addition to increasing access, advocates say, these programs can help destigmatize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by moving it out of the silo of specialized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and clinics. In Maryland, where the state health department has awarded more than $10m to the University of Maryland to train new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, the program is also looking to teach emergency room doctors and pediatricians \u8211\'20\'13 the kind of specialties that don\u8217\'20\'19t typically offer \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy dream for all primary care is: you see someone has a positive pregnancy test, you tell them they have a positive pregnancy test, and then you\u8217\'20\'19re able to offer them any option that they choose,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jessica Lee, an associate professor of medicine at the University of Maryland School of Medicine who is helping run the program.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Don Downing, co-director of the Pharmacist \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access Project and a professor emeritus of pharmacy at the \uc2 University of Washington\uc2 , was surprised by how many patients and pharmacists were interested in the project.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI really thought, about two or three years ago, that we might have difficulty finding pharmacists who are willing to prescribe a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D Downing said. \u8220\'20\'1CWell, it turns out the pharmacists were as upset as I was with the Dobbs decision, and we got our 10 pharmacists \u8211\'20\'13 we wanted to keep it small \u8211\'20\'13 in very quick order.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Pharmacists\u8217\'20\'19 ability to prescribe medication varies from state to state. In Washington state, the Pharmacist \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access Project takes advantage of decades-old law, which authorizes pharmacists to prescribe drugs. Recent changes in the Food and Drug Administration\u8217\'20\'19s regulation of the common \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone also expanded the types of providers who can prescribe and dispense the drug to include certified non-physicians.\par \par \uc2 But those changes could prove to be short lived. Shortly after Roe fell, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists filed a lawsuit asking a court to roll back the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s 2000 approval of mifepristone. That case ultimately reached the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  last year, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/jun/13/supreme-court-abortion-pill-access#:~:text=US%20supreme%20court%20unanimously%20upholds,US%20supreme%20court%20%7C%20The%20Guardian"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unanimously ruled}}}\uc2  to preserve access to mifepristone \u8211\'20\'13 but did so on technical grounds, leaving the door open for further attacks on the drug. That lawsuit is now continuing in a lower court. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills now account for roughly two-thirds of all US abortions.\par \par \uc2 There is also the prospect of narrower rollbacks. The incoming {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump administration}}}\uc2  could {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/nov/07/abortion-donald-trump-return-to-white-house"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reverse more recent changes}}}\uc2  to mifepristone\u8217\'20\'19s regulation \u8211\'20\'13 including changes that have enabled pharmacists to prescribe the drug.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s pretty frightening,\u8221\'20\'1D Downing said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19d be like saying pharmacists can\u8217\'20\'19t provide flu shots anymore or Covid shots.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 If those rollbacks happen, Liese and Lee said that their states\u8217\'20\'19 respective programs would focus on teaching providers how to offer surgical abortions as well as medication abortions that only use the drug misoprostol (which is safe but can lead to more complications).\par \par \uc2 Whatever happens, Rivin hopes that other blue states will try to replicate the Pharmacist \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access Project. In New York \u8211\'20\'13 which already permits some non-doctors, such as physician assistants and nurse practitioners, to perform abortions \u8211\'20\'13 lawmakers introduced a bill that would effectively allow pharmacists to administer \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. That bill stalled, but a similar measure has already been introduced this year. Legislators have also introduced a bill to create a state training program for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur model is definitely a model that those states might be interested in. It\u8217\'20\'19s more important than ever in 2025 and for the next few years,\u8221\'20\'1D Rivin said. \u8220\'20\'1CSo we\u8217\'20\'19re encouraging leaders in those states to get in touch with us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250113el1d001mf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
