\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Permanent contraception surged after Roe v Wade overturned, study finds\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 639 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 January 2025\par 22:39 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Young adults living in states likely to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  obtained tubal sterilizations and vasectomies in months after ruling\par \par \uc2 In the months after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade, permanent contraception in the form of tubal sterilizations and vasectomies surged among young adults living in states likely to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , new research released on Monday found.\par \par \uc2 Compared to May 2022, when the opinion overturning Roe leaked, August 2022 saw 95% more vasectomies and 70% more tubal sterilizations performed on people between the ages of 19 and 26, according to the study, which was conducted by researchers at the \uc2 George Washington University\uc2 , the \uc2 University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill\uc2  and the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In addition to analyzing data about medical visits for permanent contraception before and after the opinion\u8217\'20\'19s leak, the researchers also examined survey responses from more than 600 people between the ages of 14 and 24 who were asked about the fall of Roe.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt has made me want to be sterilized more,\u8221\'20\'1D said one 24-year-old female survey respondent from the US south. \u8220\'20\'1CThe pill isn\u8217\'20\'19t 100% effective and I\u8217\'20\'19m afraid of losing access to it, and I do not want children in the future and would much rather be sterilized. I\u8217\'20\'19m afraid of getting pregnant and not being able to make decisions for myself.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Published in the journal Health Affairs, the study also found that, overall, tubal sterilizations \u8211\'20\'13 which surgically alter women\u8217\'20\'19s fallopian tubes and are colloquially known as \u8220\'20\'1Cgetting tubes tied\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 were more popular than vasectomies. There were about seven more tubal sterilizations performed per state a month in the second half of 2022, compared to roughly three more vasectomies a month per state.\par \par \uc2 That popularity probably reflects the longstanding expectation that women shoulder the burden of contraception. But Julia Strasser, the study\u8217\'20\'19s lead author, suspects that there may be other reasons, too.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy guess is that it\u8217\'20\'19s because people, who may be pursuing these methods because they fear that they will no longer have access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, may feel like they want to be the ones who are protecting their own sort of bodily autonomy versus relying on a partner,\u8221\'20\'1D said Strasser, who is also the director of the Jacobs Institute of Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health at the George Washington University\u8217\'20\'19s Milken Institute School of Public Health.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s study is the only latest piece of evidence indicating that more people sought permanent contraception in the wake of Roe\u8217\'20\'19s collapse. Last year, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/12/sterilizations-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 another study also discovered}}}\uc2  that the rates of people under 30 who underwent tubal ligations and vasectomies increased after Roe fell. That study, however, did not break down its findings by state.\par \par \uc2 To analyze patients\u8217\'20\'19 interest in permanent contraception, Strasser and her co-authors relied on data from the health information company IQVIA, which broke down patient visits for permanent contraception in 2021 and 2022. Rather than examine states that enacted post-Roe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, the researchers split states into those that were thought likely to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  versus those likely to protect it. Fear over a potential ban, they reasoned, could make someone want permanent contraception.\par \par \uc2 The study may in fact be underestimating the post-Roe demand for permanent contraception among young adults, Strasser said, since it focused on individuals who actually underwent tubal sterilizations and vasectomies. Even when they are sure they do not want children, young people often struggle to find medical providers who will perform those services on them.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t want them to be seeking them out of fear or concerns about their safety,\u8221\'20\'1D Strasser said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut also, I think it\u8217\'20\'19s important to recognize that young people, young adults, have autonomy over their contraceptive and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  choices.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250106el16003jv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
