\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Is it safe to have a child? Americans rethink family planning ahead of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s return\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,324 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 November 2024\par 13:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Some in the US are reconsidering children, with fears over reproductive healthcare and the climate crisis front of mind\par \par \uc2 Chris Peterson wasn\u8217\'20\'19t surprised that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump }}}\uc2  won the 2024 presidential election. But he was surprised by how quickly he and his wife started asking one another: should we try to have another baby before a possible nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban takes effect? Or should we give up on having a second child?\par \par \uc2 Peterson and his wife, who live in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/northcarolina"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 North Carolina }}}\uc2 , are thousands of dollars in debt because their first child needed to spend weeks in the hospital after being born prematurely. They had wanted to pay off that debt and wait a few years before having a second baby. But now, \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  are again in the balance \u8211\'20\'13 Trump has said he would veto a nationwide {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  ban, but his {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/nov/17/anti-abortion-groups-trump-2025"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 allies are emboldened }}}\uc2  to push through more restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Peterson is terrified of what is to come, and that his wife might not be able to get the medical care she needs if they decide to conceive again.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe should be happy thinking about expanding our family,\u8221\'20\'1D said Peterson, who is, like his wife, in his late 30s. \u8220\'20\'1CWe shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be worried that we\u8217\'20\'19re going to have medical complications and I might end up being a single father.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Peterson is not the only American who, in the weeks after the US election, is rethinking plans around having children. On 6 November, the number of people booking vasectomy appointments at \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  health centers spiked by 1,200%, IUD appointments by more than 760% and birth control implant appointments by 350%, according to a statement provided to the Guardian by \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 . Traffic to \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s webpages on tubal ligation, vasectomies and IUDs has also surged by more than 1,000% for each.\par \par \uc2 After the election, the Guardian heard from dozens of people in the US reconsidering whether to have children. Most pointed to fears over the future of reproductive healthcare, the economy and the climate in explaining their concerns.\par \par \uc2 Since Trump won, I\u8217\'20\'19ve decided not to have more children and have destroyed my remaining embryos just in case the government gets any crazy ideas about them\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI hesitate to bring more children into a world with an uncertain ecological future, assuming that the incoming administration pulls out of the Paris climate accord and ceases to support green energy transition,\u8221\'20\'1D a 34-year-old Minnesota mother of one wrote to the Guardian in response to a callout inviting readers to share their thoughts about post-election family planning. Trump pulled the US out of the historic agreement during his first administration; doing so again \u8211\'20\'13 which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2024/11/07/climate/trump-climate-cop-negotiations.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump has promised to do }}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 could \u8220\'20\'1Ccripple\u8221\'20\'1D the it, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2024/nov/01/a-trump-presidency-could-cripple-the-paris-climate-agreement-warns-un-chief-antonio-guterres"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UN secretary general }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have two children and I have desperately wanted a third \u8211\'20\'13 but now I am fearful of being able to get adequate care if I get pregnant,\u8221\'20\'1D wrote another woman who lives in Louisiana. \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t risk leaving my two children behind if [I] die because I can\u8217\'20\'19t get adequate care here. It feels like a dystopian novel, and yet here we are.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 These worries are not necessarily new. In 2023, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/social-trends/2024/07/25/the-experiences-of-u-s-adults-who-dont-have-children/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pew Research Center survey }}}\uc2  found that 47% of 18- to 49-year-old US adults say they are unlikely to ever have kids \u8211\'20\'13 a steep jump from 2018, when 37% said the same. Of the people who are unlikely to have kids, 38% said \u8220\'20\'1Cconcerns about the state of the world\u8221\'20\'1D were a major part of their decision-making. Roughly a quarter pointed to fears about the environment.\par \par \uc2 Working in disaster relief, Catherine regularly sees the effects of the climate crisis up close. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m in Washington DC right now and flowers are blooming. It\u8217\'20\'19s November. This should not be happening,\u8221\'20\'1D she said in an interview. \u8220\'20\'1CWhile I have always wanted kids, that choice has become tinged with a level of despair and anger that I didn\u8217\'20\'19t have two years ago.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She continued: \u8220\'20\'1CWhy would I bring a child into this world that is dying?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, Catherine got a copper IUD, which can block pregnancy for more than a decade.\par \par \uc2 Like developed countries around the world, the United States is in the midst of a fertility slump. In 2023, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/pressroom/nchs_press_releases/2024/20240525.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US fertility rate }}}\uc2  fell by 3% and reached a historic low.\par \par \uc2 But this decline is not evenly distributed across the political spectrum. After Trump won the presidency in 2016, births in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://today.ucsd.edu/story/the-trump-baby-bump-among-republicans-after-the-2016-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republican-leaning counties }}}\uc2  rose sharply compared to those that leaned Democratic. Today, Democrats are likelier than Republicans to be childfree \u8211\'20\'13 a trend that, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/business/2024/08/02/data-childless-cat-ladies/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Washington Post }}}\uc2  has hypothesized, is likely also related to the rightward drift of big-family white Protestants.\par \par \uc2 That the outcome of the 2024 election has spurred such fear and hesitation around having children is apt \u8211\'20\'13 not only are US political parties on diverging paths when it comes to babies, but the election itself was in many ways a referendum on families and fertility. While \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  made support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights a key plank in her platform, \uc2 Donald Trump{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.c-span.org/video/?c5060348/user-clip-trump-baby-boom"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 promised }}}\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cbaby booms\u8221\'20\'1D and pledged to give people \u8220\'20\'1Cbaby bonuses\u8221\'20\'1D. Trump\u8217\'20\'19s vice-president-elect, JD Vance, has built his political brand on pronatalism, a movement that urges people to have babies to benefit the greater good. Vance has a track record of deriding \u8220\'20\'1Cchildless cat ladies\u8221\'20\'1D and raising the alarm about the US fertility rate.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe want more babies because children are good,\u8221\'20\'1D Vance once said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd we believe children are good, because we are not sociopaths.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 M, a Texan mother of three who asked to go by her first initial because she feels stigmatized for voting for Trump, hopes that Trump\u8217\'20\'19s victory will improve the economy to the point that she and her husband can afford to have a fourth child.\par \par \uc2 We want to be able to dream of having a family the way we want to, on our terms\par \par \uc2 Ruth\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI still have a child in childcare now \u8211\'20\'13 like daycare \u8211\'20\'13 and just seeing those costs rise year after year since 2020, it\u8217\'20\'19s been really hard for our family to consider having another baby,\u8221\'20\'1D M said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe possibility of that being alleviated through better economic policy or even just those costs being taken away somewhere else \u8211\'20\'13 whether it\u8217\'20\'19s groceries or utilities to whatever it is \u8211\'20\'13 that really makes it possible for us to consider having another kid.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 M, who opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , feels confident that she would be able to get adequate care if she had a miscarriage. (Since Roe v Wade fell, at least {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/nov/25/texas-porsha-ngumezi-miscarriage-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 three women have died in Texas }}}\uc2  after doctors reportedly delayed treating them for miscarriages or gave them inadequate miscarriage care.) The climate crisis worries M less than making sure her kids have access to clean water and healthy, chemical-free food.\par \par \uc2 Not everyone reconsidering having kids has totally ruled it out. N, a 26-year-old from New York, is for now only delaying her plans to have kids until after Trump leaves office. (She asked to go by her first initial because she previously had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .) Ruth, who has a newborn at home and is married to an undocumented person, fears \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and her husband being deported \u8211\'20\'13 but still wants to keep the conversation about having a second kid alive.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe want to be able to dream of having a family the way we want to, on our terms,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ruth, who lives in Florida and asked to be identified by her middle name due to her husband\u8217\'20\'19s immigration status. \u8220\'20\'1CMy husband being an immigrant \u8211\'20\'13 we feel that it shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t foreclose our options to build a family. We have just as much of a right to build a family on our terms as anyone else.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241130ekbu001jl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
