\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 I thought Britain was worlds away from Trump\u8217\'20\'19s America \u8211\'20\'13 until I needed to get an  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 Anonymous \par \uc2 1,220 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 November 2024\par 12:35 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 I quickly learned that the decision to terminate a pregnancy wasn\u8217\'20\'19t purely a matter of 'my body, my choice\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Roughly 36 hours after I first heard about the horrifying Maga taunt \u8220\'20\'1Cyour body, my choice\u8221\'20\'1D, I learned that I was pregnant, despite having a contraceptive coil. My relief that I lived in the UK, not the US \u8211\'20\'13 where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is rapidly becoming illegal or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/jul/29/abortion-laws-bans-by-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 inaccessible at best }}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 was profound. Yet I realised that I had no idea how to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , having complacently assumed that it would always be available if I needed it. Some fraught Googling led me to the \uc2 British Pregnancy Advisory Service\uc2 . A couple of days later, I had my first appointment and very quickly learned that it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t purely \u8220\'20\'1Cmy choice\u8221\'20\'1D, even in Britain.\par \par \uc2 Of all the words you don\u8217\'20\'19t want to hear by surprise, \u8220\'20\'1Ctransvaginal\u8221\'20\'1D is up there. I thought the scan to determine how pregnant I was would be the kind where a technician slathers goop on your stomach. I wasn\u8217\'20\'19t told until I arrived that it would be internal, because of the assumed early gestation. A second surprise: the coil was gone, most likely sucked out by my period cup. Later that day, I had a phone consultation. The nurse told me two doctors would have to sign off on the termination and asked me to justify why my life would be negatively affected if I were forced to continue with the pregnancy. Horrified, I said I should just be able to say: I don\u8217\'20\'19t want to. She was extremely kind and agreed, but said this was a legal requirement under the \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Act.\par \par \uc2 I told her I lived hundreds of miles from my partner. We hadn\u8217\'20\'19t been together very long and were united on this. I lived in a one-bedroom flat. I could barely afford my own life. My career would suffer. The presence \u8211\'20\'13 or so I thought \u8211\'20\'13 of a coil should show that I had been actively warding against pregnancy. What more did she want? I am bullish in the face of authority I disagree with, but felt furious for any less headstrong person seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 already grappling with guilt and overwhelmed at dealing with the medical establishment \u8211\'20\'13 who might doubt their own needs when confronted in this way.\par \par \uc2 Accessing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is like a full-time job, one that sent me to three clinics across north, south and deep west London in order to resolve the situation as soon as possible. If I had waited for a local appointment \u8211\'20\'13 or if a restrictive job or childcare prevented me from travelling \u8211\'20\'13 it would have been another two weeks of increasing nausea, exhaustion and soreness. Termination was not an emotional decision for me and my partner, but the process was beyond draining, even with friends\u8217\'20\'19 loving support. So was dealing with well-meaning people who projected sadness on to the situation. I asked them to talk to me as if I were having a wisdom tooth out (except you don\u8217\'20\'19t need two doctors to sign off on that).\par \par \uc2 On our way to west London for the termination last Monday, I read the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2024/nov/18/jacob-rees-mogg-on-abortion-religion-and-reality-tv-ive-been-called-worse-than-a-nazi"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 interview with Jacob Rees-Mogg }}}\uc2 , who believes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is \u8220\'20\'1Cmorally indefensible\u8221\'20\'1D even in cases of rape or incest. I vibrated with rage while we navigated a delayed Northern line in a panic. What is morally indefensible is the notion that any woman or person who can bear children should have to live with the unwitting products of such violence. If doctors must be satisfied that continued pregnancy would cause \u8220\'20\'1Cgrave permanent injury to the physical or mental health of the pregnant woman\u8221\'20\'1D, I defy any situation to more accurately meet those requirements.\par \par \uc2 I had the sense, as a person going through it, that even in the UK the decision to terminate is perceived by some as frivolous, a luxury; not a frankly violating last resort that often leaves you struggling to advocate for yourself in the midst of appalling pain. The male doctor doing my procedure told me period cups were unhygienic and that it was better to use tissue. (The Lancet cites studies that say cups are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lanpub/article/PIIS2468-2667(19)30111-2/fulltext"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 less likely to cause infection }}}\uc2  than tampons or pads.) \u8220\'20\'1CSpoken like someone who has never had periods,\u8221\'20\'1D I said, exchanging a look with the female nurse. \u8220\'20\'1CI mean pads,\u8221\'20\'1D he stuttered from between my legs.\par \par \uc2 Not that I wasn\u8217\'20\'19t naive: I had no idea that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bills.parliament.uk/publications/53621/documents/4252#:~:text=Women%20accessing%20abortion%20in%20Great,legal%20in%20certain%2C%20fixed%20circumstances."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 technically illegal in Great Britain }}}\uc2  unless certain conditions are met. You might think: if \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains accessible, surely this is just a technicality. But there has been a sudden rise in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/nov/10/the-women-being-prosecuted-in-great-britain-for-abortions-her-confidentiality-was-completely-destroyed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 women being prosecuted }}}\uc2  for having so-called illegal abortions, with reports of healthcare providers breaching patient confidentiality. This continuing criminalisation enables the erosion of rights. The new Conservative leader, \uc2 Kemi Badenoch\uc2 , voted in 2022 against legislation to introduce buffer zones around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and hospitals {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/oct/31/anti-abortion-activists-set-up-outside-clinic-safe-zones-on-first-day-of-new-law-in-england"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to limit harassment }}}\uc2  by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protesters, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://members.parliament.uk/member/4597/voting?page=19"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 against }}}\uc2  the government\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/mar/30/mps-vote-to-continue-abortion-pills-by-post-scheme-in-england"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pills by post }}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D scheme to facilitate home \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without in-person consultation (both laws passed). Another {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/article/2024/may/17/uk-decriminalise-abortion-politicians-england-and-wales"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Conservative amendment }}}\uc2  earlier this year aimed to reduce the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  limit to 22 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Labour must move forward with decriminalising \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  (a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/apr/08/mps-propose-decriminalising-abortion-up-to-24-weeks-england-wales-stella-creasy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cross-party proposal }}}\uc2  to decriminalise it up to 24 weeks was shelved after this year\u8217\'20\'19s snap election) and enact rigorous protections for anyone seeking such healthcare as a matter of urgency, particularly as it faces the threat of a Tory party that is an existential concession to Reform UK \u8211\'20\'13 one that leans further right, socially, than many people of reproductive age will have experienced in our lifetimes. Any person seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is already going through enough stress without being subject to a system that legitimises a culture of shame and suspicion.\par \par \uc2 It must also make that fight intersectional. Two days before my \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I still ran a 10k that I had entered months prior, determined not to be stopped. Without really thinking about it, I started my playlist with Planningtorock\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=37afaihOBj4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Get Your Fkng Laws Off My Body }}}\uc2 . It\u8217\'20\'19s a techno anthem about transgender rights, but I hadn\u8217\'20\'19t anticipated how Jam Rostron\u8217\'20\'19s lyrics would gut me that morning. It viscerally underscored that denying bodily autonomy to any group legitimises all denial of bodily autonomy \u8211\'20\'13 a basic fact missed by anyone who sees the rights of one group as an attack on another. \u8220\'20\'1CMy body, my choice\u8221\'20\'1D is just another empty slogan if it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t apply to everyone.\par \par \uc2 Now on the other side in a state of stunned relief \u8211\'20\'13 and still furiously sore boobs \u8211\'20\'13 my only lasting shame is for my previous ignorance, at having learned the hard way that obtaining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the UK isn\u8217\'20\'19t that easy at all. I am bottomlessly grateful that I didn\u8217\'20\'19t have to cross state lines, but the process still felt, at points, like touching the hem of powerlessness. It felt awful.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The author lives in London\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Do you have an opinion on the issues raised in this article? If you would like to submit a response of up to 300 words by email to be considered for publication in our {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/letters"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 letters }}}\uc2  section, please {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:mailto:guardian.letters@theguardian.com?body=Please%20include%20your%20name,%20full%20postal%20address%20and%20phone%20number%20underneath%20your%20letter.%20Letters%20are%20usually%20published%20with%20the%20author’s%20name%20and%20city/town/village.%20The%20rest%20of%20the%20information%20is%20for%20verification%20only%20and%20to%20contact%20you%20if%20your%20letter%20is%20used."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 click here }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241128ekbs001gv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
