\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 We must defend elective abortions, not just the most politically palatable cases\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 1,123 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 November 2024\par 18:42 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 After Dobbs, pro-choice advocates have emphasized women in medical crises. But those tragic cases are a limited picture\par \par \uc2 A Kentucky woman known by the pseudonym Mary Poe recently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aclu.org/documents/complaint-mary-poe-v-russell-coleman-et-al"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed a lawsuit }}}\uc2  against her state, seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for what was once a banal reason: because she wanted one.\par \par \uc2 Poe, who was about seven weeks pregnant at the time of the lawsuit\u8217\'20\'19s filing, has since had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://kentuckylantern.com/briefs/woman-challenging-kentucky-abortion-ban-has-ended-her-pregnancy-but-not-the-legal-challenge/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 out of state }}}\uc2 . But her attorneys argue that she still has standing to sue to overturn {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2024/11/13/us/kentucky-abortion-lawsuit/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kentucky\u8217\'20\'19s two \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  bans }}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 a six-week ban and a separate total ban \u8211\'20\'13 arguing that the laws violate the state constitution. This much, at least, is typical: lawsuits challenging \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans have sprung up across the country since Dobbs, with women and their families seeking to overturn bans, expand exceptions, or get some compensation from the state for the graphic, distressing, disabling or deadly outcomes that the bans have made them suffer.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  justices who voted to overturn Roe in Dobbs {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ballsandstrikes.org/law-politics/brett-kavanaugh-dobbs-conurrence-lie-unraveling/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cited }}}\uc2  the surge in activist litigation around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 a product of conservative investment in anti-choice legal shops \u8211\'20\'13 as part of their reason for doing so. Surely this must be a contentious, controversial issue that the federal bench is ill equipped to resolve, the judges from the conservative legal movement reasoned \u8211\'20\'13 because look how many complaints the conservative legal movement has filed against it!\par \par \uc2 This rationale was always disingenuous, but it has also been proved flatly wrong: Dobbs has not got the courts out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  business. Instead, lawsuits over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have exploded. The anti-choice camp has pounced, seeking to further restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by banning pills; targeting \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds and sexual health educators; claiming rights for fetuses or embryos; or by asserting that men who father pregnancies have a right to keep women from terminating them.\par \par \uc2 But the pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights side has been busy with litigation, too. Women who have been put at great health risk or made to suffer terrible, painful complications as a result of bans brought a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2024/05/31/texas-supreme-court-zurawski-abortion/#:~:text=The%20case%2C%20Zurawski%20v.,20%20women%20and%20two%20doctors."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 class-action lawsuit }}}\uc2  in Texas. Bans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/womens-health-policy/issue-brief/legal-challenges-to-state-abortion-bans-since-the-dobbs-decision/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have been challenged }}}\uc2  over and over again \u8211\'20\'13 on religious liberty grounds, on the grounds of state constitutional provisions securing the right to make individual healthcare decisions, under a federal law that guarantees emergency room treatment for patients needing stabilizing care, and under state constitutional clauses guaranteeing liberty, due process and privacy.\par \par \uc2 The Kentucky lawsuit is part of this latter camp. Mary Poe has cited Kentucky\u8217\'20\'19s constitutional guarantees of individual rights to both privacy and self-determination, which she says have been violated by the bans. \u8220\'20\'1CI feel overwhelmed and frustrated that I cannot access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care here in my own state,\u8221\'20\'1D she said in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aclu.org/press-releases/kentucky-woman-files-class-action-lawsuit-against-two-abortion-bans-2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a statement }}}\uc2  delivered via her lawyers at \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CI am bringing this case to ensure that other Kentuckians will not have to go through what I am going through, and instead will be able to get the healthcare they need in our community.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This kind of desire for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 the dignified simplicity of it \u8211\'20\'13 has been missing from much of the post-Dobbs \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights discourse. After the ruling, as trigger bans shot into effect across the country, clinics shuttered their doors, and scared women tried to discern their options, there was no shortage of tragic stories highlighting the brutality, indignity and gendered bigotry of the laws. But as the dust settled and members of the Democratic party, the major \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocacy groups and the liberal legal movement surveyed the national scene, a consensus emerged that the face of the mainstream pro-choice movement would be the patient who experienced a medical emergency.\par \par \uc2 Women who had suffered horrific medical complications became lucid, moving and highly sought after tellers of their own stories, explaining how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans has risked their health: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2024/08/27/amanda-zurawksi-politics-democratic-national-convention-texas-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Amanda Zurawski }}}\uc2 , for example, was denied an emergency \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 18 weeks, subsequently went into septic shock twice, and one of her fallopian tubes was so scarred that it is now permanently closed, inhibiting her future fertility. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/kate-cox-on-her-legal-fight-for-abortion-trisomy-18/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kate Cox }}}\uc2  was denied an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after discovering that her fetus has trisomy 18, a rare genetic condition which is incompatible with life, and which, because of Cox\u8217\'20\'19s own medical history, also endangered her fertility and life.\par \par \uc2 During her presidential run, Kamala Harris ran an ad featuring a woman identified {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4zNmmiYjDgQ&ab_channel=KamalaHarris"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 only as Ondrea }}}\uc2 , who suffered a miscarriage at 16 weeks and was denied the standard care due to her state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. She developed sepsis and almost died. The ad features a shot of Ondrea in a bathroom, staring at her body in a mirror wearing only a sports bra. Her belly bears the scars of the emergency surgery that eventually saved her life \u8211\'20\'13 the surgery that she never would have had to have if it weren\u8217\'20\'19t for the ban.\par \par \uc2 It does not diminish these women\u8217\'20\'19s bravery, their suffering, or the wrongness of what was done to them to say that they are only one small fraction of those who need abortions in America. These are married, middle-class women with wanted pregnancies; Zurawski and Cox are both white. Cox has spoken movingly about her hopes to meet her future child, a girl; in the ad that features Ondrea, she and her husband hold a baby blanket. These are women whose suffering at the hands of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans has nothing to do with a refusal or distaste for heterosexual, married, middle-class life. Their suffering can be made visible precisely because they are so acceptable.\par \par \uc2 Not so with Mary Poe. Poe may well be married, middle-class and white; from her statement, in which she talks about the difficulty of finding childcare, we can infer that she, like most \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients, is already a mother. But Poe is not suffering a physical emergency; she is not enduring any pain or medical misfortune that she can use to purchase social license. She is not, in other words, a woman whose claim to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is based on a plea for mercy. She is merely a woman who seeks to be in control of her own life, one who believes that things like privacy and self-determination apply to her, too.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI have decided that ending my pregnancy is the best decision for me and my family,\u8221\'20\'1D Poe writes in her public statement. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a personal decision, a decision I believe should be mine alone, not made by anyone else.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This was not always a radical proposition. But in the post-Dobbs world, it has sadly become one.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241125ekbp001be\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
