\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Texas woman dies after receiving inadequate treatment for a miscarriage\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 549 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 November 2024\par 18:25 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Porsha Ngumezi, 35, is the fifth pregnant woman \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  reports to have died in connection to the fall of Roe v Wade\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/texas"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas }}}\uc2  woman has died after receiving inadequate medical treatment for a miscarriage, according to a new report from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/porsha-ngumezi-miscarriage-death-texas-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ProPublica }}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 the fifth pregnant woman the publication has found to have died since the fall of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v Wade }}}\uc2  after receiving inadequate care or being denied a legal {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Porsha Ngumezi, a 35-year-old mother of two, died in June 2023 after experiencing a miscarriage in Texas, where nearly all abortions are banned, \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  reported on Monday. Ten weeks into her pregnancy, Ngumezi started to bleed and went to Houston Methodist Sugar Land, which is part of the \uc2 Houston Methodist\uc2  hospital chain and located in the Houston metropolitan area. While at the hospital, Ngumezi continued to bleed for several hours. She underwent multiple blood transfusions.\par \par \uc2 Doctors who reviewed Ngumezi\u8217\'20\'19s case told \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  that she should have been offered a dilation and curettage, or D&C, a common procedure that can be used for miscarriages and abortions to clear tissue from the uterus. However, some doctors in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/jul/29/abortion-laws-bans-by-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 states with \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  bans }}}\uc2  have become hesitant to offer D&Cs, doctors said, because they are afraid of being punished for violating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans \u8211\'20\'13 even in situations where women\u8217\'20\'19s pregnancies have ended, as in Ngumezi\u8217\'20\'19s case.\par \par \uc2 Rather than being offered a D&C, a doctor gave Ngumezi misoprostol, \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  reported. Although misoprostol is frequently used in miscarriages and abortions, it can be dangerous to give to women who are \u8211\'20\'13 like Ngumezi \u8211\'20\'13 bleeding heavily.\par \par \uc2 However, in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, doctors may feel more comfortable giving patients misoprostol than giving them D&Cs, because D&Cs can attract too much attention.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou have to convince everyone that it is legal and won\u8217\'20\'19t put them at risk,\u8221\'20\'1D Dr Alison Goulding, a Houston OB-GYN, told \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  of D&Cs. \u8220\'20\'1CMany people may be afraid and misinformed and refuse to participate \u8211\'20\'13 even if it\u8217\'20\'19s for a miscarriage.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ngumezi started complaining of chest pain, but the doctor treating Ngumezi did not order any additional tests, according to \uc2 ProPublica\uc2 . Several hours after her arrival at the hospital, Ngumezi started fighting to breathe.\par \par \uc2 Her husband was at the hospital when Ngumezi died.\par \par \uc2 Doctors and nurses involved in Ngumezi\u8217\'20\'19s care did not respond to \uc2 ProPublica\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s requests for comment. \uc2 Houston Methodist\uc2  also did not answer questions from the outlet.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAll \uc2 Houston Methodist\uc2  hospitals follow all state laws, including the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law in place in Texas,\u8221\'20\'1D a hospital spokesperson told \uc2 ProPublica\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  has previously reported on four other deaths of women who had their medical care delayed after miscarriages or who were unable to undergo legal abortions. Two of the women, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/oct/30/texas-woman-death-abortion-ban-miscarriage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Josseli Barnica }}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/nov/01/teen-dies-abortion-ban-texas-neveah-crain"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nevaeh Crain }}}\uc2 , also lived in Texas. Two others, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/georgia-abortion-ban-amber-thurman-death"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Amber Nicole Thurman }}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/candi-miller-abortion-ban-death-georgia"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Candi Miller }}}\uc2 , lived in Georgia, which bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 In reporting on Thurman and Miller\u8217\'20\'19s deaths, \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  obtained reports from a state committee in Georgia that reviews the deaths of pregnant women and issues recommendations about how to improve the state\u8217\'20\'19s maternal mortality rates. Georgia subsequently dismissed all 32 members of that committee, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/georgia-dismisses-maternal-mortality-committee-amber-thurman-candi-miller"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ProPublica reported }}}\uc2  last week.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241125ekbp002bf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
